	Right now,  $\frac bp$  and  $\frac cp$  	are just to the right of 	 $\frac ur$  and  $\frac vr$  for some  $u$  and  $v$  with  $u+v=r$ . 	The issue is that the there is some fraction just to the right of 	 $\frac bp$  and  $\frac cp$  from an earlier value of  $r$ , 	and by hypothesis its denominator is going to be strictly greater than  $1$ .
It is at this point we are going to use the properties of Farey sequences. 	When we consider the fractions with denominator  $r+1$ , 	they are going to lie outside of the interval 	they we have constrained  $\frac bp$  and  $\frac cp$  to lie in. 	Indeed, if right now  $\frac ur < \frac bp < \frac st$ , 	then Farey theory says the next fraction 	inside the interval  $[\frac uv, \frac st]$  	is  $\frac{u+s}{v+t}$ , and since  $t > 1$ , we have  $v+t > r+1$ . 	In other words, we get an inequality of the form 	\[ \frac ur < \frac bp < \text{something} \le \frac{u+1}{r+1}. \] 	The same holds for  $\frac cp$  as 	\[ \frac vr < \frac cp < \text{something} \le \frac{v+1}{r+1}. \] 	Finally, 	\[ \frac dp < \frac{r-1}{r} < \frac{r}{r+1}. \] 	So now we have that 	\[ \left\lfloor \frac{(r+1)b}{p} \right\rfloor 		+ \left\lfloor \frac{(r+1)c}{p} \right\rfloor 		+ \left\lfloor \frac{(r+1)d}{p} \right\rfloor 		\le u + v + (r-1) = 2r-1 \] 	which is a contradiction.  $\blacksquare$ Now, since \[ \frac{p-3}{p-2} < \frac dp 	\implies d > \frac{p(p-3)}{p-2} = p - 1 - \frac{2}{p-2} \] which for  $p > 2$  gives  $d = p-1$ .

