To show this is the optimal set, let  $S=\{x_0<x_1<\dots <x_{2k}\}$  which, by the problem condition gives 
\begin{align*}
N+1& \le x_0+x_1+\dots +x_{2k}
\frac{N}{2}& \ge x_{k+1}+\dots +x_{2k}.
\end{align*}
Now, subtracting twice the  $2$ nd inequality from the first gives 
\begin{align*}
x_0& \ge 1+(x_{k+1}-x_1)+(x_{k+2}-x_2)+\dots +(x_{2k}-x_k)
& \ge 1+\underbrace{k+k+\dots +k}_{k\  \text{times}}
&=1+k^2.
\end{align*}
Now, \begin{align*}
\frac{N}{2}& \ge \sum_{j=k+1}^{2k} x_j
& \ge (x_0+(k+1))+(x_0+(k+2))+\dots +(x_0+2k)
&=kx_0+\frac{k(3k+1)}{2}
& \ge k(k^2+1)+\frac{k(3k+1)}{2} \implies N \ge k(2k^2+3k+3).\  \blacksquare
\end{align*}</details>

