This was straightforward, yet took me considerable amount of time to finish. :oops:**Solution.**Define \(\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i=S_n\) and  $\tfrac{S_n}{n}=\ell_{n}$ . We claim that  $\ell_i$  is decreasing at first and will eventually become constant. Notice that  $S_n \to S_{n+1}$  increases by atmost  $n$ . Hence 
\begin{align*}
n &\ge (n+1)[\ell_{n+1}]-n[\ell_n] 
&=n\ell_{n+1}-n(\ell-\ell_{n+1}) 
\end{align*}

which rearranges to give   $$ n(\ell_n-\ell_{n+1})\ge n(\ell_{n+1}-1)\ge 0 $$  Therefore, we get  $\ell_n\ge\ell_{n+1}$ . It's easy to see that  $S_{n+1}=n\ell_n+\{\ell_n \pmod{n+1}\}$ . Now if  $\ell_i=\ell_{i+1}$  for some  $i$ . Then   $$ S_{i+1}=(i+1)\ell_{i+1}=(i+1)\ell_i=i\ell_i+\ell_i $$   and  $S_i=i\ell_i$ . Consequently, we get  $a_{m+1}=\ell_i$  for all  $m\ge i$  which finishes. Otherwise if  $\ell_n > \ell_{n+1}$  and decreases, tilll we get some  $j$  such that  $\ell_j<j+1$ . Then notice that   $$ S_{j+1}=j\ell_j+\{\ell_j \pmod{j+1}\}=j\ell_j+\ell_j=(j+1)\ell_j $$   Consequently, we get  $a_{m+1}=\ell_j$  for all  $m \ge j$  finishing the problem and claim.  $\blacksquare$

Here's my solution:

We can write that   $a_1 +  a_2 + \cdots + a_k = Nk$  for some positive integer  $N$ . Observe that \begin{align*}a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_k \leq & n + 1 + 2 + \cdots + k  = & n + \frac{k(k-1)}{2}. \end{align*}

<span style="color:#00f">Claim</span>. There exists a  $k \in \mathbb{Z}^+$  such that  $N \leq k - 1$ .

*Proof.* Assume otherwise, i.e. $k \leq N$  for all  $N, k$  . Then \begin{align*} k^2 \leq Nk \leq & n + \frac{k(k-1)}{2}  \implies 2k^2 \leq & n + k^2 - k  \implies k^2 + k \leq & n \end{align*}
for all  $k \in \mathbb{Z}^+$ , which is absurd.  $\blacksquare$ Now chose a  $k$  such that  $a_1 +  a_2 + \cdots + a_k = Nk$  and  $N \leq k - 1$ . Then we can write  $a_i = N$  for all  $i > k$ , and we are done.

For each  $k\ge1$  let  $b_k=\dfrac{a_1+\ldots+a_k}k$ . By the problem condition, we know  $b_k\in\mathbb Z$  for all  $k$ . Now, suppose for the sake of contradiction that there is some  $k$  for which  $b_{k+1}>b_k$ . Then,  $\dfrac{a_1+\ldots+a_{k+1}}{k+1}>\dfrac{a_1+\ldots+a_k}k$ , so
\[ ka_{k+1}>a_1+\ldots+a_k. \]
Now, note that the RHS is a multiple of  $k$ . Thus, since it is less than  $ka_{k+1}$ , we know there is some positive integer  $r$  for which  $a_1+\ldots+a_k=k(a_{k+1}-r)$ . Then, taking this equation $\mod{k+1}$  yields
\[ -a_{k+1}\equiv -a_{k+1}+r\pmod{k+1}, \]
so  $k+1\mid r$ . But since  $r>0$ , this means  $r\ge k+1$ . But then,
\[ k(a_{k+1}-r)\le k\cdot k-k\cdot(k+1)<0, \]
but  $a_1+\ldots+a_k$  is positive, so we have reached the desired contradiction. Thus,  $b_{k+1}\le b_k$  for all  $k$ . Since the  $b_k$  are positive integers for all  $k$ , eventually the sequence must become constant. Finally, note that if  $b_m=b_{m+1}=c$ , then 
\begin{align*}
	\frac{a_1+\ldots+a_m}m&=\frac{a_1+\ldots+a_{m+1}}{m+1} 
	a_1+\ldots+a_m&=m\cdot a_{m+1} 
	c=\frac{a_1+\ldots+a_m}m&=a_{m+1}.
\end{align*}
Thus, since there exists positive integers  $M, c$  such that  $c=b_{m}$  for all  $m\ge M$ , we also have  $c=a_{m+1}$  for all  $m\ge M$ , so the sequence eventually becomes contant as desired.  $\blacksquare$

