<details><summary>Different Inversion Solution</summary>In this solution, let  $AB=c$ ,  $h_a$  be the altitude from  $A$ , and cyclic variations. Furthermore,  $s=\frac{a+b+c}{2}$ ,  $K$  is the area of  $\triangle ABC$ ,  $r$  is the inradius and  $R$  is the circumradius.

Invert about  $A$  with radius  $s-a$  composed with a reflection about the  $A$  angle bisector, and denote inverses with a  $'$ . Since the incircle is orthogonal to our inversive circle, it remains fixed. Note that the image of the circumcircle is a line parallel to  $BC$  tangent to the incircle on the opposite side of the incircle as  $A$ , hence it is line  $BC$ . Accordingly we deduce that  $P_A'$  is the reflection of  $A$  over  $BC$ , so that  $AP_A=\frac{(s-a)^2}{2h_a}$ . Similarly,  $Q_A'$  is the reflection of  $A$  over the other tangent to the incircle parallel to  $BC$ . Note that the ratio of the homothety sending  $Q_A'$  to  $P_A'$  is the same as the ratio sending the incircle to the  $A$ -excircle, so that   $$ AQ_A'=2h_a\cdot \frac{s-a}{s}\Longrightarrow AQ_A=\frac{s(s-a)}{2h_a} $$   Thus,   $$ P_AQ_A=AQ_A-AP_A=\frac{s(s-a)}{2h_a}-\frac{(s-a)^2}{2h_a}=\frac{a(s-a)}{2h_a} $$  Now we have   $$ 8\prod_{\text{cyc}}\frac{a(s-a)}{2h_a}=\frac{abc(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}{h_ah_bh_c}=\frac{4RK\cdot \frac{K^2}{s}}{\frac{2K^2}{R}}=\frac{2R^2K}{s} $$  The problem statement is equivalent to   $$ \frac{2R^2K}{s}\leq R^3 \Longrightarrow 2K\leq Rs \Longrightarrow 2r\leq R  $$  Good night!</details>

