Let  $P_A'$  be the image of  $P_A$  under a homothety of ratio  $2$  at  $A$  (which lies on  $\omega_A$ ), and let  $P_A''$  be the image of  $P_A'$  under an inversion at  $A$  with radius  $s-a$  (which fixes the incircle). Let  $Q_A''$  be defined similarly. 

The image of  $\omega_A$  under this inversion is the further line tangent to the incircle perpendicular to  $AO$ , the image of  $S_A$  is the closer line (to  $A$ ). Then  $P_A'',Q_A''$  are the intersections of these lines with  $AO$ . Let  $K$  be the foot of  $I$  onto  $AO$ . Then  $AQ_A''=AK-r,AP_A''=AK+r$ . Then  $2P_AQ_A=P_A'Q_A'=AQ_A'-AP_A'=(s-a)^2(\frac{1}{AQ_A''}-\frac{1}{AP_A''})=\frac{2r(s-a)^2}{AK^2-r^2}$ .

Extend  $AI$  to meet  $S$  again at  $M$ . Let  $A'$  be the antipode of  $A$  on  $S$ . We have  $IKA'M$  is cyclic, so  $AK = \frac{AI\cdot AM}{AA'}$ . By Ptolemy's theorem we have that  $AM\cdot a = BM\cdot b+CM\cdot c = (b+c)\cdot MI$ , so  $AK=\frac{AI\cdot MI}{2R}\cdot\frac{b+c}{a}=\frac{b+c}{a}\cdot\frac{R^2-OI^2}{2R}=\frac{b+c}{a}\cdot r$ . So  $AK^2-r^2=r^2\Bigg(\bigg(\frac{b+c}{a}\bigg)^2-1\Bigg)=r^2\Bigg(\frac{(b+c-a)(a+b+c)}{a^2}\Bigg)=r^2\frac{2(s-a)\cdot 2s}{a^2}$ . 

Thus  $2P_AQ_A=\frac{2r(s-a)^2}{r^2\frac{2(s-a)\cdot 2s}{a^2}}=\frac{a^2(s-a)}{2rs}$ . So 
\[8P_AQ_A\cdot P_BQ_B\cdot P_CQ_C=\frac{(abc)^2(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}{8K^3}=\frac{(abc)^2s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}{8K^3s}=\frac{(abc)^2}{8Ks}=\frac{abcR}{2s}=\frac{2abcrR}{4K}\]
\[=2rR^2=(R^2-OI^2)R=R^3-R\cdot OI^2\le R^3.\] $\square$

