The answer is all  $n \ge 3$  expressible in the form  $n = \boxed{2^a+2^b}$  for  $a$ ,  $b$  nonnegative integers.

Call  $n$  *triangular* if it satisfies the conditions of the problem. We start with this claim:**Claim.** If  $n$  is *triangular* then  $2n$  is also.
*Proof.* Start with a  $2n$ -gon. Assume  $n$  is *triangular*. Consider the isosceles triangles formed with two adjacent sides of the polygon. Remove a set of  $n$  of these disjoint triangles - then we will be left with a regular  $n$ -gon, which we know is *triangular*. This means  $2n$  is as well.  $\square$ In light of this, it suffices to show that odd  $n$  are *triangular* if and only if they are  $1$  more than a power of  $2$ .

