This can be extended for higher  $n$  - the pattern should be evident.

Now we show that these are the only ones that work. Choose two vertices  $A$  and  $B$ , draw diagonal  $AB$ , and consider the polygon of the resulting two that doesn't contain the center. Define the *boundary* length as the number of sides between  $A$  and  $B$ .**Claim.** This polygon can only be triangulated into isosceles triangles if the *boundary* length is a power of  $2$ .
*Proof.* Let the *boundary* length be  $k$ . Since the polygon does not contain the center, it follows that the only isosceles triangles that can be constructed with vertices of the polygon are ones whose legs are sides of the polygon. Since every vertex must be included in a triangle, we must be able to draw all these border isosceles triangles. This implies that  $k$  should be even. Now removing these triangles leaves us with another polygon, whose *boundary* length is  $\frac{k}{2}$ . Clearly its circumcenter is not in the polygon, so we repeat the process. Iterating, we find that  $k$  must be a power of  $2$ , as needed.  $\square$    
Now go back to the original  $n$ -gon. The very first isosceles triangle divides the  $n$ -gon into four polygons - the triangle, and three polygons whose *boundary* lengths are  $a_1$ ,  $a_2$ ,  $a_3$  such that  $a_1+a_2+a_3 = n$ . Clearly two of these should be equal - WLOG  $a_2 = a_3$ , which means that  $a_1$  must be the *boundary* length of the polygon touching the base of the isosceles triangle. By our second claim, in order for these to be triangulated into isosceles triangles, all of  $a_1$ ,  $a_2$ ,  $a_3$  must be powers of  $2$ . But since  $n$  is odd, by definition  $a_1$  should also be odd. This means  $a_1 = 1$ . Since  $a_2+a_3 = 2 \cdot 2^m$  for some  $m$ , it follows that  $n = 2^{m+1}+1$ , completing the direction.

We conclude that all  $n = 2^x+1$  are *triangular*, so by the first claim, all  $n \ge 3$  in the form  $n = \boxed{2^a+2^b}$  are *triangular*.  $\blacksquare$

candidates:
The answer is  $n$  of the form  $2^a(2^b+1)$  where  $a$  and  $b$  are nonnegative integers not both zero.
Call the polygon  $A_1 \dots A_n$  with indices taken modulo  $n$ . We refer to segments  $A_1 A_2$ ,  $A_2 A_3$ , \dots,  $A_n A_1$  as *short sides*. Each of these must be in the triangulation. Note that  
- when  $n$  is even, the isosceles triangles triangle using a short side  $A_1 A_2$  are  $\triangle A_n A_1 A_2$  and  $\triangle A_1 A_2 A_3$  only, which we call *small*.
- when  $n$  is odd, in addition to the small triangles, we have  $\triangle A_{\frac{1}{2}(n+3)} A_1 A_2$ , which we call *big*.

This leads to the following two claims.**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** If  $n > 4$  is even, then  $n$  works iff  $n/2$  does.
*Proof.* All short sides must be part of a small triangle; after drawing these in, we obtain an  $n/2$ -gon.
[asy]
    size(7cm);
    draw(unitcircle, dotted);
    picture p;
    dot(p, dir(0));
    dot(p, dir(30));
    dot(p, dir(60));
    fill(p, dir(0)--dir(30)--dir(60)--cycle, opacity(0.1)+lightred);
    draw(p, dir(0)--dir(30)--dir(60), blue);
    draw(p, dir(0)--dir(60), red);
    for (int i=0; i<6; ++i) {
      add(rotate(60*i)*p);
    }
[/asy]
Thus the sides of  $\mathcal P$  must pair off, and when we finish drawing we have an  $n/2$ -gon.  $\blacksquare$ Since  $n = 4$  works, this implies all powers of  $2$  work and it remains to study the case when  $n$  is odd.**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** If  $n > 1$  is odd, then  $n$  works if and only if  $n = 2^b+1$  for some positive integer  $b$ .
*Proof.* We cannot have all short sides part of small triangles for parity reasons, so some side, must be part of a big triangle. Since big triangles contain the center  $O$ , there can be at most one big triangle too.
Then we get  $\frac{1}{2}(n-1)$  small triangles, pairing up the remaining sides. Now repeating the argument with the triangles on each half shows that the number  $n-1$  must be a power of  $2$ , as needed.  $\blacksquare$

\begin{proof}
We claim that the solution set is   $$ S = \{a\mid a = 2^p(2^q+1),\;  \text{ $p$ ,  $q$  nonnegative integers not both  $0$ }\} $$   Then let  $f(n)$  denote the existence of a triangulation of a regular  $n$ -gon into isosceles triangles, where  $f(n) = 1$  if there exist one, and  $f(n) = 0$  otherwise.
Consider the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}
By writing an even positive integer  $n$  as  $2^k\cdot m$  where  $m$  is odd, we have   $$ f(n) =1 \iff f(m) = 1 $$  \end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Notice that for each side of this polygon, it must be a part of an isosceles triangle. However since  $n$  is even, each side can only form an isosceles triangle with the its two adjacent sides. Then we connect the vertices such that we have  $n$  isosceles triangles on the side of the polygon and another regular  $\frac{n}{2}$ -gon inside it. For example, consider the regular  $8$ -gon. For each side of it, the only isosceles triangle it can be a part of are the four small ones up to rotation. Hence after connecting the segments, we are left to deal with the square inside.
Then we can continue this process on the regular  $\frac{n}{2}$ -gon, where if  $\frac{n}{2}$  is even, we draw in the  $\frac{n}{4}$  isosceles triangles and continue with the  $\frac{n}{4}$ -gon inside. Therefore this implies that the value of  $f(n)$  is equivalent to that of  $m$ , as desired.
\end{proof}
Now we can focus on the value of  $f(n)$  for odd  $n$ s. Since there are an odd number of sides, there has to be an odd number of sides forming a triangle that doesn't have two sides on the polygon. It is not hard to see that the only isosceles triangles with sides on the polygon for  $n$  odd is are either having one side on the polygon or having two sides on the polygon. For the case where it only has one side on the polygon, its vertices are two adjacent ones and another that is directly opposite the side connecting the two adjacent vertices, where for brevity we shall name this type of triangles  $\Delta Z$ . In particular, for a triangulation, there can be at most one isosceles triangle like such. Thus we can first draw in this triangle, and deal with the rest  $n-1$  sides. For convenience, all the reasoning that follows are WLOG referring to the right longer side of  $\Delta Z$ . 
For the two longer sides of  $\Delta Z$ , it is evident that there can be no other isosceles triangle in the polygon with this side as one of its sides of equal length. Thus we may draw in the perpendicular-bisector of this side of  $\Delta Z$  in hope to hit a vertex. Since the diagonals of the polygon must not intersect in the interior, this bisector must hit an vertex on the right side of the polygon. However this bisector hits an vertex if and only if  $\frac{n-1}{2}$  is even, where if it is, we may draw in the diagonals connecting the two right vertices of  $\Delta Z$  with this 'midpoint' of the right  $n-1$  sides of the polygon to create an isosceles triangle. This connection forms another two smaller section on the right side, each with  $\frac{n-1}{4}$  sides. By continuing this process, we notice that if at some point  $\frac{n-1}{2^k}$  is  $2$ , we have found a valid triangulation. However if  $\frac{n-1}{2^k}$  is odd, by previous reasoning it is not hard to see that there cannot exist a valid triangulation. In particular, we also know that  $n=3$  can obvious work. Therefore   $$ f(n) = 1 \iff \frac{n-1}{2^k}=2\Rightarrow n = 2^{k+1}+1 $$   for odd  $n$  and nonneg integer  $k$ . 
Lastly, we have shown that the solution set for odd numbers are  $2^a+1$  for positive integer  $a$ . Moreover, by doing the previous process, it is not hard to see that a such triangulation exists for all numbers of the form  $2^b(2^a+1)$  for nonnegative integer  $b$ , and we are done.

Outline:

We claim the answer is all numbers of the form  $\boxed{2^i+2^j}$  for non-negative integers  $i$  and  $j$ .

We notice that for all evens,  $n$  works if and only if  $\tfrac{n}{2}$  works (pair adjacent sides together). Hence, we just have to show that all odds of the form  $2^i + 1$  work, and all others don't.

We start by providing a proof of construction for  $n = 2^i + 1$ . Consider the following constructions for  $i = 2, 3, 4$ :
[asy]size(15cm);

pair[] drawRegular(int n, pair C, real R, real rotDeg = -90, pen border=black+1) {
    pair[] P = new pair[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        real angle = rotDeg + 360.0 * i / n;
        P*= C + R * dir(angle);
    }

    path g = P[0];
    for(int i=1; i<n; ++i)
        g = g -- P[i];
    g = g -- cycle;

    filldraw(g, opacity(0.08)+lightgray, border);

    for(int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        dot(P[i], blue+2);
        pair offset = 0.14 * dir(rotDeg + 360.0 * i / n);
        label(format("%d", i+1), P[i] + offset, fontsize(10));
    }
    return P;
}

pair C1 = (-4, 0);
pair C2 = (0, 0);
pair C3 = (4, 0);

real R5  = 1.6;
real R9  = 1.45;
real R17 = 1.4;

pair[] pent = drawRegular(5,  C1, R5,  -90, black+1.2);
pair[] nona = drawRegular(9,  C2, R9,  -90, black+1);
pair[] hept = drawRegular(17, C3, R17, -90, black+0.9);

draw(pent[0]--pent[2]--pent[4], dashed+red);
draw(nona[0]--nona[2]--nona[4]--cycle, dashed+red);
draw(nona[4]--nona[6]--nona[8]--cycle, dashed+red);
