It is clear how this generalizes.

Now, we will show that if  $n$  cannot be expressed as  $2^i+1$ , then it cannot be triangulated as required. Call a triangle [i]major* if has vertices on two adjacent vertices of the regular polygon, and one on the opposite vertex. Clearly, we must have exactly one major triangle, since if there were zero, then a parity argument contradicts this, while if there were more than two, then they all contain the center of the regular polygon, which is a contradiction. Repeating this argument for both of the new polygons created, we find that they both have to have a power of two number of vertices, which contradicts the assumption.  $\square$

