<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>Lemma:
Consider a triangle  $ \triangle ABC$  with  $ M$  the midpoint of  $ \overline{BC}$ .:
[asy]size(200);
pair a = dir(73), b=(0,0), c=(2,0), m=(b+c)/2;
dot(a); dot(b); dot(c); dot(m);
draw(a--b--c--a--m);
label("A",a,NW);
label("B",b,SW);
label("C",c,SE);
label("M",m,S);[/asy]

By the law of sines,  $ \frac{BM}{\sin BAM} \equal{} \frac{AM}{\sin B}$  and  $ \frac{MC}{\sin CAM} \equal{} \frac{AM}{\sin C}$ .
Thus, $ \frac{\sin B}{\sin BAM} \equal{} \frac{AM}{BM} \equal{} \frac{AM}{MC} \equal{} \frac{\sin C}{\sin CAM}$ , so $ \frac{\sin B}{\sin C} \equal{} \frac{\sin BAM}{\sin CAM}$ .



Let  $ A \equal{} \angle BAC, A_1 \equal{} \angle BAM, A_2 \equal{} \angle MAC, B \equal{} \angle ABC, C \equal{} \angle ACB$ .
From the lemma, we know that  $ \frac{\sin B}{\sin C} \equal{} \frac{\sin A_1}{\sin A_2}$ .

In addition, we know that since  $ PD$  and  $ EN$  are perpendicular bisectors,  $ \triangle ABD$  and  $ \triangle ACE$  are isosceles. Thus,  $ A_1 \equal{} \angle MAB \equal{} \angle DAB \equal{} \angle DBA \equal{} \angle FBA$  and  $ A_2 \equal{} \angle MAC \equal{} \angle EAC \equal{} \angle ECA \equal{} \angle FCA$ .

Now apply the trigonometric version of Ceva's theorem on point  $ F$  inside  $ \triangle ABC$ . That gives $ \frac{\sin \angle FBC \sin \angle FCA \sin \angle FAB}{\sin \angle FBA \sin \angle FCB \sin \angle FAC} \equal{} 1$ . $ \angle FBC \equal{} B \minus{} \angle FBA \equal{} B \minus{} A_1$ . $ \angle FCA \equal{} A_2$  $ \angle FBA \equal{} A_1$  $ \angle FCB \equal{} C \minus{} \angle FCA \equal{} C \minus{} A_2$ , so we have $ \frac{\sin \angle FAB}{\sin \angle FAC} \cdot \frac{\sin(B\minus{}A_1)\sin(A_2)}{\sin(C\minus{}A_2)\sin(A_1)} \equal{} 1$ , or $ \frac{\sin \angle FAB}{\sin \angle FAC} \equal{} \frac{\sin(C\minus{}A_2)\sin(A_1)}{\sin(B\minus{}A_1)\sin(A_2)}$ . Now, since  $ B \plus{} A_1 \plus{} C \plus{} A_2 \equal{} \pi$ ,  $ \frac{\sin(C\plus{}A_2)}{\sin(B\plus{}A_1)} \equal{} 1$ . Thus, the RHS equals $ \frac{\sin(C\minus{}A_2)\sin(C\plus{}A_2)\sin(A_1)}{\sin(B\minus{}A_1)\sin(B\plus{}A_1)\sin(A_2)}$  $ \equal{} \frac{(\sin(C)\cos(A_2)\minus{}\cos(C)\sin(A_2))(\sin(C)\cos(A_2)\plus{}\cos(C)\sin(A_2))\sin(A_1)}{(\sin(B)\cos(A_1)\minus{}\cos(B)\sin(A_1))(\sin(B)\cos(A_1)\plus{}\cos(B)\sin(A_1))\sin(A_2)}$  $ \equal{} \frac{(\sin^2C\cos^2A_2 \minus{} \cos^2C\sin^2A_2)\sin A_1}{(\sin^2 B \cos^2 A_1 \minus{} \cos^2 B \sin^2 A_1)\sin A_2}$  $ \equal{} \frac{(\sin^2 C (1\minus{}\sin^2 A_2) \minus{} (1\minus{}\sin^2 C)\sin^2 A_2)\sin A_1}{(\sin^2 B (1\minus{}\sin^2 A_1) \minus{} (1\minus{}\sin^2 B)\sin^2 A_1)\sin A_2}$  $ \equal{} \frac{(\sin^2C \minus{} \sin^2 A_2)\sin A_1}{(\sin^2 B \minus{} \sin^2 A_1)\sin A_2}$ .

Now, from the lemma,  $ \frac{\sin B}{\sin C} \equal{} \frac{\sin A_1}{\sin A_2}$ . It follows that  $ \frac{\sin^2 C \minus{} \sin^2 A_2}{\sin^2 B \minus{} \sin^2 A_1} \equal{} \frac{\sin^2 A_2}{\sin^2 A_1}$ , since  $ \frac ab \equal{} \frac cd \implies \frac ab \equal{} \frac cd \equal{} \frac{a\minus{}c}{b\minus{}d}$ .

Now, We put this back in and we get that  $ \frac{\sin\angle FAB}{\sin\angle FAC} \equal{} \frac{\sin A_2}{\sin A_1}$  and that  $ A_1\plus{}A_2 \equal{} \angle FAB \plus{} \angle FAC \equal{} A$ . It then follows that  $ \angle FAB \equal{} A_2$  and  $ \angle FAC \equal{} A_1$  (or rather, that they are equal mod  $ \pi$ , and they are all clearly less than  $ \pi$ ). This is why:

Suppose we have four angles  $ A, B, C, D$  with  $ A\plus{}B \equal{} C\plus{}D \equal{} \theta$  and  $ \frac{\sin A}{\sin B} \equal{} \frac{\sin C}{\sin D} \equal{} K$ . We will show that  $ A \equal{} C$  and  $ B \equal{} D$ .
Write it as follows: $ K \equal{} \frac{\sin(\theta \minus{} D)}{\sin D} \equal{} \frac{\sin\theta \cos D \minus{} \cos\theta \sin D}{\sin D} \equal{} \sin\theta\cot D \minus{} \cos\theta$ , so  $ \cot D \equal{} \frac{K\plus{}\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}$ , which means that  $ D$  is unique modulo  $ \pi$ .

Okay, so we've established that  $ \angle FAB \equal{} A_2$  and  $ \angle FAC \equal{} A_1$  (or equivalently,  $ F$  is on the symmedian from  $ A$  (there are nicer ways to do this, as shown in other solutions)). Now, we notice that  $ FP$  is a median of  $ \triangle AFB$ . As such, our lemma states that $ \frac{\sin \angle AFP}{\sin \angle BFP} \equal{} \frac{\sin\angle FAB}{\sin \angle FBA} \equal{} \frac{\sin A_2}{\sin A_1} \equal{} \frac{\sin C}{\sin B}$ . Also, notice that  $ \angle AFP \plus{} \angle BFP \equal{} B\plus{}C$ , since  $ \angle FAB \plus{} \angle FBA \equal{} A_2 \plus{} A_1 \equal{} A \equal{} \pi \minus{} B \minus{} C$ . Thus, by the thing shown above,  $ \angle AFP \equal{} C$ . By applying the same argument to  $ \triangle AFC$ , we obtain  $ \angle AFN \equal{} B$ . Thus,  $ \angle PAN \plus{} \angle PFN \equal{} A \plus{} \angle AFP \plus{} \angle AFN \equal{} A \plus{} B \plus{} C \equal{} \pi$ , so  $ APFN$  is cyclic.  $ \blacksquare$ <details><summary>comment</summary>The hardest part of the problem was proving that  $ AF$  is the symmedian. The multiplication by  $ \frac{\sin(C\plus{}A_2)}{\sin(B\plus{}A_1)}$  took me around an hour to find.</details></details>

