Without Loss of Generality, assume  $AC >AB$ . It is sufficient to prove that  $\angle OFA = 90^{\circ}$ , as this would immediately prove that  $A,P,O,F,N$  are concyclic.
By applying the Menelaus' Theorem in the Triangle  $\triangle BFC$  for the transversal  $E,M,D$ , we have (in magnitude)
\[ \frac{FE}{EC} \cdot \frac{CM}{MB} \cdot \frac{BD}{DF} = 1 \iff \frac{FE}{EC} = \frac{DF}{BD} 
\] 
Here, we used that  $BM=MC$ , as  $M$  is the midpoint of  $BC$ . Now, since  $EC =EA$  and  $BD=DA$ , we have
\[ \frac{FE}{EA} = \frac{DF}{DA} \iff \frac{DA}{AE} = \frac{DF}{FE} \iff AF \text{ bisects exterior } \angle EFD 
\]
Now, note that  $OE$  bisects the exterior  $\angle FED$  and  $OD$  bisects exterior  $\angle FDE$ , making  $O$  the  $F$ -excentre of  $\triangle FED$ . This implies that  $OF$  bisects interior  $\angle EFD$ , making  $OF \perp AF$ , as was required.

Let  $\alpha=\angle{MAB}$  and  $\beta=\angle{CAM}$  (all angles will be directed modulo  $180^\circ$ ), and note that  $O=PD\cap NE$ . Then simple angle chasing gives us  $\angle{CFB}=2\alpha+2\beta=\angle{COB}$ , whence  $OFBC$  is a cyclic quadrilateral. Note that  $\triangle{APN}$  and  $\triangle{ABC}$  share tangent  $AA$  (this can be seen by taking a homothety with center  $A$ ). Consider the radical center  $X$  of the circles  $(APON),(ABC),(OFBC)$ , so  $X=AA\cap BC$ . By a simple angle chase,
\[\angle{MOX}=\angle{MAX}=90^\circ-\angle{OAM}=90^\circ-\angle{OAB}+\angle{MAB}=\angle{BCA}+\alpha.\]But we also have
\begin{align*}
\angle{MOF}=\angle{MOP}-\angle{FOD}
&=-\angle{ABC}-(\angle{BOP}-\angle{BOF})
&=-\angle{ABC}-\angle{BCA}+\angle{BCF}
&=-\angle{ABC}-\angle{BCA}+\angle{BCA}-\angle{ECA}
&=-\angle{ABC}-\beta.
\end{align*}Thus
\[\angle{MOX}=\alpha+\angle{BCA}=\angle{CAB}-\beta+\angle{BCA}=-\beta-\angle{ABC}=\angle{MOF},\]so  $F$  lies on the radical axis  $XO$  of  $(APON)$  and  $(OFBC)$  and  $\{O,F\}=(APON)\cap(OFBC)$ , as desired.

