Without Loss of Generality, assume  $AC >AB$ .
Note that \[\begin{aligned} \angle BFC &= \angle CAF + \angle ACF + \angle BAF + \angle ABF &= (\angle FAC + \angle FAB) + (\angle DAB + \angle EAC) &= 2 \angle BAC  &= \angle BOC\end{aligned}\] implying  $B,F,O,C$  concyclic.
Let the second point of intersection of this circle with lines  $AB,AC$  be  $X,Y$  respectively.
Note that as  $O$  is the midpoint of arc  $BC$ , therefore,  $XO$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle BXC$ . Also,  $\angle BXO = \angle OXC = 90^{\circ} - \angle BAC$ , implying that the foot of perpendicular on  $AC$  of  $X$  is  $N$ , and thus,  $X,O,E,N$  are collinear. Similarly,  $Y,O,D,P$  are collinear.
Now, let  $Z$  be the second point of intersection of  $OM$  with the circle. We'll prove that  $A,F,Z$  are collinear, and then we're done as  $OZ$  is the diameter of the circle.
By Pascal's on the hexagon  $BFZOYC$ , we get that  $BF \cap OY = D, FZ \cap YC, OZ \cap BC = M$  are collinear. This implies that the lines,  $DM, CY, FZ$  are concurrent. But  $DM,CY$  intersect at  $A$ . $\square$

Here are some observations about the point  $F$  that I don't think have been made yet: The point  $F$  lies on the [Brocard circle](http://mathworld.wolfram.com/BrocardCircle.html) of triangle  $ABC$  (the circle with diameter  $OK$ , where  $K$  is the symmedian point of triangle  $ABC$ ).  In fact, $\bullet$   $F$  is the second point of intersection of  $AK$  with the Brocard circle; in other words,  $F$  is the projection of  $O$  onto  $AK$ . $\bullet$   $F$  is a vertex of the [second Brocard triangle](http://mathworld.wolfram.com/SecondBrocardTriangle.html) of triangle  $ABC$ .

(These properties may be equivalent, depending on your definition of the second Brocard triangle.)

[asy]
pair symmedianpoint(pair A, pair B, pair C) {
  real sideA, sideB, sideC, xcoord, ycoord, zcoord;
  sideA = abs(B - C);
  sideB = abs(C - A);
  sideC = abs(A - B);
  xcoord = sideA^2;
  ycoord = sideB^2;
  zcoord = sideC^2;
  return((xcoord*A + ycoord*B + zcoord*C)/(xcoord + ycoord + zcoord));
};

