Let  $X=(BMP)\cap AM$ , and  $Y=(CMN)\cap AM$ . Let  $F$  be the point on  $(ANP)$  such that  $AF$  is a symmedian of  $ANP$ . Let  $G=AM\cap PN$ . We will show that  $F=BD\cap CE$ , which would solve the problem.

Note that  $O$  is on all three circles  $\omega_A=(APN),\omega_B=(BPM),\omega_C=(CMN)$ , and that the reflection of  $\omega_A$  in  $OP$  is  $\omega_B$ , and the other two cyclic variants. We will show that  $X$  and  $F$  are reflections in  $OP$ , and to do this, it suffices to show that  $PX=PF$  since  $X\in\omega_B$  and  $F\in\omega_A$ . Note that
\[PX/BM=AP/AM\implies PX = AP\cdot BM/AM = AP\cdot GN/AG.\]
Since  $AF$  is a symmedian, we have  $\triangle APF\sim\triangle AGN$ , so  $GN/AG=PF/AP$ , so  $PX=PF$ , as desired. Thus,  $X$  is the reflection of  $F$  in  $OP$ , and we can similarly show that  $Y$  is the reflection of  $F$  in  $ON$ .

Note that the reflection of  $DB$  in  $OP$  is  $AM$ , so since  $X\in AM$ , we have  $F\in DB$ . Similarly, we show that  $F\in EC$ , so  $F=DB\cap AC$ , as desired.

