Conversely, suppose  $T$  was *not* a spanning tree, i.e.\  $T \neq G$ . Since in this odd-odd case,  $G$  has one fewer edge than vertex, if  $G$  is not a tree, then it must contain at least one cycle. That cycle encloses every special square of  $T$ . In particular, this means that  $T$  can't contain any special squares from the outermost row or column of the  $n \times n$  grid. In this situation, we therefore have  $k(C) \le \left( \frac{n-3}{2} \right)^2$ .
