The integers from  $1$  to  $9$  can be arranged into a  $3\times3$   array (as shown on the right) so that the sum of the numbers in every row, column, and diago­nal is a multiple of  $9$ .

(a.) Prove that the number in the center of the array must be a multiple of  $3$ .

(b.)  Give an example of such an array with  $6$  in the center.

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10));size(100);
int i,j;
for(i=0; i<4; i=i+1) {
draw((0,2i)--(6,2i));
draw((2i,0)--(2i,6));
}
string[] letters={"G", "H", "I", "D", "E", "F", "A", "B", "C"};
for(i=0; i<3; i=i+1) {
for(j=0; j<3; j=j+1) {
label(letters[3i+j], (2j+1, 2i+1));
}}[/asy]

solution_md:
The four sums involving the center square,  $A + E + I$ ,  $B + E + H$ ,  $D + E + F$ , and  $C + E + G$  must each be multiples of  $9$ .

Therefore the sum  $A +B + C + D + F + G + H + I + 4E = 36k$  for some integer  $k$ , because it is a multiple of  $9 \times 4 = 36$ .

Since  $A + B + C + D + E + F + G + H + I = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 + 9 = 45$ , that means that  $3E + 45 = 36k$ .

Simplifying that, we get  $3E = 36k - 45$ , or  $E = 12k - 15$ .  Factoring gives  $E = 3(4k - 5)$ .  Since  $k$  is an integer,  $4k - 5$  is an integer, and  $E$  is three times an integer, which is, by definition, a multiple of  $3$ .

Why did I pick that approach?  The key question that I had when reading part  $A$  is "what's special about  $E$  that isn't true about all eight other boxes?"  Clearly, it's the fact that  $E$  is included in the totals of two diagonals -AND- an up-down -AND a left-right combo.   $A, C, G, I$  are only included in  $3$  such combos, while  $B, D, F, H$  are only included in  $2$  combos.

Now, let's fill in the  $6$ :

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10));size(100);
int i,j;
for(i=0; i<4; i=i+1) {
draw((0,2i)--(6,2i));
draw((2i,0)--(2i,6));
}
string[] letters={"G", "H", "I", "D", "6", "F", "A", "B", "C"};
for(i=0; i<3; i=i+1) {
for(j=0; j<3; j=j+1) {
label(letters[3i+j], (2j+1, 2i+1));
}}[/asy]

Now, every row must have a sum that is either  $9$  or  $18$ .  ( $27$  is too big for a sum of three distinct one digit numbers, and clearly  $0$  is too small.)  That means each pair of numbers  $(A, I), (B,H), (C, G), (D,F)$  must have a sum that's either  $3$  or  $12$ .  Since  $A + B + C + D + F + G+ H + I = 45 - 6 = 39 = 3 \times 12 + 1 \times 3$ , we must have three sums of  $12$  and one sum of  $3$ .  That means that we immediately know that  $(1,2)$  are paired up.  The other pairs are  $(9,3)$ ,  $(8,4)$ , and  $(7,5)$ .

There are only two distinct places to place  $(8,4)$ :  in either  $(A,I)$  or  $(B,H)$ .  In any other position, it's a mere reflection or rotation.  Let's try  $8$  and  $4$  in a column:

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10));size(100);
int i,j;
for(i=0; i<4; i=i+1) {
draw((0,2i)--(6,2i));
draw((2i,0)--(2i,6));
}
string[] letters={"G", "8", "I", "D", "6", "F", "A", "4", "C"};
for(i=0; i<3; i=i+1) {
for(j=0; j<3; j=j+1) {
label(letters[3i+j], (2j+1, 2i+1));
}}[/asy]

Now we know that  $G + I = 10$ .  Then they are either  $7,3$  or  $9,1$ .  Plugging in  $7,3$  would give  $2,9$  on top for  $A$  and  $C$ , which gives a sum that's not a multiple of  $9$  for  $A + B + C$ .  Thus, we must have  $9,1$  on the bottom, with order not mattering due again to symmetry:

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10));size(100);
int i,j;
for(i=0; i<4; i=i+1) {
draw((0,2i)--(6,2i));
draw((2i,0)--(2i,6));
}
string[] letters={"9", "8", "1", "D", "6", "F", "A", "4", "C"};
for(i=0; i<3; i=i+1) {
for(j=0; j<3; j=j+1) {
label(letters[3i+j], (2j+1, 2i+1));
}}[/asy]

This will put  $2,3$  on top:

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10));size(100);
int i,j;
for(i=0; i<4; i=i+1) {
draw((0,2i)--(6,2i));
draw((2i,0)--(2i,6));
}
string[] letters={"9", "8", "1", "D", "6", "F", "2", "4", "3"};
for(i=0; i<3; i=i+1) {
for(j=0; j<3; j=j+1) {
label(letters[3i+j], (2j+1, 2i+1));
}}[/asy]

This leaves  $5$  and  $7$  leftover, which fit in nicely:

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10));size(100);
int i,j;
for(i=0; i<4; i=i+1) {
draw((0,2i)--(6,2i));
draw((2i,0)--(2i,6));
}
string[] letters={"9", "8", "1", "7", "6", "5", "2", "4", "3"};
for(i=0; i<3; i=i+1) {
for(j=0; j<3; j=j+1) {
label(letters[3i+j], (2j+1, 2i+1));
}}[/asy]

That's the only solution where rotations and reflections are ignored, and where  $4$  and  $8$  are placed on a row.  What if  $4$  and  $8$  are in the corner, like this:

[asy]
defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)+fontsize(10));size(100);
int i,j;
for(i=0; i<4; i=i+1) {
draw((0,2i)--(6,2i));
draw((2i,0)--(2i,6));
}
string[] letters={"G", "H", "4", "D", "6", "F", "8", "B", "C"};
for(i=0; i<3; i=i+1) {
for(j=0; j<3; j=j+1) {
label(letters[3i+j], (2j+1, 2i+1));
}}[/asy]

I leave it as a challenge to see if this configuration is possible.  (Warning:  symmetry has broken down significantly in this configuration!)

