First lets deal with he minimum value. The minimum  $ m$  of the length of the side can be written as the average of the sum of all the sides (since all sides have an equal value), so  $ m = \frac {(a + b + c) + ( c + d + e) + (e + f + g) + (g + h + i) + (i + j + a)}{5}$  or  $ m = \frac {(a + b + c + d + e + f + g + h + i + j) + (a + c + e + g + i)}{5}$ . We know that  $ a + b + c + \dots + j = 1 + 2 + 3 \dots + 10 = 55$  so we can rewrite  $ m$  as  $ m = \frac {55 + a + c + e + g + i}{5}$  or  $ m - 11 = \frac {a + c + e + g + i}{5}$ . We want to minimize  $ m$ , so to do that we need to minimize  $ a + c + e + g + i$ , which clearly occurs when  $ a,c,e,g,i$  range from 1-5. Then  $ m - 11 = \frac {15}{5}$  and  $ \boxed{minimum = 14}$ Now, just for fun, lets apply the same concept to find the maximum
For the maximum  $ M$ , we want to maximize  $ a + c + e + g + i$  which clearly occurs when  $ a,c,e,g,i$  range from 6 - 10. Then  $ M - 11 = \frac {40}{5}$  and  $ \boxed{Maximum = 19}$  

