We will look at the cross sections using planes perpendicular to the  $z$ -axis to find an area function, and we will integrate it to compute the volume. Let  $A = \begin{bmatrix}0  0 0\end{bmatrix}$ ,  $B = \begin{bmatrix}3  0 0\end{bmatrix}$ ,  $C = \begin{bmatrix}3  3 0\end{bmatrix}$ ,  $D = \begin{bmatrix}0  3 0\end{bmatrix}$ ,  $P = \begin{bmatrix}1  1 3\end{bmatrix}$ , and  $Q = \begin{bmatrix}2  2 3\end{bmatrix}$ . We will work with  $P$  first. It is well known that a vector collinear with two other vectors is a weighted mean of the two vectors. The lines  $AP, BP, CP, DP$  are defined by
\begin{align*} AP & = (1-t)A + tP = \begin{bmatrix} t  t 3t\end{bmatrix} 
BP & = (1-t)B + tP = \begin{bmatrix} 3 - 2t  t  3t \end{bmatrix}
CP & = (1-t)C + tP = \begin{bmatrix} 3 - 2t  3-2t  3t \end{bmatrix} 
DP & = (1-t)D + tP = \begin{bmatrix} t  3-2t  3t \end{bmatrix}
\end{align*}
If we find the intersection points of these four lines with the plane  $z = h$  where  $h \in [0,3]$ , then we get
\[h = 3t \implies t = \frac h3 \implies A'_P =\begin{bmatrix} \frac h3  \frac h3  h\end{bmatrix},B'_P = \begin{bmatrix}3 -\frac{2h}3  \frac h3  h\end{bmatrix}, C'_P = \begin{bmatrix} 3 -\frac{2h}3  3 -\frac{2h}3  h \end{bmatrix}, D'_P = \begin{bmatrix}\frac h3  3 -\frac{2h}3  h \end{bmatrix}\]
where  $X'_P$  represents the intersection of  $XP$  with the plane for  $X \in \{A,B,C,D\}$ . We can do the same thing with  $Q$  to get
\[A'_Q = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{2h}3  \frac{2h}3  h \end{bmatrix}, B'_Q = \begin{bmatrix} 3 - \frac h3  \frac{2h}3  h \end{bmatrix}, C'_Q = \begin{bmatrix} 3 - \frac h3  3 - \frac h3  h \end{bmatrix}, D'_Q = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{2h}3  3 - \frac h3  h\end{bmatrix}\]
If we view the points from the plane, we should see
