We see that the intersection of the two squares is the area that we need. Luckily, from symmetry, we know that this area is a square and it has diagonal  $A'_Q C'_P$ . The area can be given by 
\[A(h) = \frac{d^2}2 = \frac12 |\begin{bmatrix} \frac{2h}3  \frac{2h}3  h \end{bmatrix} - \begin{bmatrix} 3 -\frac{2h}3  3 -\frac{2h}3  h \end{bmatrix}|^2 = (\frac{4h}3 - 3)^2.\]
Now we must determine the bounds of the integral. We must start at  $h=0$ , and go to the point where the area is 0. The area function is 0 when  $\frac{4h}3 - 3 = 0 \implies h = \frac 94$ . Thus our integral is
\begin{align*}
\int^\frac94_0{(\frac 43 h - 3 )^2 \, dh} & = \int^\frac94_0{(\frac{16}{9}h^2 - 8h + 9) \, dh} 
& = \left( \frac{16}{27}h^3 - 4h^2 + 9h)|^\frac94_0 
& = \frac{16}{27}\cdot\frac{729}{64} - \frac{81}{4} + \frac{81}{4} 
& = \frac {27}4
\end{align*}
so the volume is  $\frac{27}{4}$ .

