<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let the strengths of the  $20$  players be  $a_1, a_2, \cdots, a_{20}$ . In what follows, the notation  $(a_i, a_j, \cdots, a_k)$  represents a team composed of  $a_i, a_j, \cdots a_k$ . We claim the matches 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1. \hspace{0.2in} (a_1, a_2, \cdots, a_{10}) & \textbf{ vs. } & (a_{11}, a_{12}, \cdots, a_{20}) 
2. \hspace{0.2in} (a_1, a_2, \cdots, a_9, a_{20}) & \textbf{ vs. } & (a_{11}, a_{12}, \cdots, a_{19}, a_{10}) 
3. \hspace{0.2in} (a_1, a_2, \cdots, a_{8}, a_{19}, a_{20} & \textbf{ vs. } & (a_{11}, a_{12}, \cdots, a_{18}, a_9, a_{10}) 
& \vdots & 
10. \hspace{0.2in} (a_1, a_{12}, a_{13}, \cdots a_{20}) & \textbf{ vs. } & (a_{11}, a_2, a_3, \cdots a_{20}) \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
satisfy the problem. 

First, we show that two consecutive games cannot both result in ties. WLOG, suppose that games 1 and 2 both result in ties. Then,
\begin{eqnarray*}
a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{10} & = & a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{20} 
a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_9 + a_{20} & = & a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{19} + a_{10} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
Subtracting these two equations, we get
\begin{eqnarray*}
a_{10} - a_{20} & = & a_{20} - a_{10} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
It follows that  $a_{10} = a_{20}$ , contradicting the fact that all players have distinct strengths. 

Now, we show that if some game results in a tie, the USAMTS team can select a valid representative. We know that if game 2 results in a tie (other games resulting in ties have identical proofs), game 1 must result in one team winning. Hence, we either have  $a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{10} > a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{20}$ , or  $a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{10} < a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{20}$ . WLOG, suppose the former case. Then, \begin{eqnarray}
a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{10} & > & a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{20} 
a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_9 + a_{20} & = & a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{19} + a_{10} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray}
Subtracting (2) from (1), we get
\begin{eqnarray*}
a_{10} - a_{20} & > & a_{20} - a_{10} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
It follows that  $a_{10} > a_{20}$ . Therefore, person  $10$  is not the weakest person on the team, so he is a valid representative. This completes the proof of this case. 

Let the first team in a matchup be team  $1$  and the second team be team  $2$  (i.e., in matchup 1,  $(a_1, a_2, \cdots, a_{10})$  is team  $1$  and  $(a_{11}, a_{12}, \cdots, a_{20})$  is team  $2$ ). We show that if team  $1$  wins in some matchup and loses in the next (or vice versa), the USAMTS staff can select a valid representative. WLOG, suppose team  $1$  wins matchup  $1$  and loses matchup  $2$ . Then,
\begin{eqnarray*}
a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{10} & > & a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{20}  
a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_9 + a_{20} & < & a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{19} + a_{10} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
Since these two inequalities have different signs, we can subtract the second one from the first. This yields
\begin{eqnarray*}
a_{10} - a_{20} & > & a_{20} - a_{10} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
Hence,  $a_{10} > a_{20}$ , so person  $10$  is not the weakest person on the team. This completes the proof of this case. 

If neither of the above cases takes place, all games must result in team  $1$  winning, or all games must result in team  $2$  winning. WLOG, suppose the latter case. Matchups  $1$  and  $10$  yield
\begin{eqnarray*}
a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{10} & > & a_{11} + a_{12} + \cdots + a_{20} 
a_1 + a_{12} + a_{13} + \cdots + a_{20} & > & a_{11} + a_2 + a_3 + \cdots + a_{10} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
Adding these two inequalities, we get
\begin{eqnarray*}
2a_1 + (a_2 + a_3 + \cdots + a_{10} + a_{12} + a_{13} + \cdots + a_{20}) & > & 2a_{11} + (a_2 + a_3 + \cdots + a_{10} + a_{12} + a_{13} + \cdots + a_{20}) 
2a_1 & > & 2a_{11} \mbox{.}
\end{eqnarray*}
Hence,  $a_1 > a_{11}$ . This completes the proof of this case. 

Thus, the USAMTS staff can choose a valid representative using a tournament with  $10$  rounds, as desired.  $\Box$</details>

