We define the \textit{total sum} of all the mangoes as the sums of the values of the positions of each of the mangoes. For example, when  $2019$  mangoes are at position  $0,$  the total sum is  $2019.$  

We observe what happens to the total sum throughout the process of sharing and eating. When we eat a mango, we originally have two mangoes at position  $x$  with total value  $2^{x + 1}.$  When we eat a mango, we pass one to the right, hence giving it a value of  $2^{x + 1}.$  Therefore, the total sum remains the same. 

When we share a mango, let the three mangoes we want to share be at position  $z.$  Then, we have a total value of  $3 \cdot 2^z.$  When we share, we pass two mangoes to the right, giving them a combined value of  $2 \cdot 2^{z - 1} = 2^z,$  and one mango to the left, giving it a value of  $2^{z + 1}.$  Therefore, the total value is  $2^{z + 1} + 2^z = 3 \cdot 2^z,$  so it also remains unchanged. 

We can conclude that no matter what sequence of sharing and eating we do, the total sum of the mangoes will always be  $2019.$  Therefore, at the ending position, it will also be  $2019.$  

Consider the ending position. Everyone will have  $0$  or  $1$  mangoes, and each mango that is present represents a power of  $2.$  Furthermore, the total sum of all the mangoes is  $2019.$  This is the definition of  $2019$  when expressed in base  $2.$  

As  $2019 = 11111100011_2,$  we can conclude that the number of people with mangoes is equivalent to the number of  $1$ s in this number. Since there are  $8$  ones, there are  $8$  people with mangoes at the end, so we have proved that there will be  $8$  people that are stuck with mangoes. \hfill  $\blacksquare$</details>

My <details><summary>Solution:</summary>4/1/31: Let a  $pseudo-binary$  representation of a number  $x$  be a string of  $n$  digits  $d_{n}d_{n-1}d_{n-2}...d_{0}$  for  $any$  non-negative integers  $d_{i}$  such that   $$ x = \sum_{i=0}^{n} 2^{i}d_{i}. $$   For example, one of the infinitely many pseudo-binary representations of the decimal number  $25$  is  $97,$  because  $9*2^1 + 7*2^0 = 25.$ \vspace*{12px}

Let each friend be one digit of a pseudo-binary number, and let the number of lemons a friend has be the value of their digit. Additionally, since at most  $2/3$  of a digit's value can be passed to the left and at most  $1/2$  can be passed to the right, we do not need to worry about the fact that the friends are in a circle. In other words, we only have to consider the friends that can receive mangos, which form a line.

\vspace*{12px}



When the friend corresponding to the  $n$ th digit shares, the  $(n-1)$ th digit increases by  $2$ ,the  $n$ th digit decreases by  $3$ , and the  $(n+1)$ th digit increases by  $1.$  Therefore, the total value of these three digits (which are the only ones that are affected) increases by  $2*2^{n-1}-3*2^{n}+1*2^{n+1}=2^n - (2*2^n + 2^n) + 2^{n+1}=2^n-2^{n+1}-2^n+2^{n+1}=0.$  Thus, sharing does not change the value of the pseudo-binary number.

\vspace*{12px}

When the friend corresponding to the  $n$ th digit eats, the  $n$ th digit decreases by  $2$  and the  $n+1$ th digit increases by  $1.$  Therefore, the total value of these two digits increases by  $-2*2^n+1*2^{n+1}=-2^{n+1}+2^{n+1}=0.$  Thus, eating does not change the value of the pseudo-binary number. 

\vspace*{12px}

The above two paragraphs show that no matter what the friends do and in what order, the value of the pseudo-binary number they form remains constant. The initial configuration had value  $2019*2^i,$  where the friend who started with the 2019 mangos corresponds to the  $i$ th digit, so the final pseudo-binary number must also have this value. We know the final configuration can only contain  $0$ s and  $1$ s, so it is the binary representation of  $2019*2^i,$  which is  $11111100011_2*(10_2)^i.$  We only care about the number of  $1$ s, because these represent people with one mango. Regardless of  $i,$  there are  $8$  ones.

\vspace*{12px}

Thus, exactly eight people are stuck with mangos that cannot be shared or eaten.</details>

