<details><summary>Lengthy Solution</summary>\textit{Note: We use  $\dfrac{d}{dx}(f(x))$  to denote the derivative of  $f(x).$ } \newline

This solution is lengthy and involves calculus bashing. Here is the outline of our solution: 

\begin{enumerate}
\item Set up the diagram in the cartesian coordinate plane, with  $U = (0, 0),$  and scaling  $PLUM$  to a  $2\times2$  square so that  $P = (2, 2).$  (This simplifies our calculations.) 
\item Let  $k$  be the slope of  $\overline{UE},$  and find the coordinates of  $I$  and  $E$  in terms of  $k.$  
\item Use the shoelace formula to find the area of  $\triangle PIE$  in terms of  $k.$  
\item Differentiate this in  $k,$  and find when this derivative is positive. This tells us when the area of  $\triangle PIE$  is maximized. 
\item Calculate the area of  $\triangle PIE$  at this value of  $k.$  
\end{enumerate}
(Continued on page 2.) \newpage

\phantom{https://artofproblemsolving.com/texer/xoarzmwm}
[asy]
size(10cm);
dotsize();

path omega = circle((1, 1), 1);
pair P, L, U, M, I, E;

P = (2, 2); L = (0, 2); U = (0, 0); M = (2, 0); 
I = (1, 1) + rotate(120, (0, 0)) * (1, 0); 
E = (1, 1) + rotate(210, (0, 0)) * (1, 0);

draw(omega); 
draw(P -- L -- U -- M -- cycle); 
draw(U -- E);
draw(P -- I -- E -- cycle); 

dot(P); dot(L); dot(U); dot(M); dot(I); dot(E); 
label(" $P$ ", P, NE); 
label(" $L$ ", L, NW); 
label(" $U$ ", U, SW); 
label(" $M$ ", M, SE); 
label(" $I$ ", I, NW); 
label(" $E$ ", E + (-0.01, 0.001), SW); 
[/asy]

We let  $U = (0, 0)$  and scale the diagram such that  $P = (2, 2).$  Thus, we must divide our answer by  $2 ^ 2$  at the end, since our answer is an area. 

Let the line  $UE$  which passes through  $I$  have the equation   $$ y = kx. $$   

We know  $\omega$  has the equation   $$ (x - 1) ^ 2 + (y - 1) ^ 2 = 1, $$   

since it has center  $(1, 1)$  and radius  $1.$  We wish to find  $I$  and  $E$  in terms of  $k.$  For this, we can substitute  $y = kx$  into the equation for  $\omega$  to get 

\begin{align*}
&& (x - 1) ^ 2 + (kx - 1) ^ 2 &= 1  
\iff&& x ^ 2 - 2x + 1 + k ^ 2 x ^ 2 - 2kx + 1 &= 1  
\iff&& (k ^ 2 + 1) x ^ 2 - 2(k + 1)x + 1 &= 0.  
\end{align*}

Now, we can use the quadratic formula to solve for  $x$  and get two roots,  $r_1$  and  $r_2.$  

\begin{align*}
r_{1, 2} &= \dfrac{2k + 1 \pm \sqrt{4(k ^ 2 + 2k + 1) - 4(k ^ 2 + 1)}}{2k ^ 2 + 2}  
&= \dfrac{2k + 1 \pm \sqrt{8k}}{2k ^ 2 + 2}.  
\end{align*}

Therefore, we can let   $$ r_1 = \dfrac{2k + 1 - \sqrt{8k}}{2k ^ 2 + 2}, r_2 = \dfrac{2k + 1 + \sqrt{8k}}{2k ^ 2 + 2}, $$   

where  $E$  has  $x$ -coordinate  $r_1;$  then, since it lies on  $y = kx,$  we must have  $E = (r_1, kr_1).$  

Similarly,  $I = (r_2, kr_2).$  We can now use the shoelace formula to find the area of  $\triangle PIE,$  knowing that  $P = (2, 2).$  

\begin{align*}
[\triangle PIE] &= \dfrac12|(2kr_1 + kr_1r_2 + 2r_2) -(2r_1 + kr_1r_2 + 2kr_2)|  
&= \dfrac12|2kr_1 - 2r_2 - 2r_1 - 2kr_2|  
&= \dfrac12|2k(r_1 - r_2) - 2(r_1 - r_2)|  
&= \dfrac12|2(k - 1)(r_1 - r_2)|  
&= |(k - 1)(r_1 - r_2)|.  
\end{align*}

We can arbitrarily let  $k > 1,$  because the diagram is symmetric about  $PU.$  In that case, we could take take the slope of  $\overline{UE}$  as  $\frac{1}{k},$  and the diagram would remain symmetric about  $PU$  (because line  $PU$  has equation  $y = x$ ), thus the area of  $\triangle PIE$  would remain the same. 

Furthermore, we know   $$ r_1 - r_2 = \dfrac{2\sqrt{8k}}{2k ^ 2 + 2} = \dfrac{2\sqrt{2k}}{k ^ 2 + 1}, $$  and that is positive, since square roots are positive and  $k ^ 2 + 1 \ge 1 > 0$  by the trivial inequality. Thus, we can substitute  $r_1 - r_2$  into our expression for  $[\triangle PIE]$  and remove the absolute value signs, since both  $k - 1$  and  $r_1 - r_2$  are positive.   $$ [\triangle PIE] = (k - 1)\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2k}}{k ^ 2 + 1}\right). $$   

We wish to maximize this expression. Let this be a function of  $x;$  that is,   $$ a(x) = (x - 1)\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right). $$   

Then  $[\triangle PIE] = a(k),$  where, again,  $k$  is the slope of  $UI.$  

We want to find the value of  $x$  such that  $a$  is maximized. This is equivalent to finding when  $a$  stops increasing and starts decreasing, which is the same as when  $a'$  goes from positive to negative, or when  $a'(x) = 0.$  Thus we wish to find the roots of  $a'.$  Recall that we decided that  $k > 1,$  so we are only looking for roots greater than 1. \newline

First, we use the product rule on  $a(x),$  finding the derivatives of  $x - 1$  and  $\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}.$  Obviously,  $\dfrac{d}{dx}(x - 1)$  is just  $1$  by the power rule. 

We can proceed by finding  $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right)$  using the quotient rule. First, we find the derivative of the numerator: 

\begin{align*}
\dfrac{d}{dx}(2\sqrt{2x}) &= 2 \cdot \dfrac{d}{dx}(\sqrt{2x})  
\dfrac{d}{dx}(2\sqrt{2x}) &= 2 \cdot \dfrac{d}{dx}\left((2x) ^ \frac12\right)  
&= 2\cdot \left(\dfrac12 \cdot (2x) ^ {-\frac12}\right)  
&= \sqrt{\dfrac2x}.  
\end{align*}

The derivative of the denominator,  $x ^ 2 + 1,$  is  $\dfrac{d}{dx}(x ^ 2) = 2x$  by the power rule. We can now use the quotient rule to find the derivative of  $\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}.$  

\begin{align*}
\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right) &= \dfrac{\sqrt{\dfrac2x} \cdot (x ^ 2 + 1) - 2x \cdot 2\sqrt{2x}}{(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2}  
&= \dfrac{\sqrt{\dfrac2x \cdot (x ^ 2) ^ 2} + \sqrt{\dfrac2x} - 4\sqrt{2x \cdot x ^ 2}}{(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2}  
&= \dfrac{\sqrt{2x ^ 3} + \sqrt{\dfrac2x} - 4\sqrt{2x ^ 3}}{(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2}  
&= \dfrac{\sqrt{\dfrac2x} - 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3}}{(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2}.  
\end{align*}

We can now use the product rule to find the roots of  $a'.$  

\begin{align*}
a'(x) &= \dfrac{d}{dx}\left((x - 1)\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right)\right)  
&= \dfrac{d}{dx}(x - 1) \cdot \left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right) + \dfrac{d}{dx}\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right) \cdot (x - 1)  
&= 1 \cdot \left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right) + \dfrac{\sqrt{\dfrac2x} - 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3}}{(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2} \cdot (x - 1).  
\end{align*}

We know that  $(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2 \cdot a'(x)$  will have the same roots as  $a',$  because any value of  $x$  with  $a'(x) = 0$  will also satisfy  $(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2 \cdot a'(x) = 0.$  

This will introduce new roots where  $(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2 = 0,$  which has roots when  $x ^ 2 + 1 = 0 \implies x ^ 2 = -1 \implies x = \sqrt{-1},$  which is non-real, so we can ignore those roots. 

\begin{align*}
(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2 \cdot a'(x) &= (x ^ 2 + 1) \cdot \left(\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2x}}{x ^ 2 + 1}\right) + \dfrac{\sqrt{\dfrac2x} - 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3}}{(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2} \cdot (x - 1)\right)  
&= 2\sqrt{2x}(x ^ 2 + 1) + \left(\sqrt{\dfrac2x} - 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3}\right)(x - 1)  
&= (2\sqrt{2x} \cdot x ^ 2 + 2\sqrt{2x}) + \left(x\sqrt{\dfrac2x} - 3x\sqrt{2x ^ 3} - \sqrt{\dfrac2x} + 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3}\right)  
&= 2\sqrt{2x ^ 5} + 2\sqrt{2x} + \sqrt{2x} - 3\sqrt{2x ^ 5} - \sqrt{\dfrac2x} + 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3}  
(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2 \cdot a'(x) &= -\sqrt{2x ^ 5} + 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3} + 3\sqrt{2x} - \sqrt{\dfrac2x}.  
\end{align*}

If this expression  $(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2 \cdot a'(x) = 0$  and  $(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2 \not = 0 \iff x \not = \pm i,$  then we have  $a'(x) = 0$  (dividing both sides by  $(x ^ 2 + 1) ^ 2$ ). Thus, we wish to find  $x \not = \pm i$  such that 

\begin{align*}
&& -\sqrt{2x ^ 5} + 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3} + 3\sqrt{2x} - \sqrt{\dfrac2x} &= 0  
\iff&& 3\sqrt{2x ^ 3} + 3\sqrt{2x} &= \sqrt{2x ^ 5} + \sqrt{\dfrac2x}  
\iff&& (3\sqrt{2x ^ 3} + 3\sqrt{2x}) ^ 2 &= \left(\sqrt{2x ^ 5} + \sqrt{\dfrac2x}\right) ^ 2  
\iff&& 9(2x ^ 3) + 18(\sqrt{4x ^ 4}) + 9(2x) &= (2x ^ 5) + 2\left(\sqrt{2x ^ 5 \cdot \dfrac2x}\right) + \dfrac2x  
\iff&& 18x ^ 3 + 18x + 36x ^ 2 &= 2x ^ 5 + 4x ^ 2 + \dfrac2x  
\iff&& -2x ^ 5 + 18x ^ 3 + 32x ^ 2 + 18x - \dfrac2x &= 0  
\iff&& -2x ^ 6 + 18x ^ 4 + 32x ^ 3 + 18x ^ 2 - 2 &= 0  
\iff&& x ^ 6 - 9x ^ 4 - 16x ^ 3 - 9x ^ 2 + 1 &= 0.  
\end{align*}

Now, we factor. Looking at easy roots, we find that  $-1$  is a root, so we can factor out an  $x + 1.$  We notice that  $-1$  is yet again a root, so we factor out another  $(x + 1).$  It turns out that this polynomial has  $4$  factors of  $x + 1,$  and we end up with   $$ x ^ 6 - 9x ^ 4 - 16x ^ 3 - 9x ^ 2 + 1 = (x + 1) ^ 4 (x ^ 2 - 4x + 1). $$   

We do not care about the root  $-1,$  because remember that  $k$  is positive. Thus, we solve  $x ^ 2 - 4x + 1 = 0.$  Using the quadratic formula yields 

\begin{align*}
x &= \dfrac{4 \pm \sqrt{(-4) ^ 2 - 4(1)(1)}}{2(1)}  
&= \dfrac{4 \pm \sqrt{12}}{2}  
&= 2 \pm \sqrt{3}.  
\end{align*}

Thus,  $a$  is maximized at  $x = 2 \pm \sqrt{3},$  so the maximum area of  $[\triangle PIE]$  is obtained when  $k = 2 \pm \sqrt{3}.$  However, note that we said  $k > 1,$  and  $2 - \sqrt{3} < 1,$  so we have  $k = 2 + \sqrt{3}$  - note that  $2 - \sqrt{3}$  yields the same diagram, just reflected over  $PU.$  

Now all we must do is find  $a(2 + \sqrt{3}).$  We have 

\begin{align*}
a(2 + \sqrt{3}) &= (2 + \sqrt{3} - 1)\left(\dfrac{2\sqrt{2(2 + \sqrt{3})}}{(2 + \sqrt{3}) ^ 2 + 1}\right)  
&= \dfrac{(1 + \sqrt{3}) \cdot 2\sqrt{4 + 2\sqrt{3}}}{4 + 4\sqrt{3} + 3 + 1}  
&= \dfrac{(1 + \sqrt{3}) \cdot 2(1 + \sqrt{3})}{8 + 4\sqrt{3}}  
&= \dfrac{2(4 + 2\sqrt{3})}{8 + 4\sqrt{3}}  
&= 1.  
\end{align*}

Hence, the maximum area of  $[\triangle PIE]$  is 1. However, we must scale this area by  $\dfrac14$  because we scaled the diagram up by a factor of 2 at the beginning, and area is proportional to the square of the linear scale factor. Thus, our answer is   $$ \boxed{\dfrac14}. $$</details>

