The maximum possible area of  $\triangle PIE$  is  $\frac{1}{4}.$  

Let  $O$  be the center of circle  $\omega.$  We set  $O$  to be the origin of the Cartesian Plane, with the sides of the square parallel to the axes. Therefore, we can see that   $$ \displaystyle P = \left(-\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2}\right), $$   and similarly,   $$ L = \displaystyle \left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2}\right), $$    $$ U = \displaystyle \left(\frac{1}{2}, -\frac{1}{2}\right), $$    $$ \displaystyle M = \left(-\frac{1}{2}, -\frac{1}{2}\right). $$   

Since circle  $\omega$  is centered at the origin and has radius  $\frac{1}{2},$  we get that the its equation is   $$ x^2 + y^2 = \frac{1}{4}. $$   

Let point  $E = (x, y).$  We first compute the area of  $\triangle PEU$  and then get  $\frac{IE}{UE}$  to get the area of  $\triangle PIE.$  

We know that the area of triangle  $PEU$  is  $\sqrt{2}$  multiplied by the distance from  $E$  to the line  $PU,$  or  $E$  to the line  $y = -x.$  This can be rewritten as  $x + y = 0,$  and the distance is now   $$ \frac{|x + y|}{\sqrt{2}}. $$   

Without loss of generality, let the points on line  $UE$  be  $U, I, E$  in that order. Therefore,  $E$  is in the first quadrant so the distance can be written as   $$ \frac{x + y}{\sqrt{2}}, $$   so the area of  $PEU$  is   $$ \frac{x + y}{2}. $$   

 It is clear that  $UE$  is   $$ \sqrt{\left(x - \frac{1}{2}\right)^2 + \left(y + \frac{1}{2}\right)^2} = \sqrt{\frac{3}{4} - x + y}. $$   We now see that since  $I$  is on the circle, we have   $$ UI \cdot UE = \frac{1}{4} $$   from Power of a Point. Then   $$ UI = \frac{1}{4\sqrt{\frac{3}{4} - x + y}}. $$   Then,   $$ IE = \sqrt{\frac{3}{4} - x + y} - \frac{1}{4\sqrt{\frac{3}{4} - x + y}}. $$   

We can now compute the area of  $PIE.$  It is   $$ \frac{IE}{UI} \cdot \left(\frac{x + y}{2}\right) = \frac{\sqrt{\frac{3}{4} - x + y} - \frac{1}{4\sqrt{\frac{3}{4} - x + y}}}{\sqrt{\frac{3}{4} - x + y}} \cdot \frac{x + y}{2} = \left(1 - \frac{1}{4\left(\frac{3}{4} - x + y\right)}\right) \cdot \frac{x + y}{2}. $$   

Now, we can simplify even further, writing this as   $$ \frac{x + y}{2} - \frac{x + y}{6 - 8x + 8y} = \frac{(x + y)(3 - 4x + 4y) - (x + y)}{6 - 8x + 8y} = \frac{(x + y)(2 - 4x + 4y)}{6 - 8x + 8y}. $$   Cancelling out the factor of  $2,$  we have that the area is   $$ \frac{(x + y)(1 - 2x + 2y)}{3 - 4x + 4y}. $$   We now set this equal to  $\frac{1}{4}.$  

When   $$ \frac{(x + y)(1 - 2x + 2y)}{3 - 4x + 4y} = \frac{1}{4}, $$   we see   $$ 4(x + y)(1 - 2x + 2y) = 3 - 4x + 4y. $$   The left hand side can also be written as   $$ 4(x + y - 2x^2 + 2y^2) = 4x + 4y - 8x^2 + 8y^2 = 3 - 4x + 4y. $$   This means that   $$ 8x^2 - 8x + 3 = 8y^2 = 2 - 8x^2 \implies 16x^2 - 8x + 1 = 0. $$   

Since the discriminant of the quadratic is  $0,$  the graph of the area function intersects the line  $y = \frac{1}{4}$  at exactly one place. We can easily find this to be  $x = \frac{1}{4}.$  Furthermore, since the function is continuous in the range  $[-0.5, 0.5],$  which  $\frac{1}{4}$  is a part of, we can conclude that the graph is tangent to the line  $y = \frac{1}{4}.$  

Since the function has values smaller than  $\frac{1}{4},$  by the Intermediate Value Theorem,  $\frac{1}{4}$  is the maximal value of the function and hence the area of  $\triangle PIE,$  as desired. \hfill  $\blacksquare$</details> 

