Because each group of jesters has an even count, the median is the average of the two middle heights; we may write those heights as  $51-k,51+k$  inches for some positive  $k.$  The other four jesters in the group can have any other height, so long as two are shorter than  $51-k$  inches and the other two are taller than  $51+k$  inches. Then on a given day  $n$  and given middle heights  $51-k,51+k$ , the group count is  $$ \binom{(51-k)-1}{2}\binom{n-(51+k)}{2}=\binom{50-k}{2}\binom{n-k-51}{2}. $$  We can now start constructing a table in  $n,k$  with these group counts. The least  $n$  where groups of jesters may be formed is  $n=54$ , since any earlier day would not have enough jesters on the upper end (the second binomial coefficient in the above expression would be  $\binom{2-k}{2}$  or less—i.e., 0), and the range of  $k$  for a given  $n$  is  $1\leq k\leq n-53$ .

