For the column sum, we have from the hockey-stick identity that  $$ \text{sum of column }k=\binom{50-k}{2}\left(\sum_{i=0}^{47-k}\binom{2+i}{2}\right)=\binom{50-k}{2}\binom{50-k}{3} $$  And for the diagonal sum, we have similarly (where "diagonal  $n$ " describes the down-right diagonal starting with cell  $(n,1)$  in the table)  $$ \text{sum of diagonal }n=\binom{n-52}{2}\left(\sum_{i=n-51}^{49}\binom{i}{2}\right)=\binom{n-52}{2}\left(\binom{50}{3}-\binom{n-51}{3}\right). $$  Let  $n=101-m$ , so  $m$  ranges from 1 to 47. Then
\begin{align*}\text{sum of diagonal }n &= \binom{(101-m)-52}{2}\left(\binom{50}{3}-\binom{(101-m)-51}{3}\right)  &= \binom{49-m}{2}\left(\binom{50}{3}-\binom{50-m}{3}\right).\end{align*}
The total candied cherries from the first 100 days of courting is the sum of the above expressions for  $k=1\dots47$  and  $m=1\dots47$  which we will compute later. However, on the 101st day, the middle two jesters can instead have heights of  $k,101-k$  inches for  $k=3\dots50$ , and the number of ways to choose the remaining jesters is  $\binom{k-1}{2}\binom{100-(101-k)}{2}=\binom{k-1}{2}^2.$  Now, at last, we can find the total number of cherries Princess Pear receives by adding the sums of the columns and rows to the sum of  $\binom{i-1}{2}^2$  for  $i=3\dots50$  as above.
\begin{align*}
    \text{total candied cherries} &= \text{total column sum} + \text{total diagonal sum} + \text{sum on final day} 
    &= \left(\sum_{i=1}^{47}\binom{50-i}{2}\binom{50-i}{3}+\binom{49-i}{2}\left(\binom{50}{3}-\binom{50-i}{3}\right)\right) 
    &\quad~+\left(\sum_{i=3}^{50}\binom{i-1}{2}^2\right).
\end{align*}
Reindexing the first sum from  $i$  to  $50-i$  gives a new sum which ranges from  $i=3$  to  $49$ ; this can be combined with the other sum:
\begin{align*}
    \text{total candied cherries} &= \binom{50-1}{2}^2+\sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{50-(50-i)}{2}\binom{50-(50-i)}{3} 
    &\quad~+\binom{49-(50-i)}{2}\left(\binom{50}{3}-\binom{50-(50-i)}{3}\right)+\binom{i-1}{2}^2 
    &=\binom{49}{2}^2+\sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{2}\binom{i}{3}+\binom{i-1}{2}\left(\binom{50}{3}-\binom{i}{3}\right)+\binom{i-1}{2}^2 
    &=\binom{49}{2}^2+\sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{3}\left(\binom{i}{2}-\binom{i-1}{2}\right)+\binom{i-1}{2}\binom{50}{3}+\binom{i-1}{2}^2 
    &=\binom{49}{2}^2+\sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{3}\binom{i-1}{1}+\binom{i-1}{2}\binom{50}{3}+\binom{i-1}{2}^2
\end{align*}
The above sum can be separated into two sums, one consisting of the first and last  $i$  terms and the other consisting of the middle. The second can be directly evaluated with the hockey-stick identity:  $$ \sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i-1}{2}\binom{50}{3}=\binom{(49-1)+1}{2+1}\binom{50}{3}=\binom{49}{3}\binom{50}{3}. $$  The first can be decomposed carefully using Pascal's identity  $\binom{a}{b}+\binom{a}{b+1}=\binom{a+1}{b+1}$ :
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{3}\binom{i-1}{1}+\binom{i-1}{2}^2 &= \sum_{i=3}^{49}\left(\binom{i-1}{2}+\binom{i-1}{3}\right)\binom{i-1}{1}+\binom{i-1}{2}^2 
    &= \sum_{i=3}^{49}\left(\binom{i-1}{1}+\binom{i-1}{2}\right)\binom{i-1}{2}+\binom{i-1}{3}\binom{i-1}{1} 
    &= \sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{2}\binom{i-1}{2}+\binom{i-1}{3}\binom{i-1}{1} 
    &= \sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{2}\binom{i-1}{2}+\binom{i-1}{3}\left(\binom{i}{2}-\binom{i-1}{2}\right) 
    &= \sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{2}\left(\binom{i-1}{2}+\binom{i-1}{3}\right)-\binom{i-1}{3}\binom{i-1}{2} 
    &= \sum_{i=3}^{49}\binom{i}{3}\binom{i}{2}-\binom{i-1}{3}\binom{i-1}{2}.
\end{align*}
The sum telescopes, so the total is  $\binom{49}{3}\binom{49}{2}-\binom{2}{3}\binom{2}{2}=\binom{49}{3}\binom{49}{2}.$  Then at last, the full total is
\begin{align*}
    \binom{49}{2}^2+\binom{49}{2}\binom{49}{3}+\binom{49}{3}\binom{50}{3} &= \binom{49}{2}\left(\binom{49}{2}+\binom{49}{3}\right)+\binom{49}{3}\binom{50}{3} 
    &= \binom{49}{2}\binom{50}{3}+\binom{49}{3}\binom{50}{3} 
    &= \binom{50}{3}^2=\boxed{384160000\text{ candied cherries.}}
\end{align*}</details>

