<details><summary>Motivation/Sketch</summary>The  $3 \times 3$  grid can essentially tile the plane, and the grid is "citrus" if the pattern/coloring onlys repeats once every three blocks horizontally/vertically.</details>

<details><summary>Solution</summary>Moving the top row to the bottom twice is equivalent to moving the bottom row to the top. Let the block move that moves the top row to the bottom be a down move (D), and let the block move that moves the bottom row to the top be an up move (U). Similarly define a left move (L) and a right move (R). Classify U and D moves as vertical moves, and L and R moves as horizontal moves.

First observe that the order of moves does not matter. We now claim that there are two ways in which Sophia can change the board:

I. One horizontal move or vertical move
II. One horizontal move and one vertical move

To see this, express Sophia's net block movement as a composition of D and R moves. Suppose the net block movement was  $d$  D moves and  $r$  R moves. Then  $0 \leq d,r \leq 2$ . If either  $d=2$  or  $r=2$ , this is equivalent to one U and one L move respectively. So there's either zero or one instance of each a horizontal and vertical movement. Since we cannot have the identity block movement from  $d=r=0$ , the claim is clear.

Our strategy now will be to count the number of colorings that are not citrus. To do this, we can develop a method for classifying if a block coloring remains invariant under (I), and do the same for (II). Firstly, if a block coloring is invariant under (I), then it is easy to show that either each row is monocolor or each column is monocolor.

We now examine the case in which the block coloring is instead invariant under (II). If we label the blocks from 1 to 9, and assume WLOG that Sophia made a D and R move, the transformation of the grid will look like:

[asy]
size(5cm);
void drawGrid(pair x){
	for(int i=0; i<=3; ++i){
    	draw(x+(i,0)--x+(i,3));
    }
    for(int i=0; i<=3; ++i){
    	draw(x+(0,i)--x+(3,i));
    }
}
void drawCustomNumGrid(pair x, int[][] labels){
	drawGrid(x);
	for(int i=0; i<=2; ++i){
    	for(int j=0; j<=2; ++j){
                string s = (string) (labels[i][j]);
    		label(s,x+(j+.5,2.5-i));	 
    	}
    }
}
int[][] L = {{1,2,3},{4,5,6},{7,8,9}};
drawCustomNumGrid((0,0),L);
int[][] K = {{5,6,4},{8,9,7},{2,3,1}};
drawCustomNumGrid((5,0),K);
draw((3.2,1.5)--(4.8,1.5),Arrow);
[/asy]

By comparing the grids, we see blocks 1, 5, and 9 have to be the same color. Likewise, blocks 2, 6, 7 are the same color, and blocks 3, 4, 8 are the same color. Similar criteria can be found if Sophia made an U and R move instead.

We now accumulate our results. For case (I), there are  $2^3 = 8$  ways to color the blocks so that each row is monocolor, and 8 ways to color the blocks so that each column is monocolor. For all three columns and all three rows to be monocolor, the whole board is either all-orange or all-lime. So there are  $8+8-2=14$  boards that are invariant under (I). By a similar argument, there are  $8+8-2=14$  boards that are invariant under (II).

Finally, by inspection, the only boards that are invariant under both (I) and (II) are the all-orange and all-lime ones. Thus, there are  $14+14-2=26$  boards that are not citrus. This gives a probability of  $1-\dfrac{26}{512} = \boxed{\dfrac{243}{256}}$</details>

