I begin with  $2$  lemmas. \newline $\textbf{Lemma.}$  The circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  is the nine-point circle of  $\triangle I_AI_BI_C$ , and the orthocenter of  $\triangle I_AI_BI_C$  is  $I$ . \newline
\begin{proof}
It's well known that the internal angle bisector of an angle is perpendicular to the external angle bisector. In this case,  $AI_A$ , the internal angle bisector, is perpendicular to  $I_BI_C$ , the external angle bisector, and similarly  $BI_B \bot I_AI_C, CI_C \bot I_AI_B$ . Since the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  passes through  $A, B, C$ , which are altitude feet, it is the nine-point circle of  $\triangle I_AI_BI_C$ . Also, since  $I$  is the intersection of  $AI_A, BI_B, CI_C$ , which are altitudes, it is the orthocenter.
\end{proof} $\textbf{Lemma.}$  Contact  $\triangle DEF$  and excentral  $\triangle I_AI_BI_C$  are homothetic, with homothetic center  $P$ . \newline
\begin{proof}
By the tangent condition,  $AD = AE$ , so  $\triangle ADE$  is isosceles, and so the  $A$ -angle bisector is perpendicular to  $DE$ . Also, as established in the first lemma, the  $A$ -angle bisector is perpendicular to  $I_BI_C$ . Therefore,  $DE \parallel I_AI_B$ , and similarly  $EF \parallel I_BI_C, FD \parallel I_CI_A$ , so the two triangles are homothetic. $I_BE \cap I_CF = P$ , so by definition, it's the homothetic center. Note that  $I_AD$  also passes through  $P$ , because the lines concur at the homothetic center by definition.
\end{proof}
From the second lemma, the homothety that sends  $\triangle DEF$  to  $\triangle I_AI_BI_C$  sends the corresponding circumcenters of the two triangles to one another, i.e., it sends  $I$  to  $O_E$ . Since  $P$  is the homothetic center, we know  $P, I, O_E$  are collinear. From the first lemma,  $O$  is the nine-point center. It's well known that the nine-point center of a triangle is the midpoint of the segment connecting the orthocenter of the triangle and the circumcenter of the triangle. Therefore,  $O$  is the midpoint of  $I, O_E$ , i.e.  $O$  lies on  $IO_E$ . This means that  $I, O, P$  are collinear, so the problem just boils down to proving that  $\angle A = 60^{\circ}$  if the line through  $O$  perpendicular to  $OI$  passes through  $I_A$ . \newline
Since  $IO \bot OI_A$ ,  $O$  lies on the circle with diameter  $II_A$ . By the Incenter-Excenter Lemma, the center of this circle, call  $L$ , lies on the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$ . Then  $OL = R \implies IL = R$ . However, we can also express the length of  $IL$  in another way.\newline $\textbf{Lemma. } IL = 2R\sin{\frac{\angle A}{2}}$  \newline
