First we show that if  $D$  is the foot of the altitude from  $I$  to  $FG$ , then  $FT=GT$ . Let  $J, M,$  and  $N$  be the midpoints of segments  $FG, DF,$  and  $DG$  respectively. Let  $H$  be the orthocenter of  $\Delta IFG$  and let  $I'$  be the point diametrically opposite  $I$  on the circumcircle of  $\Delta IFG$  (note  $I'$  satisfies  $\angle IFI' = \angle IGI' = 90^\circ$ ). Finally, let  $J'$  be the reflection of  $D$  over  $J$ . We will show that  $TJ \perp FG$ , which will imply that  $\Delta TFG$  is iscosceles as desired.

First we show a well-known lemma.

Lemma:  $I'$  is the reflection of  $H$  over  $J$ . 

Proof: Take a homothety of ratio  $2$  at  $H$ , which sends the nine-point circle of  $\Delta FIG$  to the cirucumcircle of  $\Delta FIG$ . Since  $DJ$  is a chord of the nine point circle,  $D'I'$  must be a chord of the circumcircle of  $\Delta FIG$  that is parallel to line  $FG$  (because homothety preserves parallel lines), where  $D'$  is the image of  $D$ . Thus we can see that  $\angle ID'I' = 90^\circ$ , as desired.

Take a homothety of ratio  $2$  at  $D$ , which sends  $M$  and  $N$  to  $F$  and  $G$  respectively. Thus the line through  $M$  perpendicular to  $IF$  gets sent to the line through  $F$  perpendicular to  $IF$ , and similarly the line through  $N$  perpendicular to  $IG$  gets sent to the line through  $G$  perpendicular to  $IG$ . Thus the point  $T$  must be sent to the point  $I'$ , which we know is the point such that  $\angle I'FI = \angle I'GI = 90^\circ$ . Note  $J$  is sent to  $J'$ , so since we wanted to show  $TJ \perp FG$ , it is equivalent to show that  $I'J' \perp FG$ .

Consider a reflection of the entire figure about point  $J$  (that is, a homothety of ratio  $-1$ ). Note  $D$  is sent to  $J'$ , and  $H$  is sent to  $I'$  by the lemma. Thus triangles  $\Delta JDH$  and  $\Delta JJ'I'$  are congruent, meaning that  $\angle I'J'J = \angle HDJ = 90^\circ$ , as desired. Thus we have shown that  $TF=TG$ .

Now we show the other direction of the problem, i.e. that if  $TF=TG$ , then  $D$  is the foot of the altitude from  $I$  to  $FG$ . Let  $S$  be the foot of the altitude from  $I$  to  $FG$ ; we want to show  $S$  and  $D$  are the same point.

[asy]
unitsize(5cm);
