<details><summary>Sol</summary>Let  $x = \frac{1}{a}$  and  $y = \frac{1}{b}$ . Then we have \[\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)^{1/x} = \left(\frac{1}{y}\right)^{1/y}.\] Raising both sides to the  $xy$  power, we get \[\left(\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)^{1/x}\right)^{xy} = \left(\left(\frac{1}{y}\right)^{1/y}\right)^{xy} \implies \left(\frac{1}{x}\right)^{y} = \left(\frac{1}{y}\right)^{x} \implies \frac{1}{x^y} = \frac{1}{y^x} \implies x^y = y^x\,,\] where all these steps are defined because  $x$  and  $y$  are nonzero. Now since  $a$  and  $b$  are rational,  $x$  and  $y$  are rational. Hence it suffices to solve  $x^y = y^x$  over rationals, and then take the reciprocal of the results.

Let  $y = kx$  for some rational  $k \neq 1$ . Then we have  $x^{kx} = (kx)^x$ . Simplifying, we have
\begin{align*}
    x^{kx} = (kx)^x &\implies \left(x^k\right)^x = (kx)^x 
    &\implies kx = x^k \implies x^{k-1} = k
    &\implies x = k^{\frac{1}{k - 1}}
\end{align*}
Now since  $y = kx$ , we have  $y = k^{\frac{k}{k - 1}}$ . Since  $k$  is rational, let  $\frac{u}{v} = \frac{1}{k - 1}$ , where  $u$  and  $v$  are relatively prime positive integers. Then  $k - 1 = \frac{v}{u}$ , so  $k = \frac{u + v}{u}$ , and  $\frac{k}{k - 1} = \frac{u + v}{v}$ . Hence we have \[x = \left(\frac{u + v}{u}\right)^{u/v} \text{ and } y = \left(\frac{u + v}{u}\right)^{(u+v)/v}.\]

Since  $x$  and  $y$  are rationals, then  $\sqrt[v]{u}$  and  $\sqrt[v]{u+v}$  are integers, so  $u$  and  $u+v$  are perfect  $v-$ th powers. Let  $u = r^v$ . If  $v > 1$ , then  $r^v < u+v < (r+1)^v = \binom{v}{0} r^v + \binom{v}{1} r^{v-1} + \dots = u + vr^{v-1} + \dots$ . Therefore  $v=1$ , so  $x = \left(\frac{u+1}{u}\right)^u$  and  $y = \left(\frac{u+1}{u}\right)^{u+1}$  are all the solutions. Finally, changing back to our original variables, the solutions are \[\boxed{(a,b) = \left(\left(\frac{u}{u+1}\right)^{u+1}, \left(\frac{u}{u+1}\right)^{u}\right) \forall u \in \mathbb{Z}_{+}.}\]</details>

The answer is all pairs of the form  $(a,b) = \left( \frac{x^{x+1}}{(x+1)^{x+1}} , \frac{x^{x}}{(x+1)^{x}} \right)$  as  $x$  ranges over all positive integers. It is easy to see that all such pairs indeed satisfy  $a^a = b^b$  and  $b>a$ , since  $\frac{x}{x+1}<1$  and   $$ \left( \frac{x^{x+1}}{(x+1)^{x+1}} \right)^{x^{x+1}/(x+1)^{x+1}} = \left( \frac{x^x}{(x+1)^x} \right)^{x^x/(x+1)^x} $$  ; now we show these are the only ones. Relax the condition  $b>a$  to simply  $b \neq a$ .

Let  $a=\frac{m}{n}, b=\frac{r}{s}$  in simplest terms, so we want to solve  $\left( \frac{m}{n} \right)^{m/n} = \left( \frac{r}{s} \right)^{r/s}$  or equivalently   $$ \left( \frac{m}{n} \right)^{ms} = \left( \frac{r}{s} \right)^{rn} $$  . Since both fractions are already in simplest terms, we can compare the numerators and denominators to get
\begin{align*}
    m^{ms} &= r^{rn} 
    n^{ns} &= s^{rn} 
\end{align*}
. Let  $x= \frac{rn}{\text{gcd} (ms, rn)}, y=\frac{ms}{\text{gcd} (ms,rn)}$  (i.e.  $\frac{rn}{ms} = \frac{x}{y}$  in simplest terms). Note  $\text{gcd} (x,y) = 1$ . Then the above equations imply that there exists positive integers  $k,j$  such that  $m=k^x, r=k^y, n=j^x, s=j^y$  (also note  $\text{gcd} (k,j)=1$ ). WLOG  $y>x$  (recall that we relaxed the condition to  $b \neq a$ ; if  $y=x$  then we would have  $b=a$ ). Thus we want to solve the equation   $$ \left( \frac{k^x}{j^x} \right) ^{k^x/j^x} = \left( \frac{k^y}{j^y} \right) ^{k^y/j^y} $$  . Note this implies we must have   $$ \frac{k^x}{j^x} \cdot \frac{x}{y} = \frac{k^y}{j^y} $$   which is equivalent to   $$ \left( \frac{k}{j} \right)^{y-x} = \frac{x}{y} $$  . Since  $\text{gcd} (k,j) = \text{gcd} (x,y) = 1$ , we get that  $x,y$  are both  $y-x$ -th powers (recall we assumed  $y>x$ ). In other words if  $p=y-x$  then we have distinct positive integer  $p$ -th powers which differ by  $p$ ; this can only happen if  $p=1$  (for instance, if  $p \geq 2$  then we have  $y-x \geq 2^p-1^p>p$ ). Thus  $y=x+1$  and we get  $\frac{k}{j} = \frac{x}{x+1}$ , implying
\begin{align*}
a &= \frac{k^x}{j^x} = \frac{x^x}{(x+1)^x} 
b &= \frac{k^y}{j^y} = \frac{x^{x+1}}{(x+1)^{x+1}} 
\end{align*}. Since we assumed without loss of generality that  $y>x$ , we also have to consider the solution set  $(a,b) = \left( \frac{x^{x+1}}{(x+1)^{x+1}} , \frac{x^{x}}{(x+1)^{x}} \right)$ . But since the problem gives us that  $b>a$  the only actual solution set is the second one, i.e. the one described at the beginning.

