The only value of  $\frac{AB}{BC}$  is  $2 + \sqrt{3}$ .

Let  $\ell_A, \ell_B, \ell_C, \ell_D$  be the internal angle bisectors of  $\angle A, \angle B, \angle C, \angle D$  respectively. Define  $W = \ell_A \cap \ell_D, X = \ell_D \cap \ell_C, Y = \ell_C \cap \ell_B, Z = \ell_B \cap \ell_A$  and let  $E, F, G, H$  be the points on the perimeter of  $ABCD$  distinct from its vertices such that  $AD = AE, BC = BF, CB = CG, DA = DH$ , respectively. (These points exist because  $AD = BC < AB = CD$ .) We are given that  $[WXYZ] = [ABCD]$  (here brackets mean area).

First we will prove that the only diagram possible is the diagram above, hence eliminating configuration issues.

\medskip

\textbf{Claim 1.}  $WXYZ$  is a rectangle.

\textbf{Proof.}  $\angle XWZ = \angle AWD$  since  $X,W,D$  are collinear and  $A,W,Z$  are collinear. But  $\angle A + \angle D = 180^{\circ}$  since  $ABCD$  is a parallelogram. Therefore  $\angle AWD = 180^{\circ} - (\angle WAD + \angle ADW) = 180^{\circ} - (\frac{1}{2}\angle A + \frac{1}{2}\angle D) = 180^{\circ} - 90^{\circ} = 90^{\circ}$ . The process is the same for the rest of the angles of  $WXYZ$ .

\medskip

Now note that  $\angle AED = \angle ADE$  since  $AD = AE$ , and  $\angle EDC = \angle AED$  since  $ED$  is a transversal. Hence  $\angle ADE = \angle EDC$ , so  $E$  lies on  $\ell_D$ . Similarly, we can see that  $F$  lies on  $\ell_C$ ,  $G$  lies on  $\ell_B$ , and  $H$  lies on  $\ell_A$ .

\medskip

\textbf{Claim 2.} Points  $X$  and  $Z$  lie strictly outside of  $ABCD$ , and  $W$  and  $Y$  lie strictly inside it.

\textbf{Proof.} First note that  $ADHE$  is a rhombus, since  $AE = AD = HD$  and  $AE \parallel HD$ . Hence  $W$  is its center, since it the intersection of the angle bisectors of this rhombus. Therefore  $W$  lies strictly inside of  $ADHE$ , and so it lies inside of  $ABCD$ . Similarly,  $Y$  lies strictly inside of  $ABCD$ .

Now since  $W$  and  $Y$  are strictly inside of  $ABCD$ , if both  $X$  and  $Z$  are in or on  $ABCD$ , then  $[WXYZ] < [ABCD]$ . Hence at least one of  $X$  and  $Z$  are outside of  $ABCD$ . If exactly one if  $X$  and  $Z$  are outside of  $ABCD$ , then  $\angle WXY \neq WZY$ , contradiction (since  $WXYZ$  is a rectangle). Hence both  $X$  and  $Z$  lie outside of  $ABCD$ , as required.

\medskip

Now that we have eliminated configuration issues, let us prove the main crux of the problem.

\medskip

\textbf{Main Claim.} Triangles  $XEF, EAW, ADW, WDH, GHZ, YCG, BYC, BFY$  are all similar.

\textbf{Proof.} It is clear that triangles  $EAW, ADW, WDH, YCG, BYC, BFY$  are all similar because  $ADHE$  and  $BFGC$  are rhombii. Moreover, all of these six triangles are congruent, since  $AD = BC$  (implying the two rhombii are congruent).

Now note that, for example,  $\angle BFY = \angle XFE$  and  $\angle EXF = 90^{\circ} = \angle BYF$ , where the last equality comes from the fact that  $Y$  is the center of rhombus  $BFGC$  (which has perpendicular diagonals). Hence  $\triangle XEF \sim \triangle YBF$ , and similarly (no pun intended)  $\triangle YCG$  is similar to  $\triangle ZHG$ .

\medskip

Now we can utilize the area condition. Note that 
\begin{align*}
[WXYZ] &= [WEFYGZH] + [XEF] + [ZHG] \text{ and } 
[ABCD] &= [WEFYGZH] + [EAW] + [ADW] + [WDH] + [YCG] + [BYC] + [BFY].
\end{align*}
For simplicity, let  $[EAW] = a$  and  $[XEF] = ka$  for some constant of similarity  $k$ . Then  $[WXYZ] = [WEFYGZH] + 2ka$  and  $[ABCD] = [WEFYGZH] + 6a$ . But  $[WXYZ] = [ABCD]$ . Hence  $2ka=6a$ , so  $k=3$ . Therefore the ratio of the areas of  $XEF$  to  $EAW$  is  $3$ , so  $EF = \sqrt{3} \cdot BF$  (since both are hypotenuses). Therefore  $AB = AE + EF + FB = BF + \sqrt{3}BF + BF = (2+\sqrt{3})BF$ , and  $BF = BC$ , so \[\frac{AB}{BC} = \frac{(2+\sqrt{3})BF}{BF} = \boxed{2+\sqrt{3}}.\]</details>

