Now comes the key part of the argument: the angle bisectors imply that  $E,F,G,H$  are the centers of their respective drawn circles. This is analogous to the incenter of a degenerate-type triangle where two of the sides are parallel (and one vertex is the point at infinity). For example, note that since  $E$  lies on the angle bisector of  $\angle{BAD}$  it must be equidistant from  $AB$  and  $AD$ ; in addition, since it lies on the angle bisector of  $\angle{CDA}$  it must be equidistant from  $AD$  and  $CD$ , which gives the circle.

