The problem holds for suboptimal play; here is my solution NOT assuming optimal play. It's really long (4-5 pages double-spaced) but pretty intuitive. 

<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>I claim that Rosencrans wins for all odd  $n$ .

We define a configuration to be \emph{full} if no more chords can be drawn. We say that an arc has \emph{size}  $1$  if it does not contain any points on the interior of the arc, and we say that an arc has \emph{size}  $k$  if it consists of  $k$  arcs of size  $1$ . Finally, we define an \emph{angle} to be formed by two chords with a common endpoint, and we say that an angle is of \emph{size}  $k$  if it faces an arc of size  $k$ . Below: An angle and an arc of size  $1$  (left), An angle and an arc of size  $3$ .

[center]
[asy]
size(8cm);
import olympiad;
draw(circle(origin, 1));
dot(dir(240));
dot(dir(300));
dot(dir(270));
dot(dir(180));
dot(dir(0));
dot(dir(60));
draw(dir(180)--dir(0)--dir(240));
pair A = (3,0);
draw(circle(origin+A, 1));
dot(dir(240)+A);
dot(dir(300)+A);
dot(dir(270)+A);
dot(dir(180)+A);
dot(dir(0)+A);
dot(dir(60)+A);
draw(dir(180)+A--dir(60)+A--dir(300)+A);
[/asy]
[/center]
Let (1) denote the condition that for every arc of size  $1$ , there exists one angle of size  $1$  that faces that arc.

The \textbf{key claim} is this: A configuration is full if and only if it satisfies (1). 

We first show the following \textbf{lemma}: Given any two angles of arbitrary size, they cannot face arcs that intersect. 

\begin{figure}[h]
\caption{Two of the chords do not intersect if the arcs intersect.}
\centering
[asy]
size(8cm);
import olympiad;
dot((0,1.25),white);
draw(circle(origin, 1));
dot(dir(240));
dot(dir(300));
dot(dir(270));
dot(dir(180));
dot(dir(0));
dot(dir(60));
draw(dir(180)--dir(0)--dir(270));
draw(dir(0)--dir(270), linewidth(1.5));
draw(dir(240)--dir(60)--dir(300));
draw(dir(60)--dir(240), linewidth(1.5));
pair A = (3,0);
draw(circle(origin+A, 1));
dot(dir(240)+A);
dot(dir(300)+A);
dot(dir(270)+A);
dot(dir(180)+A);
dot(dir(0)+A);
dot(dir(60)+A);
draw(dir(180)+A--dir(60)+A--dir(300)+A);
draw(dir(180)+A--dir(60)+A,linewidth(1.5));
draw(dir(0)+A--dir(240)+A,linewidth(1.5));
draw(dir(240)+A--dir(0)+A--dir(270)+A);
[/asy]
\end{figure}

To prove this, FTSOC suppose that two angles share some nontrivial arc. Then two of the chords are in one of the configurations above and do not intersect, contradiction (see Figure 1\footnote{These two represent the only two possible cases, up to perturbation and symmetry. Either one arc is contained completely within the other or not.}). 

Now we show the if direction of the key claim. Consider any point  $P$  in a configuration satisfying (1), and we will show that we cannot add any more chords with endpoint  $P$ .
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\caption{Case 1 (Left) and Case 2 (Right)}
[asy]
unitsize(1.5cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));
draw(dir(75)--dir(180),linewidth(1.5));
draw(dir(75)--dir(245));
draw(dir(75)--dir(270),linewidth(1.5));
dot(dir(245));
label(" $P$ ", dir(245), dir(245));

pair d = (3,0);
draw(circle(d,1));
draw(dir(75)+d--dir(245)+d);
draw(dir(75)+d--dir(270)+d, linewidth(1.5));
draw(dir(0)+d--dir(180)+d, linewidth(1.5));
draw(dir(0)+d--dir(245)+d);
dot(dir(245)+d);
label(" $A$ ", dir(75)+d,dir(75));
label(" $B$ ", dir(0)+d,dir(0));
label(" $P$ ", dir(245)+d,dir(245));
dot(dir(30)+d);
dot(dir(45)+d);
draw(dir(30)+d--dir(245)+d,dotted);
draw(dir(45)+d--dir(245)+d,dotted);
[/asy] 
\end{figure}

Case 1: The angles that face the two arcs of size  $1$  with  $P$  as endpoints have the same vertex. 

This case is obvious, since no chord from  $P$  intersects both of the bolded chords. (See Figure 2.)

Case 2: The angles that face the two arcs with  $P$  as endpoints have different vertices. 

Call the vertices of the angles  $A$  and  $B$ . Clearly  $P$  faces arc  $AB$  not containing  $P$ . Notice that the bolded chords restrict any chord having  $P$  as an endpoint. Specifically, any chord must have its other endpoint on arc  $AB$ , but we know that every point on this arc is connected with  $P$  since arc  $AB$  needs to be split into arcs of size  $1$  to satisfy (1). 

Now we show that if a configuration does not satisfy (1), then the configuration is not full. ( $*$ ) 

\textbf{Proof of ( $*$ ):}

I claim that each point must be the endpoint of some chord. (2) Proceed by induction on the number of points  $k$ , with the case  $k=3$  being trivial. Assume that the claim holds for  $k=n$ , and we show that it holds for  $n+1$  as well. 

[center]
    [asy]
    size(4cm);
     import olympiad;
     draw(unitcircle);
     dot(dir(0)^^dir(72)^^dir(144)^^dir(216)^^dir(288)^^dir(20));
     draw(dir(0)--dir(144)--dir(288)--dir(72)--dir(216)--cycle);
     draw(dir(0)--dir(216)--dir(72),linewidth(1.5));
     draw(dir(216)--dir(20),dotted);
     label(" $P$ ", dir(20), dir(20));
     label(" $A$ ", dir(72),dir(72));
     label(" $B$ ", dir(0),dir(0));
     label(" $Q$ ", dir(216),dir(216));
    [/asy]
[/center]
\textbf{Proof of (2):} Suppose otherwise, and let  $P$  be one such point that is not the endpoint of any chord. Let  $AB$  be the arc of size  $2$  that contains  $P$  on its interior. By the inductive hypothesis on the set of  $n$  points excluding  $P$ , there exists a point  $Q$  such that  $\angle AQB$  is drawn. Since every chord drawn shares a point with both  $QA$  and  $QB$ , every chord crosses the interior of  $\angle AQB$  and hence intersects  $QP$  as well. Hence we can draw  $QP$  which is a contradiction to the assumption that there exists a point  $P$  that is not an endpoint of a chord in a full configuration.  $\blacksquare$ We now continue with the proof of \textbf{( $*$ )}: For the sake of contradiction, suppose there exists an arc  $AB$  with size  $1$  that has no angle facing it. However, let  $P$  be a point such that either  $PA$  or  $PB$  is an existing chord, but not both (By (2), we know that  $P$  exists). Without loss of generality, assume it is  $PA$ . Notice that any chord that intersects  $PA$  has one endpoint on arc  $AP$  not containing  $B$ , and one endpoint on arc  $BP$  not containing  $A$  (Here we are also using that  $AB$  is a unit arc). However, there can be chords that only share  $A$  or  $P$  with  $PA$ . \newpage We now proceed in multiple cases:

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\caption{Case 1 (Left) and Case 2 (Right). \newline Dotted lines represent possible chords to aid in showing that the proofs that follow are valid.}
[asy]
unitsize(1.5cm);
draw(circle((0,0),1));
draw(dir(75)--dir(180), linewidth(1.5));
draw(dir(245)--dir(75),linewidth(1.5));
dot(dir(245));
dot(dir(75));
dot(dir(180));
dot(dir(220));
dot(dir(200));
dot(dir(90));
dot(dir(140));
dot(dir(0));
dot(dir(300));
draw(dir(180)--dir(0));
label(" $P$ ", dir(245), dir(245));
label(" $R$ ", dir(180),dir(180));
label(" $A$ ", dir(75), dir(75));
label(" $B$ ", dir(0), dir(0));
draw(dir(90)--dir(300),dotted);
draw(dir(140)--dir(300), dotted);
draw(dir(140)--dir(0), dotted);
draw(dir(75)--dir(0), dotted);
pair d = (3,0);
draw(circle(d,1));
draw(dir(245)+d--dir(75)+d,linewidth(1.5));
dot(dir(245)+d);
dot(dir(75)+d);
dot(dir(220)+d);
dot(dir(200)+d);
dot(dir(90)+d);
dot(dir(140)+d);
dot(dir(0)+d);
dot(dir(300)+d);
label(" $P$ ", dir(245)+d, dir(245));
label(" $A$ ", dir(75)+d, dir(75));
label(" $B$ ", dir(0)+d, dir(0));
draw(dir(90)+d--dir(300)+d,dotted);
draw(dir(140)+d--dir(300)+d, dotted);
draw(dir(140)+d--dir(0)+d, dotted);
draw(dir(75)+d--dir(0)+d, dotted);
draw(dir(245)+d--dir(0)+d);
draw(dir(245)+d--dir(300)+d, dotted);
draw(dir(75)+d--dir(300)+d, dotted);
[/asy] 
\end{figure}

Case 1: There is some point  $R$  such that  $RA$  is an existing chord, and  $R$  lies on arc  $AP$  not containing  $B$ .

I claim that we can draw  $RB$ . Clearly  $RB$  shares a point with  $RA$  and  $AP$ . Since  $AB$  is an arc of size  $1$ , any chord that intersects  $AR$  and  $AP$  also intersects  $RB$ . So indeed, we can draw  $RB$ . 

Case 2: There does not exist a point  $R\neq P$  such that  $RA$  is an existing chord and that  $R$  lies on arc  $AP$  not containing  $B$ . 

I claim that we can draw  $PB$ . This relies on the fact that arc  $AB$  has size  $1$ . If any chord is to share a point with chord  $PA$ , then it must have one endpoint on each ``side" of chord  $PA$ , or have one endpoint as  $P$ . 

If it has one endpoint on each side of chord  $PA$ , then since arc  $AB$  has size  $1$ , one endpoint must be on arc  $PB$  (where  $B$  is considered to be on this arc). Hence the chord has one endpoint on each side of chord  $PB$  and intersects  $PB$ . If it has one endpoint as  $P$ , then obviously  $PB$  is allowed to be drawn.

This completes the proof of the ``key claim."

Let the \emph{degree} of a point be the number of chords that have it as an endpoint. I claim that we have the following fact for full configurations:

Suppose that a point  $P$  is the vertex of angle  $\angle APB$ . Suppose point  $C$  is on the arc  $AB$  not containing  $P$ . Then  $PC$  is drawn. (3)

\textbf{Proof:} This is actually a consequence of the ``lemma" and the ``key claim." Consider a point  $D$  directly adjacent to  $C$ , allowing  $D=A$  or  $D=B$ . By the key claim, we have that some angle faces arc  $CD$ . Suppose that this angle has vertex  $Q\neq P$ . Then, the arcs that  $P$  and  $Q$  face intersect, which is a contradiction.  $\blacksquare$ We are now ready to finish the problem. I claim that  $n$  chords can always be drawn on a circle with  $n$  points, but not more. This would imply that the answer is \boxed{\text{All Odd  $n$ }}, since every time after Rosencrans draws a chord, there are an odd number of chords drawn, so Rosencrans would draw the last chord.

Let the points be labeled  $A_1$ ,  $A_2$ ,\dots  $A_n$ . Let  $a_i$  be the number of arcs of size  $1$  that are faced by an angle with vertex  $A_i$ . From (3), we know that these arcs are contiguous, i.e. we cannot have gaps. Now, notice that the number of chords that  $A_i$  is an endpoint of is exactly  $a_i+1$ , which hold in the case of exactly one chord as well (There cannot be zero chords by (2)).

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \caption{If  $A_i$  faces  $a_i$  arcs, then it has  $a_i+1$  chords.}
    [asy]
    unitsize(1.5cm);
    draw(circle((0,0),1));
    draw(dir(75)--dir(150));
    draw(dir(75)--dir(200));
    draw(dir(75)--dir(240));
    draw(dir(75)--dir(260));
    label(" $A_i$ ", dir(75),dir(75));
    [/asy]
\end{figure}
Hence, the total number of chords drawn is 
\[\frac 12\sum_{1\leq i\leq n} (a_i+1)=\frac 12 n+\frac 12\sum_{1\leq i\leq n} a_i=n\]
as desired. (We halve the sum because each chord is counted twice, once by each vertex. There are a total of  $n$  unit arcs, each faced by exactly one unit angle, so  $\sum a_i=n.$ )</details>

Tough. Here's an outline of a proof that the game always terminates in  $n$  steps. (You could probably shorten it by deleting the first claim and replacing it something weaker that's still sufficient, but I gave the full version since it gives important intuition.)**Setup**. We will use graph theory freely with vertices and edges defined in the obvious way. We also call a configuration *saturated* if no more chords can be drawn.**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  	If there is a graph-theoretic cycle, 	then it has an odd number of vertices, 	and is a  $\{k,2k+1\}$ -star.
*Proof.*  	[Outline of proof] 	Imagine actually drawing the cycle with a pencil. 	After drawing the first edge  $e_1$  and  $e_2$  of the star, 	there is always a unique arc that one can lead the next edge to. 	 	[asy] 		size(8cm);
pair P0 = dir(180); 		pair P1 = dir(0); 		pair P2 = dir(140); 		pair P3 = dir(-30); 		pair P4 = dir(70); 		pair P5 = dir(230); 		pair P6 = dir(20);
picture pic1; 		draw(pic1, unitcircle); 		dot(pic1, P0); 		dot(pic1, P1);
picture pic2; 		add(pic2, pic1); 		draw(pic2, P0--P1, lightblue, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		dot(pic2, P2);
picture pic3; 		add(pic3, pic2); 		draw(pic3, P1--P2, lightblue, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		dot(pic3, P3);
picture pic4; 		add(pic4, pic3); 		draw(pic4, P2--P3, lightblue, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		dot(pic4, P4);
picture pic5; 		add(pic5, pic4); 		draw(pic5, P3--P4, lightblue, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		dot(pic5, P5);
picture pic6; 		add(pic6, pic5); 		draw(pic6, P4--P5, lightblue, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		dot(pic6, P6);
picture pic7; 		add(pic7, pic6); 		draw(pic7, P5--P6, lightblue, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins);
add(shift(0,3)*pic1); 		add(shift(3,3)*pic2); 		add(shift(6,3)*pic3); 		add(shift(9,3)*pic4); 		add(shift(1.5,0)*pic5); 		add(shift(4.5,0)*pic6); 		add(shift(7.5,0)*pic7);
draw(shift(0,3)*(P0--P1), red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		draw(shift(3,3)*(P1--P2), red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		draw(shift(6,3)*(P2--P3), red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		draw(shift(9,3)*(P3--P4), red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		draw(shift(1.5,0)*(P4--P5), red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		draw(shift(4.5,0)*(P5--P6), red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 		draw(shift(7.5,0)*(P6--P0), red, EndArrow(TeXHead), Margins); 	[/asy] 	 	This means there is essentially a unique possible shape. 	Moreover a parity argument on which side of  $e_1$  we are on 	shows that we need on odd number of edges.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  	[pad's lemma, from post #76] 	In any maximal configuration, if  $PA$  and  $PB$  are two edges, 	then any vertices in the arc  $AB$  not containing  $P$  	must be joined to  $P$ , and only to  $P$ .
*Proof.*  	If  $C$  is a point in the arc, the edge  $PC$  can be drawn, 	because any edge intersecting  $PA$  and  $PB$  will intersect  $C$  as well. 	Conversely, if  $C$  is joined to a point  $Q$ , 	then  $CQ$  must meet both  $PA$  and  $PB$ , so  $Q = P$ .  $\blacksquare$ Consequently, if there is ever a graph-theoretic cycle, the first claim tells what it looks like, and pad's lemma gives an explicit description of the rest of the edges. We see there are exactly  $n$  edges.
-------------------**Proof that at most  $n$  chords can be drawn.** Because any graph with  $n$  vertices and  $n$  edges necessarily has a cycle, the previous paragraph applies, and this proves at most  $n$  chords can be drawn.
-------------------**Proof that at least  $n$  chords can be drawn.** Assume again there's no cycle. For any vertex  $P$  of degree at least  $2$ , we color in blue its leftmost and rightmost edge  $PA$  and  $PB$ . Then delete any remaining edges of  $P$  and the associated leaf  $PC$ ; as we explained earlier, an edge that meets  $PA$  and  $PB$  must meet  $PC$  anyways, so this does not add any additional possible moves. Repeating this until it is no longer possible, we find that the resulting picture consists of disjoint (now-blue) paths.
We now show that any vertex  $A$  of degree  $1$  can be used. Call its incident edge  $AB$ . Every edge  $e$  other than  $AB$  marks out an arc  $\Theta_e$  of vertices which  $A$  can *not* join to; this arc doesn't contain  $A$ .
[asy] 	draw(unitcircle); 	real r = 1.1; 	size(4cm); 	pair A = dir(270); 	pair B = dir(90); 	dot(" $A$ ", A, A, red); 	dot(B, red); 	label(" $B$ ", r*B, B, red); 	pair X = dir(40); 	pair Y = dir(170); 	draw(X--Y, lightblue); 	dot(X, lightblue); 	dot(Y, lightblue); 	draw(arc(origin, r*X, r*Y), deepgreen+1); 	label(" $e$ ", 0.7*Y+0.3*X, dir(-45), lightblue); 	draw(A--B, red); 	label(" $\Theta_e$ ", r*dir(120), dir(120), deepgreen); [/asy]**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  	The  $\Theta_e$  have a common intersection other than  $B$ .
*Proof.*  	Any two edges intersect, 	so any *pair* of arcs  $\Theta_e$  intersect too (possibly only at endpoints). 	This implies that all the  $\Theta_e$ 's have a common intersection. 	(You can call this ``Helly's theorem after geometric inversion at  $v$ '' 	if you want to scare your friends, but really, 	take the leftmost right endpoint and the rightmost left endpoint.)
Moreover,  $B$  was only the endpoint of one these arcs (as  $\deg B = 2$ ), 	so while the intersection could be degenerate, 	it can't be degenerate with just  $\{B\}$ .  $\blacksquare$

