Nice and fun puzzle! This one took me quite a bit cause kept fakesolving, but enjoyed it as always!

Let a  $\textit{move}$  be defined to be the process of one monster giving all their candies to the monsters behind them, and denote the  $\textit{final move}$  to be the first move that gives one beast all the candy (if this exists at all). Clearly, another beast besides Grogg temporarily holds all the candy if and only if a final move exists.

We claim that the final move exists if and only if  $n = 1$  or  $n = 2.$ First, we will show that the final move exists when  $n = 1$  and when  $n = 2.$  Consider what happens when  $n=1.$  

[asy]
size(6cm);
filldraw(circle((0, 0), 1), lightgrey, linewidth(3));
draw(circle((3, 0), 1), linewidth(3));
draw(circle((6, 0), 1), linewidth(3));
draw(circle((9, 0), 1), linewidth(3));
label(" $1$ ", (0, 0));
label(" $0$ ", (3, 0));
label(" $0$ ", (6, 0));
label(" $0$ ", (9, 0));
filldraw(circle((0, -3), 1), grey, linewidth(3));
filldraw(circle((3, -3), 1), lightgrey, linewidth(3));
draw(circle((6, -3), 1), linewidth(3));
draw(circle((9, -3), 1), linewidth(3));
label(" $0$ ", (0, -3));
label(" $1$ ", (3, -3));
label(" $0$ ", (6, -3));
label(" $0$ ", (9, -3));
label(" $\dots$ ", (12, 0));
label(" $\dots$ ", (12, -3));
[/asy]

When  $n=1,$  Grogg immediately gives to the next monster in line, meaning that the final move exists when  $n=1.$  
[asy]
