Similarly, the final move exists when  $n=2,$  as Grogg will give one candy to both the second and third monsters, and then the second monster will give it's candy to the third monster (meaning the third monster will have all two candies and that the final move exists when  $n=2$ ).

Now, we prove that the final move exists only when  $n = 1$  and when  $n = 2.$  In order to do this, we use the following lemma. $\textbf{Lemma:}$  If Monster  $A$  is ahead of Monster  $B$  and Monster  $A$  hasn't left the line, then Monster  $B$  does not have more candies than Monster  $A.$  $\textbf{Proof:}$  First, note that at the very beginning of this process, our Lemma is true (as Grogg has the most candies and every other monster only has  $0$ ).

Now, we claim that if a move gives Monster  $B$  a candy, then the move must give Monster  $A$  a candy as well. 

Notice that if Monster  $A$  is still in the line and if Monster  $B$  gets a candy, it must be getting a candy from a monster before Monster  $A$  (as if it was getting a candy from Monster  $A$  or any monster after, than monster  $A$  would no longer be in the line). Call this monster giving Monster  $B$  a candy "Monster  $C$ ."

