label(" $C$ ", (0, -2));
label(" $A$ ", (6, -2));
label(" $B$ ", (12, -2));
label(" $\dots$ ", (18, 0));
[/asy]
Notice that from the method of sharing candies, all Monsters after  $C$  and before  $B$  will get a candy if Monster  $B$  gets a candy from Monster  $C$  (more monsters might also get a candy, but that is not important to us). Thus, since we showed Monster  $A$  is behind Monster  $C$  (and we assumed it's before Monster  $B$ ), our claim is true.

Now, since our Lemma is true before any move was made and our claim makes it so that no move can change our Lemma (since no move can give Monster  $B$  more candies than it gives Monster  $A$ ), our Lemma is true.  $\square$ Now assume that the final move exists for some starting value of  $m$  candies. We will show we must have  $m \le 2.$  
Suppose that Monster  $Y$  is the monster that will hold all of the candies after the final move, and that Monster  $X$  is the monster that will undertake the final move. Consider the distribution of candies before the final move is made.

[asy]
size(9cm);
draw(circle((0, 0), 1), linewidth(3));
draw(circle((3, 0), 1), linewidth(3));
filldraw(circle((6, 0), 1), lightgrey, linewidth(3));
filldraw(circle((9, 0), 1), lightgrey, linewidth(3));
draw(circle((12, 0), 1), linewidth(3));
draw(circle((15, 0), 1), linewidth(3));
label(" $\dots$ ", (-3, 0));
label(" $X$ ", (6, -2));
label(" $1$ ", (6, 0));
label(" $Y$ ", (9, -2));
label(" $m-1$ ", (9, 0), fontsize(6pt));
label(" $\dots$ ", (18, 0));
[/asy]

Since all monsters can only gain  $1$  candy per move, Monster  $Y$  must have  $m-1$  candies right before the final move is made. Thus, since Monster  $X$  will give Monster  $Y$  a candy on the final move (as otherwise Monster  $X$  would not be the monster that undertakes the final move), Monster  $X$  must have exactly  $1$  candy (as Grogg starts out with  $m$  candies and the number of candies is invariant). 

Now, since monster  $X$  is clearly ahead of Monster  $Y,$  our lemma states that we must have  $1 \ge m-1 \Longleftrightarrow m \le 2.$  Thus, since the final move exists when  $m \in \{1, 2\}$  and it only exists when  $m \in \{1, 2\},$  we are done.  $\blacksquare$ <details><summary>Remarks</summary>This isn't a very hard problem, but I liked it. 
Also, it is trivial to show that Monster  $X$  must be right before Monster  $Y,$  but that is unnecessary for our proof.</details>

