Let's proceed with proving that quadrilateral  $POYH$  is a parallelogram. Let's start by proving that  $\overline{PO} \parallel \overline{HY}.$  We know that the circumcenter is formed by the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of a triangle. In are diagram, we have constructed dotted lines  $\overline{PO}, \overline{DO},$  and  $\overline{ON},$  which are the three perpendicular bisectors of  $\triangle ABC.$  Thus by definition, since  $\overline{PO}$  is a perpendicular bisector, it is perpendicular to  $\overline{AB},$  and forms a right triangle at  $\angle APO.$  Now we can move on to the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC,$  or  $H.$  We know that the orthocenter is formed by the altitudes of a triangle. So, with similar logic, we can construct the altitudes of  $\triangle ABC,$  as shown in the diagram above. Thus, by definition, we know that the altitude that starts from  $C$  and extends to point  $L$  on segment  $\overline{AB}$  forms a right angle and  $\angle ALC.$  Since points  $P$  and  $L$  both lie on  $\overline{AB},$  and that  $\angle APO$  and  $\angle ALC$  are both right angles, we know that  $\overline{PO}$  and  $\overline{HY}$  are parallel.

Now that we know that  $\overline{PO} \parallel \overline{HY},$  we can move on to prove  $\overline{PH} \parallel \overline{OY}$  to complete the proof that quadrilateral  $POYH$  is a parallelogram. Note that if we prove that  $\angle RAO$  and  $\angle XRH,$  then that implies that  $\overline{PH} \parallel \overline{OY}.$  By the Radius Tangent Theorem, we know that the angle formed by a tangent and the radius will be  $90^{\circ}.$  Thus, we can conclude that  $\angle RAO = 90^{\circ}.$  Now, we can move on to proving  $\angle XRH$  is also  $90^{\circ}.$  Note that proving  $P$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle AXH$  will automatically show that  $\angle XRH$  is a right angle knowing that  $\overline{RH}$  is a altitude of the triangle. 

So, we can now proceed. Firstly, we know that  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC,$  which implies that  $\overline{CH}$  is perpendicular to  $\overline{AB}.$  Thus,  $\overline{AL}$  is an altitude of  $\triangle AXH.$  Now, let's move on to proving that  $\overline{XW}$  is an altitude of  $\triangle AXH.$  To begin, let's start by focusing on  $\triangle ABT.$  Since we already know that  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC,$  we also know that  $\angle ALH$  is a right angle, meaning  $\overline{AL}$  is a altitude of  $\triangle AXH.$  Next, notice that  $\overline{OP}$  is a perpendicular bisector of  $\triangle ABC$  since  $O$  is the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC.$  So thus,  $P$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AB}.$  Moreover, we are given in the problem statement that  $X$  is the midpoint of  $AT,$  and so it is clear that  $\overline{XW}$  is parallel to  $\overline{TJ}.$  Note that  $\overline{AJ}$  is an altitude of  $\triangle ABC,$  meaning  $\angle AJT = 90^{\circ}.$  This also implies that  $\angle AWX$  is a right angle, because of our previous conclusion that  $\overline{XW} \parallel \overline{TJ}.$  So, we have now proved that  $\overline{AL}$  and  $\overline{XW}$  are altitudes of  $\triangle AXH.$  Two altitudes of a triangle predetermines its orthocenter, clearly implying  $\overline{RH}$  is an altitude and  $\angle XRH=90^{\circ}.$  Since both  $\angle RAO$  and  $\angle XRH$  are right,  $\overline{RH}$  and  $\overline{AO}$  are parallel.

Since  $\overline{RH} \parallel \overline{AO},$  we also know that  $\overline{PH} \parallel \overline{OY}.$  Now that we have concluded  $\overline{PO} \parallel \overline{HY}$  and  $\overline{PH} \parallel \overline{OY},$  we know that  $POYH$  is a parallelogram. This also implies that  $\overline{PO}=\overline{HY}.$  We can now prove that, for any triangle, a vertex to a triangles orthocenter is twice the length of the perpendicular bisector parallel to that vertex to the orthocenter. Let's start by focusing on  $\triangle ABC,$  with some things added on, as shown below.

