Let  $\omega$  be the circumcircle of triangle  $ABC$ .  First, we add several extra points and lines:


- Let  $E$  be the point on  $\omega$  such that  $CE \perp AB$ .  (Consequently  $C$ ,  $E$ , and  $X$  are collinear.)
- Let  $F$  be the point on  $\omega$  such that  $AF \perp BC$ .  (Consequently  $A$ ,  $H$ , and  $F$  are collinear.)
- Let  $P$  be the point on  $\omega$  such that  $CP \parallel AT$ .
- Let  $Q$  be the point on  $\omega$  such that  $A$ ,  $Y$ , and  $Q$  are collinear.  (Consequently  $A$ ,  $O$ , and  $Q$  are collinear.)
- Let  $R$  be the point on  $\omega$  such that  $AR \parallel CH$ .
[/list]
Let  $P_{\infty}$  be the point at infinity on line  $AT$  and  $R_{\infty}$  be the point at infinity on line  $CX$ .  Notice the following:
[list]
- $X$  is the midpoint of  $AT$  if and only if  $(T, A; X, P_{\infty})$  is a harmonic bundle.
- $Y$  is the midpoint of  $CH$  if and only if  $(C, H; Y, R_{\infty}$ ) is a harmonic bundle.
[/list]
Now consider the following:  $$ (T, A; X, P_{\infty}) \stackrel{\text{C}}{=} (B, A; E, P) $$    $$ (C, H; Y, R_{\infty}) \stackrel{\text{A}}{=} (C, F; Q, R) $$  Thus the midpoint conditions are equivalent to the fact that the cross ratios above equal  $-1$ .  Therefore it suffices to show  $(B, A; E, P) = (C, F; Q, R)$ .  To do this, we show the arrangement of points  $A, E, B, F$  (marked red) is a copy of the arrangement of points  $R, C, Q, P$  (marked green) by showing  $FP \parallel BQ \parallel EC \parallel AR$ .  This is sufficient because it shows the red points are the reflection of the green points across the perpendicular bisector of segment  $FP$ .  We do this step-by-step:
[list]
- $AR \parallel EC$ .  This is true by definition.
- $EC \parallel BQ$ .  First note that  $\angle ECB = 90 - \angle B$ , so we just need to show  $\angle CBQ = 90 - \angle B$  as well.

    Observe that  $\angle CBQ = \angle CAQ$  since  $ABQC$  is cyclic.  We know  $AQ$  is a diameter of the circle, so  $\angle ACQ = 90$ .  Thus  $\angle CAQ = 90 - \angle CQA$ .  Again, since  $ABQC$  is cyclic,  $\angle CQA = \angle B$ , so  $\angle CBQ = 90 - \angle B$ , as desired.
- $BQ \parallel FP$ .  We need  $\angle BQF = \angle QFP$ .   Notice that since  $ABFQ$  is cyclic,  $\angle BQF = \angle BAF = 90 - \angle B$ , and since  $AFPQ$  is cyclic,  $\angle QFP = \angle QAP$ .  Thus we only need  $\angle QAP = 90 - \angle B$ .

    We know  $\angle QAC = 90 - B$ .  We also know  $\angle BAP = \angle BCP$  because  $BACP$  is cyclic, and since  $CP \parallel AT$ ,  $\angle BCP = \angle ATC$ .  Since  $AT$  is a tangent to  $\omega$ ,  $\angle TAB = \angle C$ , and  $\angle TBA = 180 - \angle B$ , so  $\angle ATC = \angle B - \angle C$ .  Therefore 
    \begin{align*}
    &\angle QAP 
    = ~&\angle BAC - \angle BAP - \angle CAQ 
    = ~&\angle A - (\angle B - \angle C) - (90 - \angle B) 
    = ~&\angle A + \angle C - 90 
    = ~&90 - \angle B 
    \end{align*}

With all of this information, we can conclude that  $(B, A; E, P) = (C, F; Q, R)$ , which completes the proof.</details>

