Assume all angles are directed.Let  $C'$  be the point diametrically opposite  $C$  on  $(ABC),$  let  $H'=\overline{AH}\cap (ABC),$  and let  $D,E,F$  be the feet of the altitudes from  $A,B,C.$   $\textbf{Claim: }$   $\triangle AC'H\sim\triangle CAT$   $\emph{Proof: }$  First, write
\begin{align*}
    \angle C'AH
    &= \angle C'AB+\angle BAD
    &= \angle C'CB+(90^\circ-\angle CBA)
    &= (90^\circ-\angle BC'C)+(90^\circ-\angle CBA)
    &= (90^\circ-\angle BAC)+(90^\circ-\angle CBA)
    &= \angle ACB = \angle ACT.
\end{align*} \noindent\rule{17cm}{0.4pt} Next, by the Law of Sines on  $\triangle TXC$  and  $\triangle AXC,$  we have   $$ CT=\frac{TX\sin\angle TXC}{\sin\angle XCT}, $$    $$ CA=\frac{AX\sin\angle CXA}{\sin\angle ACX}=\frac{TX\sin\angle TXC}{\sin\angle ACX}. $$   Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
    \frac{CT}{CA}
    = \frac{\sin\angle ACX}{\sin\angle XCT}
    = \frac{\sin\angle ACF}{\sin\angle FCB}
    = \frac{\sin\angle (90^\circ-\angle BAC)}{\sin\angle (90^\circ-\angle CBA}
    = \frac{\cos\angle BAC}{\cos\angle CBA}.
\end{align*}\noindent\rule{17cm}{0.4pt} Finally, it is well-known that  $AH=2R\cos\angle BAC,$  where  $R$  is the circumradius of  $\triangle ABC,$  so   $$ \frac{AH}{AC'}=\frac{2R\cos\angle BAC}{C'C\cos\angle CC'A}=\frac{2R\cos\angle BAC}{2R\cos\angle CBA}=\frac{\cos\angle BAC}{\cos\angle CBA}. $$  \noindent\rule{17cm}{0.4pt} Now by SAS similarity,  $\triangle AC'H\sim\triangle CAT,$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$  Let  $P=\overline{C'H}\cap (ABC).$  Since  $\overline{CC'}$  is a diamter of  $(ABC),$  we know  $\overline{C'P}\perp\overline{CP}.$  Furthermore, since  $\overline{AC'}\perp\overline{CA},\overline{AH}\perp\overline{CT},$  and  $\triangle AC'H\sim\triangle CAT,$  we have  $\overline{C'H}\perp\overline{AT}.$  Thus,  $\overline{C'P}\parallel\overline{AO},$  so  $\overline{CP}\perp\overline{AO}.$  This is enough to imply that  $\overline{AO}$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{CP}.$  Since the midpoint of  $\overline{CH}$  also lies on this perpendicular bisector, we are done.

