Since  $\angle AFC = \angle ADC = 90^\circ$ , points  $A$ ,  $F$ ,  $D$ ,  $C$  are concyclic. It follows by power of a point that   $$ (HA)(HD) = (HC)(HF) = (2HY)\left(\frac{HE}{2}\right) = (HY)(HE). $$  Hence, quadrilateral  $AYDE$  is cyclic. 

We claim that  $A$ ,  $X$ ,  $E$ ,  $M$  are concyclic. Indeed, since  $XM$  is parallel to  $TB$ , we find  $$ \measuredangle MXE = \measuredangle MXC = \measuredangle BCX = \measuredangle DCF = \measuredangle DAF = \measuredangle MAE, $$  since  $AFDC$  is cyclic and  $H$  and  $E$  are reflections over  $AB$ . Let line  $CA$  meet  $(AXEM)$  again at point  $K$ . Note that  $$ \measuredangle XKA = \measuredangle XMA = \measuredangle TBA = \measuredangle CBA. $$  Next, since  $XA$  is tangent to  $(ABC)$ ,   $$ \measuredangle KAX = \measuredangle CAX = \measuredangle CAB + \measuredangle BAX = \measuredangle CAB + \measuredangle BCA = \measuredangle CBA. $$  It follows that  $\measuredangle XKA = \measuredangle KAX$ , and so triangle  $XKA$  is isosceles with  $XK = XA$ . Since  $\angle ADT = 90^\circ$ , circle  $(ATD)$  has center  $X$  with diameter  $AT$ . Hence,  $K$  lies on  $(ATD)$ , and so,  $C$  lies on the radical axis of circle  $(AXEM)$  and  $(ATD)$ . 

Consequently, by power of a point on  $(AXEM)$  and  $(ATD)$ ,  $$ (CE)(CX) = (CA)(CK) = (CD)(CT). $$  Thus, quadrilateral  $XTDE$  is cyclic. 

We conclude that since  $AYDE$  and  $XTED$  are cyclic,   $$ \measuredangle YAD = \measuredangle YED = \measuredangle CED = \measuredangle XTC = \measuredangle ATB = \measuredangle TAB+\measuredangle ABT = -\measuredangle BCA + \measuredangle ABC. $$  Next, by Lemma 1.30, we have that  $\measuredangle OAB = 90^\circ - \measuredangle BCA$ . It follows that  $$ \measuredangle OAD = \measuredangle OAB - \measuredangle DAB = (90^\circ - \measuredangle BCA) - (90^\circ - \measuredangle ABD) = -\measuredangle BCA + \measuredangle ABC. $$  It follows that  $\measuredangle YAD = \measuredangle OAD$ . Consequently, points  $A$ ,  $Y$  and  $O$  are colinear. Hence,  $AO$  bisects  $CH$ , and we are done.  $\square$

