The idea is to use harmonics. Let  $D = AH \cap BC$ ,  $E = BH \cap AC$ ,  $F = CH \cap AB$ ,  $Z = CH \cap (CAB)$  where  $Z \neq C$ ,  $G = (CH) \cap (CAB)$  where  $G \neq C$ , and  $P_{\infty}$  is the point at infinity along line  $TA$ . I'll start with a well-known lemma:**<span style="color:#B45F06">Lemma.</span>**  $(A, B; Z, G) = -1$ *Proof.* We know that if  $DE \cap AB = U$ , then  $(A, B; F, U) = -1$  by the Cevians Induce Harmonic Bundles lemma. Also,  $C$ ,  $G$ ,  $U$  are collinear because by radical axes on  $(CAB)$ ,  $(CH)$ ,  $(ABDE)$ , we get  $AB$ ,  $DE$ ,  $CG$  concurring. Thus by projecting the harmonic bundle onto  $(ABC)$  we get   $$ -1 = (A, B; F, U) \stackrel{C}{=} (A, B; Z, G) $$  which is desired.  $\square$ We also have   $$ -1 = (A, T; X, P_{\infty}) \stackrel{C}{=} (A, B; Z, CP_{\infty} \cap (ABC)) $$  By the lemma,  $(A, B; Z, G) = -1$ . Thus, since harmonic conjugates are unique, we have  $CP_{\infty} \cap (ABC) = G$ . In particular,  $CG$  is parallel to the  $A$ -tangent, so  $A$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $CG$ .

But remark that  $CG$  is the common chord of  $(CAB)$  and  $(CH)$ , so their centers lie on the perpendicular bisector of  $CG$  as well. The center of  $(CAB)$  is  $O$ , and the center of  $(CH)$  is the midpoint of  $CH$ , so  $A$ ,  $O$ , and the midpoint of  $CH$  are collinear, as needed.  $\blacksquare$

