I think this problem is great. It might've been somewhat standard for experienced competitors (in the scope of olympiads as a whole, not just USAMTS), but given that I wouldn't classify myself as anywhere near that I think this is a very nice learning experience. The key takeaway from this problem is the following deep fact:  $\text{minimum} \leq \text{average} \leq \text{maximum}$ . So in a problem related to minimums and maximums, where explicitly computing these values is difficult but finding the sum of everything is easier, we can create a fitting bound. More generally this strategy is known as "local-to-global", as noted by @2above. Here's my solution:

If  $k=41$ , no  $n$  exist.
If  $k=39$ , then all  $n\geq 381$  work.
We can visualize the group as a graph: each node represents a person, and an edge between two nodes means that those two people are friends. When we "remove" a node/person, we remove all of the edges connected to that node/person. Also note that we can restate the phrase "the number of pairs of people that know each other is at most  $\frac{n-k}{n}$  times that of the original group" as "the number of edges removed is at least  $\frac{k}{n}$  times the total number of edges." Finding all  $n$  which satisfy the problem's condition is equivalent to finding all  $n$  such that there exists a graph with  $n$  nodes such that, when we remove any set of  $20$  nodes, the amount of edges removed is at least  $\frac{k}{n}$  times the number in the original.

Consider the sum of the number of edges removed over all  $\binom{n}{20}$  possible choices of  $20$  nodes to remove. To compute this, consider an arbitrary edge. Whenever one of the nodes that is an endpoint of this edge is included in the choice of  $20$  nodes, this edge is removed once. Each endpoint of the edge is included in  $\binom{n-1}{19}$  choices of  $20$  nodes. However, this overcounts instances where both endpoints appear in the  $20$  nodes, which happens  $\binom{n-2}{18}$  times. Thus each edge is removed  $2\binom{n-1}{19}-\binom{n-2}{18}$  times. If the number of edges is  $E$ , it follows that the desired sum is:  $$ \left(2\binom{n-1}{19}-\binom{n-2}{18}\right)E $$  Since there are  $\binom{n}{20}$  possible choices of  $20$  nodes to remove, it follows that the minimum number of edges removed when removing  $20$  nodes is at most  $$ \frac{\left(2\binom{n-1}{19}-\binom{n-2}{18}\right)E}{\binom{n}{20}} $$  Noting that most of the terms in  $\frac{\binom{n-1}{19}}{\binom{n}{20}}$  and  $\frac{\binom{n-2}{18}}{\binom{n}{20}}$  cancel out, this expression simplifies to  $$ \left(\frac{40}{n}-\frac{380}{n(n-1)}\right)E $$  First, we will look at part a, where  $k=41$ . The removal of any  $20$  nodes must remove at least  $\frac{k}{n}=\frac{41}{n}$  times the number of edges in the graph. So, we require:
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{40}{n}-\frac{380}{n(n-1)}\right)E &\geq \frac{41}{n}E
\frac{40}{n}-\frac{380}{n(n-1)} &\geq \frac{41}{n}
40(n-1)-380 &\geq 41(n-1)
-380 &\geq n-1
n &\leq -379
\end{align*}
But we also require  $n>20$ . Therefore, for  $k=41$ , no  $n$  work.

Now, we will look at part b, where  $k=39$ . Since the removal of any  $20$  nodes must remove at least  $\frac{k}{n}=\frac{39}{n}$  times the number of edges in the graph, we require:
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{40}{n}-\frac{380}{n(n-1)}\right)E &\geq \frac{39}{n}E
\frac{40}{n}-\frac{380}{n(n-1)} &\geq \frac{39}{n}
40(n-1)-380 &\geq 39(n-1)
n-1 &\geq 380
n &\geq 381
\end{align*}
Note that we are able to arrive at this because we can multiply by  $n(n-1)$  without reversing the inequality's direction, since  $n>20$ . This inequality shows that we require  $n \geq 381$ , so any  $n \leq 380$  doesn't work. Now we must show that  $n \geq 381$  works.
We claim that a fully connected (or complete) graph with  $n$  nodes works. There are  $\binom{n}{2}$  total edges on this graph. Consider what happens when any set  $S$  of  $20$  nodes is removed. Each node has degree  $n-1$ , so a total of  $20(n-1)$  edges are removed. However, this overcounts all of the edges whose endpoints are both in  $S$ , of which there are  $\binom{20}{2}=190$ . Thus a total of  $20(n-1)-190$  edges are removed. We know that  $n \geq 381$ . Now, we have:
\begin{align*}
n &\geq 381
n-1 &\geq 380
40(n-1)-380 &\geq 39(n-1)
20(n-1)-190 &\geq \frac{39n(n-1)}{2n}
20(n-1)-190 &\geq \frac{39}{n}\binom{n}{2}
\end{align*}
Hence, the number of edges removed when all the nodes in  $S$  are removed is at least  $\frac{k}{n}$  times the total number of edges. As such, this construction works. We are done.  $\blacksquare$

