Interpret the problem as a graph on  $n$  vertices, where people are vertices. Pairs of people who know each other can be represented as edges between two vertices in this graph. The problem is now the following: A graph on  $n$  vertices with an arbitrary amount of edges (at least  $1$ ) has the property such that when any  $20$  vertices are removed, the final graph as at most  $\frac{n-k}{n}$  edges. Find all  $n$  for which such a graph exists. **Part (a):**

I claim that no  $n$  work. Suppose that the degree of vertex  $i$  is  $p_i,$  where  $p_i \le p_{i+1}.$  Consider removing vertices  $1$  through  $20.$  This removes at most  $p_1 + p_2 + \cdots p_{20}$  edges, since the removal of a vertex removes at most  $1$  edge for it's degree. The problem statement requests that this removes at least  $\frac{41}{n}$  edges. However, there are a total of   $$ \frac{p_1 +p_2 + \cdots + p_n}{2} $$   edges. Moreover,   $$ p_1 + \cdots p_{20} \le \frac{20}{n} (p_1 + \cdots + p_n) = \frac{40}{n} \frac{p_1 + \cdots + p_n}{2}, $$   so removing vertices  $1$  through  $20$  removes at most  $\frac{40}{n}$  of the edges, meaning that there are at least  $\frac{n-40}{40}$  edges remaining, contradiction.**Part (b):**

I claim that the answer is all  $n \ge 381.$  First, we give a construction. The complete graph on  $n$  vertices works just fine. Notice that upon each removal of  $20$  vertices, exactly   $$ (n-1) + \cdots + (n-20) = \frac{(2n-21) \cdot 20}{2} = 10(2n-21) $$   edges are removed. This must remove at least  $\frac{39}{n} \cdot {n \choose 2} = \frac{39(n-1)}{2}$  edges, so  $10(2n-21) \ge \frac{39(n-1)}{2} \implies 20(2n-21) \ge 39n - 39,$   which quickly gives  $n \ge 381$  as desired.

Consider the situation when any  $20$  vertices are removed, say  $1$  through  $20.$  If  $p_i$  is the degree of each vertex, then the total number of edges removed is  $p_1 + p_2 + \cdots + p_{20} -x,$  where  $x$  is the total number of edges between vertices in the set  $\{1, 2 ,\cdots, 20 \}.$  This is because, when deleting a vertex, we delete  $p_i$  edges. However, deleting vertices  $i$  and  $j$  where  $i$  and  $j$  are connected by an edge overcounts the number of times an edge between  $i$  and  $j$  is removed. Thus, we subtract  $x,$  the number of edges between vertices in the set of  $20$  vertices. The problem demands that, for all sets of  $20$  vertices removed, say  $v_1, \cdots v_{20},$    $$ p_{v_1} + p_{v_2} + \cdots + p_{v_{20}} - x \ge \frac{39}{n} E = \frac{39}{n} \left( \frac{p_1 + \cdots + p_n}{2} \right). $$  Consider summing this inequality over \textit{all} sets of  $20$  vertices. Then, let  $T$  be the set of sets of  $20$  distinct vertices. Let  $f(S)$  be the sum of the degrees of the vertices of some  $S \in T,$  and  $g(S)$  be the number of edges between vertices in  $S.$  Then, 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{S \in T} f(S) - g(S) \ge {n \choose 20} \frac{39E}{n} 
\end{align*}
since we sum over all  ${n \choose 20}$  sets  $S.$  

\textbf{Claim:} \[\sum_{S \in T} f(S) = {n - 1 \choose 19} \cdot 2E \quad \text{and} \quad \sum_{S \in T} g(S) = {n - 2 \choose 18} \cdot E.\]
\textit{Proof:}

We will count how many times each  $p_i$  appears in  $f(S).$  Notice that vertex  $i$  is in exactly  ${n - 1 \choose 19}$  sets of  $20$ , so each  $p_i$  is counted precisely  ${n - 1 \choose 19}$  times. Thus,   $$ \sum_{S \in T} f(S) = {n - 1 \choose 19}\left( \sum p_i \right)= {n - 1 \choose 19} \cdot 2E $$   as desired. Next we'll count  $\sum_{S \in T} g(S).$  Notice that an edge between vertices  $i$  and  $j$  is removed if and only if both vertices are included in a set of  $20.$  That is, if we look at vertices  $i$  and  $j,$  the edge (or nonexistent edge) between them is removed exactly  ${n - 2 \choose 18}$  times, since both  $i$  and  $j$  are included in a set of  $20$  exactly  ${n - 2 \choose 18}$  times. Thus,   $$ \sum_{S \in T} g(S) = {n - 2 \choose 18} \cdot E $$   as desired.

It follows that 
\begin{align*}
&\sum_{S \in T} f(S) - g(S) = {n - 1 \choose 19} \cdot 2E - {n - 2 \choose 18} \cdot E \ge \frac{39E}{n} {n \choose 20} 
&\implies 2{n - 1 \choose 19} - {n - 2 \choose 18} \ge \frac{39}{n} {n \choose 20}  
& \implies 2 \frac{(n-1)!}{19! \cdot (n-20)!} - \frac{(n-2)!}{18! \cdot (n-20)!} \ge \frac{39}{n} \frac{n!}{20! \cdot (n-20)!} 
\end{align*}

Next, multiply every term by  $\frac{(n-20)! \cdot 20!}{(n-2)!}.$  This gives   $$ 40(n-1) - 380 \ge 39(n-1) \implies n-1 \ge 380 \implies n \ge 381, $$   and we are done.  $\blacksquare$

