<details><summary>Full Solution</summary>(a) We claim that  $x_n=3x_{n-1}+x_{n-2}.$  To do this, we prove that  $x_n=(\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2})^n+(\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}{2})^n$  by induction, then we claim that this satisfies  $x_n=3x_{n-1}+x_{n-2}$ .

For convenience from now on, let  $\frac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2}=a$  and  $\frac{3-\sqrt{5}}{2}=b$ . Note that  $ab=1$ ; we will use this often. Note that  $a^0+b^0=2$  and  $a^1+b^1=3$  (these are our base cases). Now, assume that  $x_i=a^i+b^i$  for all  $0\leq x<n$ ; we want to prove  $x_n=a^n+b^n$ . Notice that
\begin{align*}
				x_n&=\frac{x_{n-1}^2+5}{x_{n-2}}=\frac{(a^{n-1}+b^{n-1})^2+5}{a^{n-2}+b^{n-2}} 
				&=\frac{a^{2n-2}+b^{2n-2}+7}{a^{n-2}+b^{n-2}} 
				&=\frac{(a^{n-2}+b^{n-2})(a^n+b^n)}{a^{n-2}+b^{n-2}} 
				&=a^n+b^n.
\end{align*}
To verify that  $(a^{n-2}+b^{n-2})(a^n+b^n)=a^{2n-2}+b^{2n-2}+7,$  note that the left hand side expands out to
\begin{align*}
				a^{2n-2}+b^{2n-2}+a^{n-2}b^n+a^nb^{n-2}&=a^{2n-2}+b^{2n-2}+b^2+a^2 
				&=a^{2n-2}+b^{2n-2}+7,
\end{align*}
as desired.

Now we claim that we may also write  $x_n=3x_{n-1}-x_{n-2}$ . Remember that by definition  $a+b=3$ . Note that
\begin{align*}
	3x_{n-1}-x_{n-2}&= 3a^{n-1}+3b^{n-1}-a^{n-2}-b^{n-2} 
				&= (a+b)(a^{n-1}+b^{n-1})-a^{n-2}-b^{n-2} 
				&= a^n+b^n+ab(a^{n-2}+b^{n-2})-a^{n-2}-b^{n-2} 
				&= a^n+b^n.
\end{align*}

Now we can prove that  $x_n$  is always integer via strong induction. We know  $x_0$  and  $x_1$  are integer by definition, and if  $x_{n-1},x_{n-2}$  are both integer then obviously  $3x_{n-1}-x_{n-2}$  is as well.

(b) The crucial claim is that  $x_{2^{\nu_2(n)}}\mid x_n$ .

Let  $2^{\nu_2(n)}=k$  and let  $a^k=p$  and  $b^k=q.$  Note that  $pq=(ab)^k=1^k=1$ . Now also note  $p^n+q^m$  is integer since we proved  $a^{km}+b^{km}$  is integer in the last part (as  $km$  is a positive integer).

Let  $r$  be any odd integer. Then we know that  $\frac{p^r+q^r}{p+q}$  is a polynomial, as  $p+q\mid p^r+q^r$ . Since it is symmetric, we also know that it can be expressed as the sum of terms of the form  $(pq)^k(p^j+q^j)$ . Each of these terms are integer, since we know that  $p^j+q^j$  is an integer and  $(pq)^k=1$ . Thus,  $x_{2^{\nu_2(n)}}\mid x_n$ , as desired.

This shows that unless  $n$  is a power of  $2$  or  $0$ ,  $x_n$  is divisible by a number other than itself that is not  $1$ , so for  $x_n$  to be prime we must have  $n=0$  or  $n=2^k$ .</details>

