    \label{lemmageneralizednosquare}

     $x_{2^m}x_{n-2^m} - x_{n-2^{m+1}} = x_n \forall m \in \mathbb{N}$ .

    We use induction. First we notice that for  $m = 0$ ,  $x_{2^0}x_{n-2^0} - x_{n-2^{0+1}} = x_1 x_{n-1} - x_{n-2} = 3x_{n-1} - x_{n-2}$ . This is true by Lemma \ref{lemmanosquare}. Now we show that it being true for  $m$  means it is true for  $m+1$ . Thus we want to show that  $x_{2^{m+1}} x_{n-2^{m+1}} - x_{n-2^{m+2}} = x_n$ . Let  $a = 2^{m}$  for clarity. Thus  $x_n = x_a x_{n-a} - x_{n-2a}$ . Since is being inducted with a base case true for all  $n$  (making this statement true for all  $n$  as well),  $x_{n-2a} = x_{a} x_{n-3a} - x_{n-4a}$ . Substituting,  $x_n = x_a x_{n-a} - (x_a x_{n-3a} - x_{n-4a}) = x_a (x_{n-a} - x_{n-3a}) + x_{n-4a}$ . Once again, we know it true for all  $n$  and get that  $x_{n-a} = x_a x_{n-2a} - x_{n-3a}$  and substitute to get that  $x_n = x_a ((x_a x_{n-2a} - x_{n-3a}) - x_{n-3a}) + x_{n-4a}$ . We do some algebra:
    \begin{align*}
        x_n &= x_a ((x_a x_{n-2a} - x_{n-3a}) - x_{n-3a}) + x_{n-4a}
        &= x_a(x_a x_{n-2a} - 2x_{n-3a}) + x_{n-4a}
        &= x_a(x_a(x_a x_{n-3a} - x_{n-4a}) - 2x_{n-3a}) + x_{n-4a}
        &= x_a(x_{n-3a}(x_a^2 - 2) - x_a x_{n-4a}) + x_{n-4a} 
        &= x_a x_{n-3a} x_{2a} - x_a^2 x_{n-4a} + x_{n-4a}\text{ (by Lemma \ref{lemmaminustwo})}
        &= x_a x_{2a} x_{n-3a} - x_{n-4a}(x_a^2 -1)
        &= x_a x_{2a} x_{n-3a} - x_{n-4a}(x_a^2 - 2) - x_{n-4a}
        &= x_{2a}(x_a x_{n-3a} - x_{n-4a}) - x_{n-4a}\text{(by Lemma \ref{lemmaminustwo})}
    \end{align*}
    Then by our assumption,  $x_a x_{n-3a} - x_{n-4a} = x_{n-2}$ . Thus we have  $x_{2a} x_{n-2a} - x_{n-4a}$ . Resubstituting in the definition of  $a$  we get  $x_{2^{m+1}} x_{n-2^{m+1}} - x_{n-2^{m+2}}$ . Thus by induction the Lemma is true.
    \end{lemma}

    Now we can guarantee all the primes in the sequence to be a power of 2 in the sequence by showing that for all  $m$ ,  $m$  is divisible by the two to the lowest power of  $2$  in the binary representation of  $m$ th term in the sequence of  $x$ . By Lemma \ref{lemmageneralizednosquare},  $x_m = x_{2^r}x_{m-2^r} - x_{m-2^{r+1}}$  where  $r$  is the smallest power of  $2$  in the binary expansion of  $m$ . Clearly  $x_{2^r} | x_{2^r}x_{n-2^r}$ , thus we need to show that  $x_{2^r} | x_{n-2^{r+1}}$ . Now note that this second term's entry in the series ( $m - 2^{r+1}$ ) is equivalent to  $m$  mod  $2^{r+1}$ . Since  $m \equiv 0 \pmod{2^{r+1}}$ , the second term will always be equivalent to  $0$  this modulo. Thus repeated application of this will bring that term to  $x_{2^{r}}$ . Therefore every term is divisible by  $x_{2^{r}}$ . Since for non-powers of 2 in the series the smallest  $r$  isn't equal to the largest, non-powers of  $2$  will always have another divisor. Therefore all primes in this sequence must be a power of two entry (note that when we refer to entry, we use indexing to account for  $x_0$  in the series).</details>

