Consider  $X$ . Because  $DX=(BA-CA)k=ck-bk+\frac{a}{2},$  so  $X=\left(\frac{a}{2}+ck-bk,o\right).$ Consider  $Y$ . The coordinates of  $E$  are  $\left(\frac{p+a}{2},\frac{q}{2}\right).$  Now consider the shift along the  $x$ -axis from  $E$  to  $Y$ . This is just  $\frac{EY}{AC}\cdot (p-a)=\frac{(a-c)k(p-q)}{b}.$  Similarly, the  $y$ -axis shift from  $E$  to  $Y$  is  $\frac{(a-c)kq}{b}.$  It follows that   $$ Y=\left(\frac{p+a}{2}+\frac{(a-c)k(p-a)}{b},\frac{q}{2}+\frac{(a-c)kq}{b}\right). $$  Consider  $Z$ . The coordinates of  $F$  are  $\left(\frac{p}{2},\frac{q}{2}\right).$  Again, the  $x$ -axis shift from  $Z$  to  $F$  is  $\frac{FZ}{AB}(p-0)=\frac{(a-b)kp}{c}$  and the  $y$ -axis shift from  $Z$  to  $F$  is  $\frac{(a-b)kq}{c}.$  Therefore,   $$ Z=\left(\frac{p}{2}+\frac{(a-b)kp}{c},\frac{q}{2}+\frac{(a-b)kq}{c}\right). $$   (Notice that using Cartesian coordinates resolve the problem of directed lengths.)

We now determine equations for lines  $l$  and  $m$ . For  $l$ , this is easy: the equation is just   $$ x=\frac{a}{2}+ck-bk. $$   For  $m$ , we use point slope form:   $$ \frac{y-\frac{q}{2}-\frac{(a-b)kq}{c}}{x-\frac{p}{2}-\frac{(a-b)kp}{c}}=-\frac{p}{q}. $$   The right hand side comes from the fact that the slope of  $m$  is the negative reciprocal of the slope of  $AB$ , which is  $\frac{q-0}{p-0}=\frac{q}{p}.$ Let us now solve this system of equations for  $Q$ , denoting  $Q$  as  $(x_Q,y_Q)$ :   $$ x_Q=\frac{a}{2}+ck-bk, $$    $$ \frac{y_Q-\frac{q}{2}-\frac{(a-b)kq}{c}}{x_Q-\frac{p}{2}-\frac{(a-b)kp}{c}}=-\frac{p}{q}. $$   Plugging the first equation into the second, we get   $$ y_Q-\frac{q}{2}-\frac{(a-b)kq}{c}=-\frac{p}{q}\left(\frac{a}{2}+ck-bk-\frac{p}{2}-\frac{akp}{c}+\frac{bkp}{c}\right), $$   which simplifies to   $$ y_Q=\frac{-apc+2bckp-2c^2kp+p^2c+2akp^2-2bkp^2+cq^2+2akq^2-2bkq^2}{2cq}. $$  We claim that  $QY$  is perpendicular to  $YC$ . To prove this claim we first calculate the slope of  $QY$ , noting that  $y_Y=\frac{q}{2}+\frac{(a-c)kq}{b}$  and  $x_Y=\frac{p+a}{2}+\frac{(a-c)k(p-a)}{b}.$  (Here,  $x_Y$  and  $y_Y$  denote the  $x$  and  $y$  coordinates of  $Y$ , respectively.)

We have 
\begin{align*}
\Delta y&=y_Y-y_M
&=\frac{q}{2}+\frac{(a-c)kq}{b}-\frac{-apc+2bckp-2c^2kp+p^2c+2akp^2-2bkp^2+cq^2+2akq^2-2bkq^2}{2cq}
&=\frac{2akcq^2-2c^2kq^2+apcb-2b^2ckp+2bc^2kp-p^2bc-2abkp^2+2b^2kp^2-2abkq^2+2b^2kq^2}{2cqb}
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\Delta x&=x_Y-x_M
&=\frac{p+a}{2}+\frac{(a-c)k(p-a)}{b}-\frac{a}{2}-ck+bk
&=\frac{bp+2kap+2kca-2kcp-2ka^2-2ckb+2b^2k}{2b}.
\end{align*}

Claim that the  $(\Delta x)_n\cdot (c(a-p))=(\Delta y)_n,$  where  $(\Delta x)_n$  represents the numerator of  $\Delta x$  and  $(\Delta y)_n$  represents the numerator of  $\Delta y.$  We thus have
\begin{align*}
(\Delta x)_n\cdot (c(a-p))&=bpca-bcp^2+2c^2kbp-2b^2cpk+2kS,
\end{align*}
where we have
\begin{align*}
S&=2a^2cp-2ac^2p+a^2c^2-a^3c-ac^2b+ab^2c-acp^2+c^2p^2
&=2acp(a-c)+a^2c(c-a)+abc(b-c)+cp^2(c-a)
&=(c-a)c(a-p)^2+abc(b-c).
\end{align*}
By the Pythagorean Theorem,  $(a-p)^2=b^2-q^2$  and  $p^2+q^2=c^2$ , so
\begin{align*}
S&=(c-a)c(b^2-q^2)+abc(b-c)
&=c^2b^2-c^2q^2-acb^2+acq^2+ab^2c-abc^2
&=cq^2(a-c)+bc^2(b-a)
&=cq^2(a-c)+b(p^2+q^2)(b-a)
&=cq^2a-c^2q^2+b^2p^2-abp^2+b^2q^2-abq^2.
\end{align*}

Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
(\Delta x)_n (c(a-p))&=bpca-bcp^2+2c^2kbp-2b^2cpk+2k(cq^2a-c^2q^2+b^2p^2-abp^2+b^2q^2-abq^2)
&=(\Delta y)_n.
\end{align*}

So, we can figure out the slope  $M$ :
\begin{align*}
M&=\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x}
&=\frac{(\Delta y)_n}{(\Delta x)_n}\cdot \frac{2b}{2cqb}
&=c(a-p)\cdot\frac{1}{cq}
&=\frac{a-p}{q}
&=-\frac{1}{\frac{q}{p-a}},
\end{align*}
so  $M$  is the negative reciprocal of the slope of  $AC$  and therefore  $\angle MYC=90^{\circ}.$  So, the line perpendicular to  $AC$  and  $y$  meets  $l$  and  $m$  at  $Q$ , so the three lines are concurrent. Therefore  $Q=P.$ Now we show that  $P$  is on  $IO.$  Consider the equations for  $y_Q$  and  $x_Q$  in terms of  $k$ :   $$ x_Q=\frac{a}{2}+ck-bk, $$     $$ y_Q=\frac{-apc+2bckp-2c^2kp+p^2c+2akp^2-2bkp^2+cq^2+2akq^2-2bkq^2}{2cq}. $$   These are both linear equations in terms of  $k$ , so as  $k$  (and therefore  $X$ ) varies,  $Q=P$  traces out a straight line. It suffices to show that  $I$  and  $O$  are on that line.

The proof for  $O$  is trivial: let  $k=0.$  Then  $l$  and  $m$  are perpendicular bisectors, and therefore intersect at  $O$ . So  $O$  is on that line.

It remains to show that  $I$  is on that line. Simply let  $k=\frac{c-b}{2}.$  Then,  $BX=ZB=\frac{a-b+c}{2}=s-b,$   $CX=YC=\frac{a+b-c}{2}=s-c,$  and  $AZ=YA=\frac{-a+b+c}{2}=s-a.$  Note that  $X,Y,Z$  are the points of tangency of the incircle with the triangle, so the perpendicular lines from  $X,Y,$  and  $Z$  must intersect at the incenter  $I$ . This proves that  $I$  is on the line too, and we are done. $\blacksquare$

