There are  $4^{n-1}$  double staircases.

Let  $S(n)$  be the set of  $n^2$  cells in an  $n\times n$  grid. Let  $T\subset S(n)$  be a double staircase. 

We wish to get a double staircase  $T'\subset S(n+1)$  by inserting a  $(n+1)\times 1$  (vertical) rectangle and hence also a  $1\times 1$  horizontal rectangle (because the  $(n+1)\times 1$  rectangle must overlap with each row, so the  $1\times 1$  horizontal rectangle must be part of it). But we cannot just insert this anywhere; it must be adjacent to the  $n\times 1$  rectangle. This is because if they are not adjacent there will be a gap in some row because none of the remaining vertical rectangles  $1\times 1, 2\times 1, \ldots , (n-2)\times 1$  are tall enough to cover the gap. Since this is not allowed, the  $n\times 1$  rectangle and the  $(n+1)\times 1$  rectangle must be next to each other. This can be done by placing the  $(n+1)\times 1$  rectangle either to the left or right of it and aligned either at the bottom or top.
[asy]
import patterns;
add("hatch",hatch(0.9mm));
size(200);
path sq(pair A) {return shift(A)*unitsquare;}
picture reflect_h(picture p) {return reflect((0,-1),(0,1))*p;}
picture reflect_v(picture p) {return reflect((-1,0),(1,0))*p;}

path p = box(right,2*right+3*up);
path[] q = unitsquare^^sq(up)^^sq(right)^^sq(right+up)^^sq(right+2*up);

picture pic, pic2, pic3, pic4;
fill(pic,p, palegray+opacity(0.25));
draw(pic,q);
add(pic);
add(shift((3,3))*reflect_v(pic));
add(shift((8,0))*reflect_h(pic));
add(shift(11,3)*rotate(180)*pic);
[/asy]
Having done this there is an unique way to complete this into a  $(n+1)\times (n+1)$  square: just fill in the row that is jutting out. The new square now has an extra  $1\times 1$  square in each row (including the newly added row). Thus we now have  $n+1$  horizontal nonoverlapping rectangles of dimensions  $1\times 1, 1\times 2, \ldots , 1\times (n+1)$  and also  $n+1$  vertical nonoverlapping rectangles of dimensions  $1\times 1, 2\times 1, \ldots , (n+1)\times 1$ , i.e., we have a double staircase.

On the other hand, by reversing the arguments above we easily see that starting from a double staircase,  $T$ , of  $S(n+1)$ , we get a double staircase,  $T'=T\cap S(n)$ , of  $S(n)$  by removing the  $(n+1)\times 1$  vertical rectangle and the row that contains the  $1\times 1$  horizontal rectangle. Thus, each and every double staircase of  $S(n+1)$  is obtained by inserting a  $(n+1)\times 1$  (vertical) rectangle to  $S(n)$ , in one of four possible ways, and then completing the square.

Denote by  $f(n)$  the desired number of  double staircases. The arguments above show that  $f(n+1)=4f(n)$ . Clearly  $f(1)=1=4^0$ , so it follows that  $f(n)=4^{n-1}$ .

