On the first turn, place card 1 into either pile. For each of the 24 remaining turns, play as follows: If Winnie played the largest available card on the previous turn, place the next largest card in the opposite pile. Call such a pair of turns a type 1 exchange. If Winnie does not play the largest remaining playable card and plays card  $x$  instead, place  $x+1$  in the opposite pile if  $x$  is even and place  $x-1$  in the opposite pile if  $x$  is odd. Call such a pair of moves a type 2 exchange.

<details><summary>Proof? that Winnie wins at most \ $75</summary>Let $ k $ be the number of type 1 exchanges. $ w_i $ and $ g_i $ be the cards Winnie and Grogg play respectively on the $ i $th type 1 exchange for $ 1\leq i\leq k $. Let $ w_{k+1} $ be the final card Winnie plays. Then we have $ w_1\geq g_1 \geq w_2\geq g_2\geq\dots\geq w_k \geq g_k \geq w_{k+1}. $ Hence the net change on the difference of the pile sums from all type 1 exchanges and Winnie's last turn is at most
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i=1}^k (w_i-g_i)+w_{k+1}&\leq\sum_{i=1}^k (w_i-w_{i+1})+w_{k+1} 
    &=w_1-w_{k+1}+w_{k+1}
    &=w_1
    &=50.
\end{align*}
Note that the net change on the difference of the pile sums from all type 2 exchanges is at most 24 since there at most 24 type 2 exchanges and each exchange changes the difference of the pile sums by one. Thus the difference of the pile sums at the end of the game is at most 1+50+24=75 and Grogg can guarantee that Winnie wins at most \$ 75.</details>

<details><summary>Proof? that you can play it</summary>Note that the sequence of cards played in type 1 exchanges alternates in parity since type 2 exchanges eliminate even/odd pairs. Since in the first type 1 exchange Willie plays 50, Willie and Grogg play even and odd cards respectively in type 1 exchanges. Suppose Willie plays card  $x$  on a turn where  $x$  is not the largest available card. If  $x$  is even,  $x+1$  must still available to play ( $x+1\neq 1,$  Grogg could not have played  $x+1$  before in a type 1 exchange because then  $x$  would have been the largest card on Willie's turn or type 2 exchange because that would only be prompted by Willie playing  $x$  the previous turn, and Willie could not have played  $x+1$  before in a type 1 exchange because  $x+1$  is odd or type 2 exchange because Grogg would have played  $x$  the following turn). If  $x$  is odd,  $x-1$  is available to play ( $x\neq 2$  so  $x-1\neq 1$ , neither player could have already played  $x-1$  in a type 1 exchange since  $x>x-1,$  Grogg could not have played  $x-1$  before in a type 2 exchange because then Winnie would have played  $x$  the previous turn, and Winnie could not have played  $x-1$  before in a type 2 exchange because Grogg would have played  $x$  the following turn). It follows that this strategy is possible to play.</details>

