The partial answer, with  $c_k$  for  $k\equiv1,3\pmod{10}\ge10$  omitted, is
\[\boxed{c_k=\begin{cases}4&\text{if }k\equiv0\pmod{2},9&\text{if }k=3,5&\text{if }k\equiv5,7,9\pmod{10}.\end{cases}}\]**Claim.**  $c_k\ge4$  with equality iff  $k\equiv0\pmod{2}$ .**Proof.** The colors of  $(0,0)$ ,  $(1,0)$ ,  $(0,1)$ , and  $(1,1)$  are all distinct, since those points are pairwise adjacent, giving the desired  $c_k\ge4$ . Equality holds when  $k\equiv0\pmod{2}$ , via the following coloring.
[asy]
size(5cm);

for(int i=-1; i<2; ++i){
draw((8*i-0.5,-10.5)--(8*i-0.5,9.5));
draw((-10.5,8*i-0.5)--(9.5,8*i-0.5));
}

for(int i=-5; i<5; ++i){
for(int j=-5; j<5; ++j){
dot((2*i,2*j),red);
dot((2*i+1,2*j),royalblue);
dot((2*i,2*j+1),green);
dot((2*i+1,2*j+1),yellow);
}
}
[/asy]
Equality cannot ever hold otherwise; to see why, for the sake of contradiction suppose it can. Then, if WLOG  $(0,0)$  and  $(0,1)$  are colored red and green,  $(1,0)$  and  $(1,1)$  must be colored blue and yellow in some order, by the adjacency condition. In turn,  $(2,0)$  and  $(2,1)$  must be colored red and green, and continuing yields that  $(k,0)$  and  $(k,1)$  must be colored blue and yellow, since  $k$  is odd by assumption. But by the  $\pmod{k}$  equivalency condition,  $(0,0)$  and  $(k,0)$  must be the same color, contradiction.  $\Box$ **Claim.**  $c_3=9$ . **Proof.**
[asy]
size(5cm);

for(int i=-1; i<2; ++i){
draw((3*i-0.5,-4.5)--(3*i-0.5,3.5));
draw((-4.5,3*i-0.5)--(3.5,3*i-0.5));
}

