Because of the  $\pmod{3}$  equivalency condition, all  $9$  points in any  $3\times3$  grid are "adjacent" in our interest, and must thus be colored distinctly ( $c_3\ge9$ ). For example, an  $\text{I}$  as shown above is adjacent not only to the corresponding  $\text{E}$ ,  $\text{F}$ , and  $\text{H}$  in the same  $3\times3$  grid, but also to  $\text{A}$ ,  $\text{B}$ ,  $\text{C}$ ,  $\text{D}$ , and  $\text{G}$  due to the grids above and to the right. And clearly, a feasible coloring with  $9$  colors exists ( $c_3=9$ ): it suffices to replace each of the letters  $\text{A}$ ,  $\text{B}$ ,  $\text{C}$ ,  $\text{D}$ ,  $\text{E}$ ,  $\text{F}$ ,  $\text{G}$ ,  $\text{H}$ , and  $\text{I}$  above with colors.  $\Box$ **Claim.**  $c_k=5$  if  $k\equiv5,7,9\pmod{10}$ .**Proof.** The first claim implies that  $c_k>4$  if  $k\equiv1\pmod{2}$ ; as a result, it suffices to provide a coloring with  $5$  colors for each of these values. (Letters will henceforth supersede colors in all diagrams.)
