Let  $E=(k,0)$ . We will begin by calculating the coordinates of the circumcenters  $O_1$  and  $O_2$  of triangles  $ADE$  and  $BCE$ , respectively.


- First,  $\overline{AD}$  has slope  $\tfrac{s}{r+1}$  and midpoint  $(\tfrac{r+1}{2},\tfrac{s}{2})$ . Hence, its perpendicular bisector has slope  $-\tfrac{r+1}{s}$  and passes through  $(\tfrac{r+1}{2},\tfrac{s}{2})$ , so it has equation  $\tfrac{y-s/2}{x-(r+1)/2}=-\tfrac{r+1}{s}$  or  $y=-\tfrac{r+1}{s}x+\tfrac{r^2+2r+1}{2s}+\tfrac{s}{2}$ . Also, the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AE}$  is simply  $x=\tfrac{k}{2}$ , so the coordinates of  $O_1$  are given by
\begin{align*}
y&=-\frac{r+1}{s}x+\frac{r^2+2r+1}{2s}+\frac{s}{2}
x&=\frac{k}{2}.
\end{align*}
Plugging the second equation into the first gives  $O_1=(\tfrac{k}{2},-\tfrac{r+1}{2s}k+\tfrac{r^2+2r+1}{2s}+\tfrac{s}{2})$ .
- Next,  $\overline{BC}$  has slope  $\tfrac{s}{r-1}$  and midpoint  $(\tfrac{r+1}{2},\tfrac{s}{2})$ . Thus, its perpendicular bisector has slope  $-\tfrac{r-1}{s}$  and passes through  $(\tfrac{r+1}{2},\tfrac{s}{2})$ , so it has equation  $\tfrac{y-s/2}{x-(r+1)/2}=-\tfrac{r-1}{s}$  or  $y=-\tfrac{r-1}{s}+\tfrac{r^2-1}{2s}+\tfrac{s}{2}$ . Additionally, the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{BE}$  is  $x=\tfrac{k+1}{2}$ , so the coordinates of  $O_2$  are given by
\begin{align*}
y&=-\frac{r-1}{s}+\frac{r^2-1}{2s}+\frac{s}{2}
x&=\frac{k+1}{2}.
\end{align*}
Plugging the second equation into the first yields  $O_2=(\tfrac{k+1}{2},-\tfrac{r-1}{2s}(k+1)+\tfrac{r^2-1}{2s}+\tfrac{s}{2})$ .

Now, we wish for  $P=(x,y)$  to lie on the radical axis of  $(ADE)$  and  $(BCE)$ , which by the Radical Axis Theorem is equivalent to it having equal power with respect to the two circles. Accordingly we write
\begin{align*}
\text{Pow}_{(ADE)}(P)&=O_1P^2-O_1E^2=\left(\frac{k}{2}-x\right)^2+(y(O_1)-y)^2-\left(\frac{k}{2}-k\right)^2-(y(O_1)-0)^2
\text{Pow}_{(BCE)}(P)&=O_2P^2-O_2E^2=\left(\frac{k+1}{2}-x\right)^2+(y(O_2)-y)^2-\left(\frac{k+1}{2}-k\right)^2-(y(O_2)-0)^2,
\end{align*}
and cancelling and equalling gives us
\[x^2-kx+y^2-\left(-\frac{r+1}{s}k+\frac{r^2+2r+1}{s}+s\right)y=x^2-(k+1)x+y^2-\left(-\frac{r-1}{s}(k+1)+\frac{r^2-1}{s}+s\right)y+k.\]
Further rearranging and simplifying yields that
\[x=\left(-\frac{r+1}{s}k+\frac{r-1}{s}(k+1)+\frac{r^2+2r+1}{s}-\frac{r^2-1}{s}+s-s\right)y=\left(\frac{-2k+3r+1}{s}\right)y+k,\]
and rewriting once more we have  $x-\tfrac{3r+1}{s}y=\left(-\tfrac{2y}{s}+1\right)k$ . In order for this equation to hold for all  $k\in[0,1]$ , it is necessary to have that  $x-\tfrac{3r+1}{s}y=-\tfrac{2y}{s}+1=0$ . Solving,  $(x,y)=\boxed{(\tfrac{3r+1}{2},\tfrac{s}{2})}$ .

