After these two steps, the general idea of how recursion works arises. Essentially, for the  $k$ th iteration of our recursion, we take the smallest  $8^{k-1}$  dilated octagons in the previous step, and for each of these octagons, take homotheties  $\mathcal H$  of these small octagons with the same dilation factor of  $\tfrac{1}{t}$  as before, with respect to their vertices, to form even smaller octagons. Then, we may configure these even smaller octagons with working configurations of  $n$  for the previous iteration to reach working configurations for the new iteration.

To rigorize our recursion idea, employ strong induction. The base case is evident, as above we described the configurations for  $n=1,2,3,4.$  Now for the inductive step. Split  $n$  into sets  $(2^k,2^{k+1}],$  where here,  $k$  denotes the iteration. Assume that all  $n \in [1,2^k]$  have working configurations. We want to prove then that  $n \in (2^k,2^{k+1}]$  work. To do so, note we may express  $n \in (2^k,2^{k+1}]$  in the form  $2^k+m,$  where  $m \in [1,2^k].$  Evidently, by assumption, both  $2^k$  and  $m$  are working configurations. Note that the construction for  $2^k$  is just the octagon recursion iterated  $k$  times. Then, consider the following steps to go from the construction for  $2^k$  to the construction for  $2^k+m:$ Consider one big equiangular octagon, that is, the one in the form of the octagon depicted for the construction of  $n=2.$  Now, as aforementioned, take a homothety  $\mathcal{H}$  of this octagon  $\mathcal{P}$  centered at all its vertices, all with equal, sufficient small dilation factors of  $\tfrac{1}{t}.$  Take one of the  $8$  new dilated octagons, and replace the dilated octagon with the construction for  $2^k$  (essentially, within this dilated octagon, we implant the configuration for  $n=2^k$ ). Next, take a dilated octagon neighboring the one we just implanted the construction of  $2^k$  in, and in this neighboring octagon, implant the construction of  $m,$  which exists due to inductive assumption. Continue implanting constructions of  $2^k$  and  $m$  into the dilated octagons, such that all octagons neighboring an octagon with construction  $2^k$  are implanted with construction  $m,$  and all octagons neighboring an octagon with construction  $m$  are implanted with construction  $2^k.$  The process of terminates when all  $8$  dilated octagons are implanted with  $m$  or  $2^k,$  and in the end we result with an alternating pattern of dilated octagons with implanted constructions of  $2^k$  and  $m.$ It suffices to verify that such a configuration works. Note that all lines  $\ell$  mentioned in the problem statement will either not pass through any points in the aforementioned configuration, or they will pass through the sides of two dilated opposite octagons, where octagons are opposite if they are reflections of each other across one of the  $8$  lines of symmetry of the large octagon. In fact, these two opposite octagons will be of different constructions, that is, one dilated octagon will be of configuration  $n=2^k$  and the other will be of  $n=m.$  This is evident due to the alternating pattern between configurations of  $2^k$  and  $m.$  Hence, taking any line of form  $\ell$  will result with  $2^k+m$  lattice points on the such line. As we defined  $m \in [1,2^k],$  it follows  $n \in (2^k,2^{k+1}]$  all work, completing the strong induction. It readily follows that there exists a configuration for when  $n=2022,$  which occurs when  $n \in (2^{10},2^{11}],$  as desired.

