This is my problem. This problem originally came from needing a (comparatively) fiendish problem for an introductory lesson on divisibility tests; the problem in its original form involved looking at  $37$  consecutive three-digit positive integers (with the idea to generalize the divisibility test for  $9$  to  $999,$  since  $37 \mid 999$ ), but I ran with this version of the question for the USAMTS submission since I figured  $101$  would be a better "signpost" for where and how one might look for a solution.

The solutions here are essentially correct, but since I have it on hand, here's the solution I originally submitted with my problem: <details><summary>Solution</summary>Any concatenation of the given numbers can be written as
\[n=a_{100} \cdot 10000^{100}+a_{99} \cdot 10000^{99}+ \cdots + a_1 \cdot 10000^1 + a_0 \cdot 10000^0 \]
for some rearrangement  $a_0,\ a_1,\ \ldots,\ a_{100}$  of the positive integers  $2024,\ 2025,\ \ldots,\ 2124.$  Then
\begin{align*}
n &\equiv a_{100} \cdot 10000^{100}+a_{99} \cdot 10000^{99}+ \cdots + a_1 \cdot 10000^1 + a_0 \cdot 10000^0  \pmod{9999} 
&\equiv a_{100} \cdot 1^{100}+a_{99} \cdot 1^{99} + \cdots + a_1 \cdot 1^1 + a_0 \cdot 1^0 \pmod{9999} 
&\equiv a_{100}+a_{99}+\cdots+a_1+a_0 \pmod{9999} 
&\equiv 2024+2025+\cdots+2124 \pmod{9999} 
&\equiv 101 \cdot 2023 + (1+2+\cdots+101) \pmod{9999} 
&\equiv 101 \cdot 2023 + \dfrac{101 \cdot 102}{2} \pmod{9999} 
&\equiv 101 \cdot (2023+51) \pmod{9999}.
\end{align*}
Since  $101 \mid 9999,$  it follows that  $n$  must be a multiple of  $101$  and therefore cannot be prime.</details>

