<details><summary>Wordy solution</summary>No, it is not possible for such a number to be prime. Denoting this number as  $N=c_{404}c_{403}\cdots c_1$ , where  $c_i$  represent digits in base  $10$ , we will show that  $N$  is always divisible by  $101$ .
\vspace{12pt}

\textbf{Lemma:}
If the sum  $\sum_{i=1}^{2n}(-1)^{i+1}(10a_{2i}+a_{2i-1})$  is divisible by  $101$ , where  $a_i$  represent digits in base  $10$ , then the  $4n$ -digit integer  $a_{4n}a_{4n-1}\cdots a_1$  is also divisible by  $101$ .

\textbf{Proof:}
Notice that  $10^{4k}\equiv 1\pmod{101}$ ,  $10^{4k+1}\equiv10\pmod{101}$ ,  $10^{4k+2}\equiv100\equiv-1\pmod{101}$ , and  $10^{4k+3}\equiv-10\pmod{101}$ , for all nonnegative integers  $k$ . It follows that
\begin{align*}
    a_{4n}a_{4n-1}\cdots a_1&=\sum_{i=1}^{4n}10^{i-1}a_{i} 
    &=10^0a_1+10^1a_2+10^2a_3+10^4a_5+\dots+10^{4n-2}a_{4n-1}+10^{4n-1}a_{4n} 
    &\equiv a_1+10a_2-a_3-10a_4+a_5+\dots+a_{4n-1}+10a_{4n}\pmod{101} 
    &=\sum_{i=1}^{2n}(-1)^{i+1}(10a_{2i}+a_{2i-1}),
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\blacksquare$ \vspace{12pt}

Notice that, for  $N$ , the sum  $\sum_{i=1}^{202}(-1)^{i+1}(10c_{2i}+c_{2i-1})$  is constant, regardless of which order the  $4$ -digit strings are concatenated in. This is because the last two digits of each of these strings will always be added while the first two digits of each string will always be subtracted.

To compute this sum, we add the numbers  $24$  through  $99$  (as the strings go from  $2024$  to  $2099$ ), and the numbers  $1$  through  $24$  (as the strings go from  $2100$  to  $2124$ ). We have  $99-24+1=76$  strings starting with  $20$  and  $25$  strings starting with  $21$ , so we subtract  $76\cdot20+25\cdot21$  from our sum. It follows that
\begin{align*}
    N&\equiv\left(\frac{99(100)}{2}+24\right)-\left(76\cdot20+25\cdot21\right)\pmod{101} 
    &=4974-2045=2929\equiv0\pmod{101}.
\end{align*}
We conclude that  $N$  is always divisible by  $101$ , regardless of which order we concatenate the  $4$ -digit strings in, and so it is impossible for  $N$  to be prime.  $\blacksquare$</details>

