<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $a$ ,  $b$ , and  $c$  be roots of the cubic polynomial  $P(z)=pz^3+qz^2+rz+s$ , for some real  $p$ ,  $q$ ,  $r$ , and  $s$ . By Vieta's formulas, we have
\begin{align*}
    a+b+c&=-\frac{q}{p} 
    ab+ac+bc&=\frac{r}{p} 
    abc&=-\frac{s}{p}.
\end{align*}
Letting  $p=2$ , it follows from the original system of equations that
\begin{align*}
    P(z)&=2z^3-2(a+b+c)z^2+2(ab+ac+bc)z-2abc 
    &=2z^3-(x+x^2+x^4+y+y^2+y^4)z^2+(x^3+x^5+x^6+y^3+y^5+y^6)z-(x^7+y^7)
\end{align*}
has roots  $a$ ,  $b$ , and  $c$ . For there to be a real solution  $(a,b,c)$ , these roots must all be real. The number of real roots of a cubic can be determined by the discriminant,  $$ \Delta=18pqrs-4q^3s+q^2r^2-4pr^3-27p^2s^2. $$  (Source: Wolfram MathWorld; https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PolynomialDiscriminant.html)

If  $\Delta\geq0$ , the cubic will have  $3$  real roots, and distinct ones if  $\Delta>0$ . Making substitutions for  $p$ ,  $q$ ,  $r$ , and  $s$ , in terms of  $a$ ,  $b$ , and  $c$ , we have
\begin{align*}
    \Delta&=288(a+b+c)(ab+ac+bc)(abc)-64(a+b+c)^3(abc)+8(a+b+c)^2(ab+ac+bc)^2-432(abc)^2.
\end{align*}
We wish to show that
\begin{align*}
    36(a+b+c)(ab+ac+bc)abc+(a+b+c)^2(ab+ac+bc)&\geq8(a+b+c)^3abc+54(abc)^2\qquad(1)
\end{align*}
whenever  $1\leq\sqrt{x}\leq y\leq x^2$ . From this inequality, we get
\begin{align*}
    2(a+b+c)&=x+x^2+x^4+y(1+y+y^3) 
    &\leq x+x^2+x^4+x^2(1+x^2+x^6) 
    &=x^8+2x^4+2x^2+x
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
    2abc&=x^7+y^7 
    &\geq x^7+x^{7/2}.
\end{align*}
Since  $x^7\leq x^8$  and  $x^{7/2}\leq x^4$ , we get  $a+b+c\geq abc$ . The degree in each term of  $ab+ac+bc$  is higher than that in  $a+b+c$ , so we conclude  $$ ab+ac+bc\geq a+b+c\geq abc. $$  We substitute  $abc$  with  $a+b+c$  in the right-hand side of inequality  $(1)$ , and  $a+b+c$  and  $abc$  with  $ab+ac+bc$  in the left-hand side to obtain
\begin{align*}
    36(ab+ac+bc)^3+(ab+ac+bc)^3&\geq8(a+b+c)^4+54(a+b+c)^2 
    37(ab+ac+bc)^3&\geq8(a+b+c)^4+54(a+b+c)^2.
\end{align*}
Notice that the polynomial on the left-hand side,  $37(x^3+x^5+x^6+y^3+y^5+y^6)^3$ , has degree  $18$  whereas the right-hand side has degree  $4\cdot4=16$ . Therefore, this inequality is true and so there exists a real solution  $(a,b,c)$  whenever  $1\leq\sqrt{x}\leq y\leq x^2$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$</details>

