WLOG assume the  $13$ -gon has sidelength  $1$ . We now angle chase in radians.

\begin{align*}
\angle A_1 &= \frac{11\pi}{13} 
\angle A_1A_2A_{13} &= \frac{\pi}{13} 
\angle A_3A_2A_{13} &= \frac{10\pi}{13} 
\angle A_2A_3A_{12} &= \frac{3\pi}{13} 
\angle A_4A_3A_{12} &= \frac{8\pi}{13} 
\angle A_3A_4A_{11} &= \frac{5\pi}{13} 
\angle A_{11}A_4A_5 &= \frac{6\pi}{13} 
\angle A_4A_5A_{10} &= \frac{7\pi}{13} 
\angle A_{10}A_5A_{6} &= \frac{4\pi}{13} 
\angle A_5A_6A_9 &= \frac{9\pi}{13} 
\angle A_9A_6A_7 &= \frac{2\pi}{13} 
\angle A_6A_7A_6 &= \frac{11\pi}{13} 
\angle A_8A_7B &= \frac{2\pi}{13} 
\angle B &= \frac{9\pi}{13}. 
\end{align*}

Inputting "regular 13-gon" into Wolfram Alpha, we can scroll down to properties and view "independent diagonal lengths". As these are in increasing order, we can see that we have  $A_2A_{13}=2\cos\frac{\pi}{13}, A_3A_{12}=\cos\frac{5\pi}{26}\csc\frac{\pi}{13}, A_4A_{11}=\frac{1}{2}\csc\frac{\pi}{26}, A_5A_{10}=\cos\frac{3\pi}{26}\csc\frac{\pi}{13}, A_6A_9=2\cos\frac{2\pi}{13}+1$ . Additionally, we have  $A_7A_8=1$ . Then, using the  $\frac{1}{2}ab\sin{C}$  formula for area of a triangle, we can find that the area of  $A_2A_3A_{12}A_{13}$  is   $$ \frac{1}{2}\left(2\cos\frac{\pi}{13}\sin\frac{10\pi}{13}+\cos\frac{5\pi}{26}\csc\frac{\pi}{13}\right). $$   Doing a similar thing for the other shaded quadrilaterals, we can find their areas to be   $$ \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{2}\csc\frac{\pi}{26}\sin\frac{6\pi}{13}+\cos\frac{3\pi}{26}\csc\frac{\pi}{13}\sin\frac{7\pi}{13}\right) $$   and  $$ \frac{1}{2}\left(\left(2\cos\frac{2\pi}{13}+1\right)\sin\frac{2\pi}{13}+\sin\frac{11\pi}{13}\right). $$   The sum of these is the total area of the shaded region. Next, using the properties from the same Wolfram Alpha input as before, we get that the area of a regular  $13$ -gon of sidelength  $1$  is  $\frac{13}{4}\cot\frac{\pi}{13}$ . We can add this to the area of triangle  $A_7A_8B$  to find the total area of the shape.  $A_7A_8B$  is just an isosceles triangle, so let the foot from  $B$  onto  $A_7A_8$  be  $M$ . Then,  $\angle A_7BM=\frac{9\pi}{26}$  and  $A_7M=\frac{1}{2}$ . Thus,  $BM=\frac{1}{2}\cot\frac{9\pi}{26}$ , so the area of the triangle is  $\frac{1}{4}\cot\frac{9\pi}{26}$ . Then, the total area of the polygon is   $$ \frac{13}{4}\cot\frac{\pi}{13}+\frac{1}{4}\cot\frac{9\pi}{26}. $$   Finally, we can sum the three shaded areas and set it equal to  $\frac{1}{2}$  of the total area we found, and input that equality into Wolfram Alpha. It outputs "True", which means that the shaded area is exactly half the total area, as needed.

