Also, trivially, we note that  $IF = IE$  as both are the inradius. Lastly, we have  $\angle IEY = \angle IFX = 90^{\circ}$ . Combining these facts yields the desired claim.  $\blacksquare$ From the claim, we may deduce that  $XF = YE$ . Now,  $P = XY \cap EF$ , now to show  $P$  is the midpoint of  $XY$ . By Law of Sines on  $\triangle YPE$  and  $\triangle XPF$ , we have:
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{XP}{\sin \angle PFX} = \dfrac{FX}{\sin \angle FPX} = \dfrac{YE}{\sin \angle YPE} = \dfrac{YP}{\sin \angle YEP}.
\end{align*}
However,  $\angle PFX = \angle AFE = \angle AEF = \angle YEP$ . Hence,  $XP = PY$ , so  $P$  is the midpoint of  $XY$ .

