solution_md:
We claim that the answer is  $\boxed{n - m \equiv 0 \pmod 4 \text{ OR } n + m \equiv 1 \pmod 4}$ \textbf{Part 1: Eliminating other values}

To prove this, assume a set  $T$  satisfies the constraint. For any integer  $i$ , let  $x_i$  represent the number of  $k$  for which  $(i, k) \in T$ , let  $y_i$  represent the number of  $k$  for which  $(k, i) \in T$ , and let  $d_i$  represent the number of  $k$  for which  $(k, i-k) \in T$ .

Note that:  $$ \sum_{(x, y) \in T} (x+y) = \sum_{(x, y) \in T} x + \sum_{(x, y) \in T} y = \sum_{i=1}^{m + n -1} ix_i + \sum_{i=1}^{m+n-1} iy_i $$  However, at the same time:  $$ \sum_{(x, y) \in T} (x+y) = \sum_{i = m+1}^{2m + n - 1}id_i $$  Thus:  $$ \sum_{i=1}^{m + n -1} ix_i + \sum_{i=1}^{m+n-1} iy_i = \sum_{i = m+1}^{2m + n - 1}id_i $$  Now, we also have that  $d_i, x_i, y_i$  are odd for all  $i$ . Thus:

\begin{align*}
    &2 \sum_{i = 1}^{m+n-1} i \equiv  \sum_{i = m+1}^{2m + n - 1}id_i \pmod 2 
\iff & \frac{(3m+n)(m + n -1)}{2} \equiv 0 \pmod 2 
\iff & 4|(n - m)(m + n -1) 
\end{align*}

Now,  $n-m$  and  $n+ m-1$  are of opposite parity. Thus, this means that we must have either  $n - m \equiv 0 \pmod 4$  or  $n + m = 1 \pmod 4$ \textbf{Part 2: Construction}

WLOG assume that  $m \le n$  (otherwise, reflect the set across the line  $x + y = m + n$ ).

\bigskip

We first prove that if  $n = 1$  and  $n \equiv 0, 1 \pmod 4$ , then the constraint is satisfied. If  $n = 0, 1$ , then just consider the empty set and the single vertex  $(1, 1)$  respectively. Henceforth assume  $n \ge 4$ .

\bigskip

Indeed, if  $n \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ , then consider the set:
\begin{align*}
T = &\{(1, 1), (1, 2), ..., (1, n)\} 
\cup &\{(1, (n+1)/2), (2, (n+1)/2 - 1), ...((n+1)/2, 1\} 
\cup &\{((n+3)/2, (n-1)/2), ((n+5)/2, (n-3)/2), ..., (n, 1)\}
\end{align*}

Clearly, any vertical line other than  $x = 0$  intersects  $T$  at only  $1$  point. On the other hand,  $x = 0$  intersects  $T$  at  $n$  points. 

Also, if  $ 1 \le i \le (n-1)/2$ , then  $y = i$  intersects  $T$  at  $3$  points, while if  $(m+1)/2 \le i \le n$ , then  $y=i$  intersects  $T$  at  $1$  point.

Furthermore, if  $1 \le i \le n+1$  and  $i \ne (n+3)/2, (n+1)$ , then  $x + y = i$  intersects  $T$  at only one point. If  $ i \in \{(n+3)/2, n+1\}$ , then  $x+ y = i$  intersects  $T$  at  $(n+1)/2$  points (which is clearly an odd number).

\bigskip

On the other hand, if  $n \equiv 0 \pmod 4$ , then consider the set:
\begin{align*}
    S = & \{(1, 1), (1, 2), (1, 4), ..., (1, n)\} 
    & \cup \{(2, n-1), (4, n-3), ..., (n, 1)\} 
    & \cup \{(n-1, 1), (n-3, 1), ..., (1, 1)\}
\end{align*}

Clearly,  $x = 1$  intersects with  $n/2 + 1$  elements of  $T$ , while all other vertical lines intersect with  $1$  element of  $T$ .

Meanwhile,  $y = 1$  intersects with  $n/2 + 1$  elements of  $T$ , while all other horizontal lines intersect with  $1$  element of  $T$ .

Finally,  $x + y = i$  intersects with  $1$  element of  $T$  for all  $i < n + 1$ . Meanwhile,  $x + y = n+1$  intersects with  $n/2 + 1$  elements of  $T$ .

Since  $n/2 + 1$  is odd,  $T$  satisfies the condition.

\bigskip

Now, we tackle the main problem. Let  $S_{m, n}$  denote the set  $S$  for a given value of  $m, n$ . We claim that we can always find a working  $T$  if  $(m, n) = (0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3), (0, 1), (1, 0), (2, 3), (3, 2) \pmod 4$ . This is equivalent to the condition  $m- n \equiv 0 \pmod 4$  or  $m+ n \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ .
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Case 1:  $m$  is even (i.e.,  $(m, n) = (0, 1), (0, 0), (2, 2), (2, 3) \pmod 4$ ).

    Consider the set  $S'$  of points in  $S_{m, n}$  that satisfy:  $m \le x, m \le y, x + y \le  m+n - 1$ . Note that this set is  $S_{1, n - m}$  translated up by  $n - m -1$ . Let  $T'$  be a subset of this set such that each of the lines parallel to either  $x = 0, y = 0,$  or  $x+y = 0$  that intersects  $S'$  at an odd number of points (which exists since in all these cases,  $n - m \equiv 0, 1 \pmod 4$ ).

