        & \cup \{ (m,1), (m, 2), ..., (m , m-1)\} 
        & \cup \{(n, m), (n + 1, m), ..., (n + m - 1, m)\}
    \end{align*}

    Conceptually,  $T$  is the union of  $T'$ , the left "half" of the lowest vertical line that intersects  $T'$ , the bottom "half" of the leftmost horizontal line that intersects  $T'$ , and the top "half" of the \textbf{lowest diagonal that lies strictly above}  $T'$  (by "halves" here, we mean closed rays created by the intersection of the lines with the set  $ m \le x, m \le y, x + y \le m+n$ , which is  $S$  with an extra diagonal). 

    For  $1 \le i < m$ , the line  $x= i$  intersects  $T$  at one point  $(i, m+n - i)$ . The line  $x = m$  intersects  $T'$  at an odd number of points, while passing through an additional  $1 + m-1 = m$  points of  $T$ . For  $m < i <n$ , the line  $x = i$  intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points. Finally, for  $n \le i \le m+n-1$ , the line  $x = i$  intersects  $T$  only at  $(i, m)$ . Thus, every vertical line that intersects  $S$  also intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points.

    For  $1 \le i < m$ , the line  $y = i$  intersects  $T$  at one point  $(m, i)$ . The line  $y = m$  intersects  $T'$  at an odd number of points, while passing through an additional  $m-1$  points of  $T$ . For  $m < i < n$ , the line  $y = i$  intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points. Finally, for  $n \le i \le m+n-1$ , the line  $y = i$  intersects  $T$  only at  $(m + n - i , i)$ . Thus, every horizontal line that intersects  $S$  also intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points.

    For  $m+1 \le i \le 2m-1$ , the line  $x + y = i$  intersects  $T$  at one point  $(m, i -m)$ . For  $2m \le i \le m + n-1$ ,  $x + y = i$  intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points by the assumption on  $T'$ . For  $i = m+n$ ,  $x + y = i$  intersects  $T$  at  $m +1$  points. Finally, for  $m+n \le i \le 2m + n -1$ ,  $x+ y = i$  intersects  $T$  only at  $(i - m, m)$ . As a result, every diagonal line intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points.

