    \begin{align*}
        T = T' & \cup \{(1, m+n-1), (2, m+n-2), ..., (m-1, n)\} 
        & \cup \{ (m,1), (m, 2), ..., (m , m-1)\} 
        & \cup \{(n+1, m), (n + 1, m), ..., (n + m - 1, m)\}
    \end{align*}

    Conceptually,  $T$  is the union of  $T'$ , the left "half" of the lowest vertical line that intersects  $T'$ , the bottom "half" of the leftmost horizontal line that intersects  $T'$ , and the top "half" of the \textbf{highest diagonal that intersects}  $T'$  (by "halves" here, we mean \textbf{ open } rays created by the intersection of the lines with  $S$ ).

    For  $1 \le i < m$ , the line  $x= i$  intersects  $T$  at one point  $(i, m+n - i)$ . The line  $x = m$  intersects  $T'$  at an odd number of points, while passing through an additional  $ m-1$  points of  $T$ . For  $m < i \le n$ , the line  $x = i$  intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points. Finally, for  $i > n$ , the line  $x = i$  intersects  $T$  only at  $(i, m)$ . Thus, every vertical line that intersects  $S$  also intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points.

    For  $1 \le i < m$ , the line  $y = i$  intersects  $T$  at one point  $(m, i)$ . The line  $y = m$  intersects  $T'$  at an odd number of points, while passing through an additional  $m-1$  points of  $T$ . For  $m < i \le n$ , the line  $y = i$  intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points. Finally, for  $i > n$ , the line  $y = i$  intersects  $T$  only at  $(m + n - i, i)$ . Thus, every horizontal line that intersects  $S$  also intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points.

    For  $m+1 \le i \le 2m-1$ , the line  $x + y = i$  intersects  $T$  at one point  $(m, i -m)$ . For  $2m \le i \le m + n-1$ ,  $x + y = i$  intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points by the assumption on  $T'$ . For  $i = m+n$ ,  $x + y = i$  intersects  $T'$  at an odd number of points while also intersecting  $T$  at an additional  $m-1$  points (which is, in total, an odd number of intersections). Finally, for  $m+n \le i \le 2m + n -1$ ,  $x+ y = i$  intersects  $T$  only at  $(i - m, m)$ . As a result, every diagonal line intersects  $T$  at an odd number of points.


\end{enumerate}

