Let points on the vertical axis of symmetry of the grid be *free points*, and let the rows which contain such points  $P$  be *free rows*. Clearly, every other row is a free row. Additionally, changing the value of a free point  $P$  does not change the parity of any rows besides its own: either  $P$  is the center of the grid, so it has no rotations, or  $P$ 's rotations lie in the same row; in either case, only the parity of  $P$ 's row is changed when  $P$  and its rotations are changed.

Lemma.
The sum of the parities of all non-free rows must be even in a symmetric subset.

Proof.
This is equivalent to stating that changing the value of any point and its rotations does not change the total parity of non-free rows. From there, we can form any symmetric subset by choosing and changing points until we reach the desired subset.

Pick a free point  $P$ . Consider it and its rotations (if  $P$  is the center of the grid, count these rotations with multiplicity). Either all three points lie on a free row, or  $P$  lies on a free row while both its rotations lie on non-free rows. Every time  $P$  moves to an adjacent point, two of the rows of the points change while the last stays on the same row. Then, the number of points on non-free rows either increases by 2, decreases by 2, or stays the same, so its parity is constant and it is always even. 

We can move from the free point  $P$  to any point on the grid by moving to adjacent points. So, changing the value of any point does not change the sum of the parities of all non-free rows.

If a grid has an odd number of non-free rows, a solution must have the property that their total number of points must be odd; as this contradicts what we have just proven, such a grid must not have any valid solution. We can calculate the number of non-free rows as follows (by using the property that there are  $m + n - 1$  total rows, that free rows and non-free rows must alternate, and caseworking on parity):   $$ (\text{Number of non-free rows}) = \begin{cases}
\frac{m + n - 1}{2} & m \not\equiv n \pmod 2 
\frac{m + n}{2} - 1 & m \equiv n \equiv 1 \pmod 2 
\frac{m + n}{2} & m \equiv n \equiv 0 \pmod 2 
\end{cases} $$   

