\end{proof}
\begin{lemma}
    With same definitions as before, points  $P$  and  $Q$  are symmetric about plane  $ABC,$  and their midpoint is the orthocenter  $H$  of  $\triangle ABC.$  In a right triangle, this means  $P=Q=H.$ \end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    The first part of the statement is true since  $S_1 \cap S_2 \cap S_3$  is the intersection of the circle with diameter  $CF,$  and  $S_3.$  But these both are symmetric across plane  $ABC,$  so  $P$  and  $Q$  must also be symmetric across the plane. Let  $H$  be the midpoint of  $PQ.$  From symmetric, we know  $H$  lies on the plane. Furthermore, since  $P$  and  $Q$  lie on  $CF,$  we know  $H$  lies on  $C$ -altitude of  $\triangle ABC.$  From symmetry  $H$  also lies on the other two altitudes of  $\triangle ABC,$  and is thus the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC.$ \end{proof}
With these two lemmas, we continue with the solution of the original problem.

For sake of simplicity, work with  $AB$  rotated parallel to the  $y$ -axis (in the end, we can simply rotate back). Now  $C$  varies along  $\omega.$  Drop the altitude from  $C$  to  $AB$  and call it  $D.$  Instead of working with varying  $C,$  we vary the distance between  $D$  and  $M,$  denoted by  $b.$  Let  $\alpha=\angle ACB,$  and  $O$  be the center of  $\omega.$  Define  $H$  the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC.$  Observe  $CD=\sqrt{1-b^2}+\cos(\alpha),$  by Pythagorean Theorem, and  $\angle BOM = \angle BCA$  by Inscribed Angle Theorem. Furthermore,  $AH=2\cos(\alpha).$  Observe the intersection of spheres with diameter  $CA$  and  $CB$  is the circle with diameter  $CD.$  In particular, the center of this circle,  $X,$  is the intersection of lines  $CD$  and the line passing through the midpoint of  $CA, CB.$  Then  $XC=\frac{\sqrt{1-b^2}+\cos(\alpha)}{2}.$  By Power of a Point, 
\begin{align*}
(2\cos(\alpha))\left(\sqrt{1-b^2}+\cos(\alpha)-2\cos(\alpha)\right)&=2\cos(\alpha)\left(\sqrt{1-b^2}-\cos(\alpha)\right)
&= \operatorname{dist}(P,H)^2 
&= \operatorname{dist}(Q,H)^2,
\end{align*}
where  $P$  and  $Q$  are the two points in the intersection of the three spheres with diameters  $AB,BC,CA.$  Let  $P$  be the point with above the  $xy$ -plane, and  $Q$  be the point below. Now we find the coordinates of the orthocenter  $H,$  of  $\triangle ABC.$  Fortunately, from our initial rotation of  $AB,$  the  $x$  and  $y$  coordinates turn out nice. The  $x$ -coordinate becomes \[\cos(\alpha)-\left(\sqrt{1-b^2}+\cos(\alpha)-2\cos(\alpha)\right)=2\cos(\alpha)-\sqrt{1-b^2}.\] The  $y$ -coordinate is just distance  $MD,$  which is  $b.$  Then, the coordinates of  $P$  are \[(x,y,z)=\left(2\cos(\alpha)-\sqrt{1-b^2}, b, \sqrt{2\cos(\alpha)(\sqrt{1-b^2}-\cos(\alpha))}\right).\] Then, by substitution, the  $z$ -coordinate reduces to \[\sqrt{(x+\sqrt{1-y^2})(\sqrt{1-y^2})-\left(\frac{x+\sqrt{1-y^2}}{2}\right)}=\sqrt{\frac{1-x^2-y^2}{2}}.\] Thus,  $P$  traces out the surface defined by  $(x,y,\sqrt{\frac{1-x^2-y^2}{2}}),$  and from symmetry,  $Q$  traces out the surface defined by  $(x,y,-\sqrt{\frac{1-x^2-y^2}{2}}).$  Observe that both these surfaces are symmetric across the  $x$  and  $y$  axes since taking some combination of transformations  $x \to -x$  and  $y \to -y$  (none, one, or both), the  $z$ -coordinate remains the same due to the the  $x$  and  $y$  both being squared. Thus, regardless of the initial orientation of  $A,B,C,$  when we rotated to make  $AB$  parallel to the  $y$ -axis, rotating back doesn't change the locus of  $P$  or  $Q.$ Thus, all points  $P$  not on the  $xy$ -plane satisfying the given condition are defined by  $(x,y, \pm \sqrt{\frac{1-x^2-y^2}{2}}),$  as desired.

