solution_md:
(a): Fix the unit circle in the plane. Take the center of the circle and project all points of the initial polygon onto the circumference of the circle. Observe that as long as the resulting number of points on the circle is  $n \ge 3,$  then we are done, since there exists a working convex polygon on those vertices. Now we must tackle edge cases of when there are only  $1$  or  $2$  points on the circumference on the circle. In the former case, the preimage is a collinear set of points, which is not a concave set of points. For the latter case, the preimage must have all points lying on two seperate collinear set of points, but then the initial polygon must be convex. We have exhausted all cases, and thus such a function exists.
\bigbreak
(b): The answer is no. It suffices to prove that for some set of vertices, regardless of what points it gets mapped to, there's always a subset of them that is nonconcave. In particular, we will show that in a set of  $5$  points, there will always be a simple polygon on  $n \ge 4$  vertices that is nonconcave. The main claim is a well known theorem, which we present a rough sketch for:

\begin{theorem}
    (Happy Ending Theorem). In a set of  $5$  points in general position (that is, no three or more points are collinear), there exists a convex quadrilateral.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Casework depending on the number of vertices on the convex hull. If the convex hull has  $ \ge 4$  vertices, we are automatically done. Otherwise, we have a  $\triangle ABC$  as the convex hull and two points  $D$  and  $E$  in the interior. Then we may take  $DE$  and one of the sides of  $\triangle ABC.$  See \href{https://uu.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:1298048/FULLTEXT01.pdf}{here} for a visualization (on pages  $3$  and  $4$ ).
\end{proof}
It remains to check the cases of three, four, and five collinear points. For the case of three collinear points, take the three collinear points and another arbitrary vertex. Then we have a simple convex quadrilateral with three vertices collinear (so, a triangle). For the case of four and five collinear points, we can just take those collinear points and they form a line, which again is not a concave polygon. Thus, we have exhausted all cases and proved that in a set of  $5$  points, there will always be a simple polygon on  $n \ge 4$  vertices that is nonconcave, implying the nonexistence of such a function.

