   Label the  $i^{\text{th}}$  mathematician to fall asleep  $M_i$ . We will now assume for the sake of contradiction that at no point in time are  $6$  mathematicians all asleep, and demonstrate that such a condition ensures the existence of  $6$  mathematicians such that no two were asleep at the same time. This will prove the problems condition as,


- If there existed  $6$  mathematicians that fell asleep at the same time, the claim follows.
- Else if there did not exist  $6$  such mathematicians that were all asleep at the same time, by way of our below argument, we may find  $6$  mathematicians such that no two were asleep at the same time.
