- If  $i < j$ , then  $A_i$  wakes up before  $A_j$  falls asleep. This is because our claim provides that we have chosen mathematician  $A_i$  such that  $A_i$  awoke before the first mathematician in  $S_{i+1}$  falls asleep. As  $i < j$ , we have  $i + 1 \leq j$ , and hence the earliest time  $A_j$  may have fallen asleep is when the first mathematician in  $S_{i + 1}$  fell asleep. This proves that  $A_i$  woke up before  $A_j$  fell asleep.

        As a result for  $i \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 5\}$ , mathematician  $A_i$  fell asleep and woke up before mathematician  $A_{i + 1}$ . Thus the claim is proven, as it is impossible for two mathematicians from the set to have fallen asleep at the same time, as at any given time at most  $1$  mathematician is asleep at a time (as before the next mathematician falls asleep, a sleeping mathematician wakes up, and initially we begin with  $0$  mathematicians asleep).  $\square$

We seperate our solution into 4 parts:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We create a more general case
    \item We describe an algorithm that will solve our problem
    \item We prove that this algorithm works
    \item We provide an example to the algorithm
\end{enumerate}
<span style="font-size:200%">**<span style="color:#666">General Case</span>**</span>
Given  $n^2 + 1$  mathematicians, there are either  $n + 1$  mathematicians such that no two were asleep at the same time, or  $n + 1$  mathematicians such that there was some point in time during which all  $n + 1$  were asleep. (Assume  $n\in\mathbb N$ ).

Problem 4 is asking for a proof where  $n = 5.$  
<span style="font-size:200%">**<span style="color:#666">Algorithm</span>**</span>
If there exist  $n + 1$  that were asleep at the same time, then we are done. Thus, we only consider when at most  $n$  were asleep.

Create a set(unordered) of  $n^2 + 1$  pairs, each pair describing the interval when each mathematician was asleep. Then, order this set based on the time they woke up; if more than 1 have the same ending time, then sort based on the time they fell asleep. Let this new set be  $\{(s_1, e_1), (s_2, e_2), \ldots, (s_{n^2 + 1}, e_{n^2 + 1})\}$ (since we ordered it, the set has the requirement that  $e_i \leq e_j$  if and only if  $i\leq j$ .) 

We begin with the  $n + 1$  mathematicians such that their indices are  $1\pmod n,$  which are the intervals  $\{(s_{1}, e_1), (s_{n + 1}, e_{n + 1})$ ,  $(s_{2n + 1}, e_{2n + 1}), \ldots, (s_{n^2 + 1}, e_{n^2 + 1})\}$ . Here is the algorithm:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We begin at the first two intervals, and then we run through each one, applying the next few steps. Assume the pair of intervals we are working on right now are  $(s_i, e_i), (s_j, e_j).$     \item If  $e_i < s_j:$  
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Continue to the next pair
    \end{enumerate}
    \item If  $s_j \leq e_i:$  
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Sort the intervals in the range  $\{(s_{i + 1}, e_{i + 1}), \ldots, (s_{j}, e_{j})\}$  by the first term. 
        \item We replace  $(s_j, e_j)$  with the last element in this sorted set. 
        \item Continue to the next pair. 
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

Observe that if  $\{1, i_2, \ldots, i_n\}$  is our set of mathematicians that we pick, it must satisfy  $i_k \leq (k - 1) \cdot n + 1.$  This can be seen by induction and how we pick our elements.
<span style="font-size:200%">**<span style="color:#666">Proof this works</span>**</span>
Note that this algorithm {\bf{\emph{always ends after  $n$  moves}}}, since there are only  $n$  consecutive pairs of intervals to run through. Thus, all that we have to prove is that the resulting pairs will have an empty pairwise intersection. 

In the first case, we have that  $(s_i, e_i)\cap(s_j, e_j) = \phi,$  and hence we only need to focus on the second case.

Let the consecutive pairs that are already valid be  $(s_{i_1}, e_{i_1}), \ldots, (s_{i_k}, e_{i_k})$  and then we are replacing  $(s_{i_{k + 1}}, e_{i_{k + 1}})$  with  $(s_j, e_j).$  First, observe that  $i_{k + 1}\equiv1\pmod n$  since with each step of the algorithm, we only change the second pair, which can't affect this one because we haven't reached this step of the algorithm before. Also notice that we must have that  $i_{k}\leq (k - 1)\cdot n + 1 = i_{k + 1} - n$ . Thus, there exist at least  $n + 1$  elements in the set  $\{i_k, i_k + 1, \ldots, i_{k + 1}\}$  since  $i_{k + 1} - i_k + 1 \geq n + 1.$  

Now, for the sake of contradiction, assume that the chosen interval,  $(s_j, e_j),$  intersects  $(s_{i_{k}}, e_{i_k}).$  Hence, we have that  $s_j \leq e_{i_k}.$  However, by our given assumption of the set being sorted by beginning order, we have that all of the other start elements are less than or equal to  $s_j.$  We also know that all of the elements have a time ending after  $e_{i_k}.$  Hence, there exist  $n + 1$  elements that contain the interval  $[s_j, e_{i_k}],$  which is a contradiction of the fact that at most  $n$  mathematicians were asleep at the same time. 

Hence, we can conclude that  $e_{i_k} < s_{j}.$  Also, since  $j$  is chosen from the range  $(i_k, i_{k + 1}),$  we can conclude that  $s_j \leq e_{i_{j + 1}},$  and so we have no concerns about it affecting the rest of the set any more than the original element. We are also selecting replacements to have indices less than or equal to the original, and hence we have no worries about there not being enough elements. 

