We let  $P(x)=\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_nx^n$  so that  $P(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})=\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_n(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^n$  where  $p_n$  is an integer for all  $n\in\mathbb{N}_0$ . We see that  $$ (\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^0=1+0\sqrt[3]{5}+0\sqrt[3]{25}, $$    $$ (\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^1=0+1\sqrt[3]{5}+1\sqrt[3]{25}, $$    $$ (\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^2=10+5\sqrt[3]{5}+1\sqrt[3]{25}, $$    $$ (\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^3=30+15\sqrt[3]{5}+15\sqrt[3]{25}, $$    $$ (\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^4=150+105\sqrt[3]{5}+45\sqrt[3]{25}. $$  We claim that if  $(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^k=a_k+b_k\sqrt[3]{5}+c_k\sqrt[3]{25}$  for  $k,a_k,b_k,c_k\in\mathbb{N}_0$ , then  $b_k\equiv c_k\pmod{2}$ . As shown above, this is true for  $k=0,1,2,3,4$ . Assume that it is true for some  $N\ge 0$ , and  $(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^N=a_N+b_N\sqrt[3]{5}+c_N\sqrt[3]{25}$  where  $b_N\equiv c_N\pmod{2}$ . Then, 
\begin{align*}
(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^{N+1}&=(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})^N&=(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})(a_N+b_N\sqrt[3]{5}+c_N\sqrt[3]{25})&=(5b_N+5c_N)+(a_N+5c_N)\sqrt[3]{5}+(a_N+b_N)\sqrt[3]{25}.
\end{align*}
Since  $b_N\equiv c_N\pmod{2}$ ,  $b_{N+1}=a_N+5c_N\equiv a_N+b_N=c_{N+1}\pmod{2}$ . Therefore, by induction, our claim is true.

We see that   $$ P(\sqrt[3]{5}+\sqrt[3]{25})=(\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_na_n)+(\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_nb_n)\sqrt[3]{5}+(\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_nc_n)\sqrt[3]{25}. $$  Since all  $a_k,b_k,c_k,p_k\in\mathbb{Z}$  for all  $k\ge 0$ , we must have that  $\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_nb_n=2$  and  $\sum_{n=0}^\infty p_nc_n=3$ . Since  $b_k\equiv c_k\pmod{2}$  for all  $k\ge 0$ , this is not possible, as desired.

Such a skibidi problem
--------
    Consider  $S = \left\{x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n \right\}$ , and let  $\psi(T)$  denote the indexed alternating sum of a subset  $T$  of  $S$ . $\textbf{Claim:}$  The set  $S$  has  $2^n$  subsets.
        $ \textit{Proof.}$  In any given subset we may choose to include or exclude  $x_i$ , for each  $i$  from  $1$  to  $n$ . Hence there are  $2^n$  possible subsets.  $\blacksquare$     Denote by  $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_{2^n}$  the subsets of  $S$ . We next present a lemma used later. $        \textbf{Claim:}$  We have that,
        \begin{align*}
            \sum_{j = 0}^{i} (-1)^j (j + 1) \binom{i}{j} = \begin{cases}
                1 & \text{if } i = 0 
    -1 & \text{if } i = 1 
    0 & \text{if } i > 1
            \end{cases}
        \end{align*} $\textit{Proof.}$         We first prove that for  $i > 1$  the sum evaluates to  $0$ . To do write,
        \begin{align*}
            \sum_{j = 0}^{i} (-1)^j (j + 1) \binom{i}{j} &=  \sum_{j = 0}^{i} (-1)^j \binom{i}{j} + \sum_{j = 0}^{i} (-1)^j j \binom{i}{j} 
        \end{align*}
        We will demonstrate each term on the right hand side is  $0$ . To demonstrate the first term is  $0$ , note that by the binomial theorem,
        \begin{align*}
            0 = (1 - 1)^i &= \sum_{j = 0}^i (-1)^{j} \binom{i}{j}
        \end{align*}
        for all  $i$  greater than  $1$ . To demonstrate the second term is  $0$ , observe that from the binomial theorem,
        \begin{align*}
            (1 - x)^i = \sum_{j = 0}^{i} (-1)^j \binom{i}{j} x^j
        \end{align*}
        Taking the derivative of this expression for  $i > 1$  yields,
        \begin{align*}
            -i(1 - x)^{i - 1} &= \sum_{j = 1}^{i} (-1)^j j \binom{i}{j} x^{j - 1} .
        \end{align*}
        Setting  $x = 1$ , we find  $i(1 - x)^{i - 1} = 0$  for  $i > 1$  so that we find the identity,
        \begin{align*}
            0 &= \sum_{j = 1}^{i} (-1)^j j \binom{i}{j} = \sum_{j = 0}^{i} (-1)^j j \binom{i}{j},
        \end{align*}
        which proves that each term is  $0$ . Hence for  $i > 1$  the expression evaluates to  $0$ . 

        Next we ensure that the claim holds for  $i = 0$ , and  $i = 1$ . To do this we expand the respective expressions,
        \begin{align*}
            \sum_{j = 0}^{0} (-1)^j (j + 1) \binom{i}{j} &= \left[(-1)^0 \binom{0}{0} \right] = 1 
            \sum_{j = 0}^{1} (-1)^j (j + 1) \binom{i}{j} &= \left[ (-1)^0 \binom{1}{0} \right] + \left[ (-1)^1  \cdot 2 \cdot \binom{1}{1}\right] = 1 - 2 = -1,
        \end{align*}
        proving the claim for  $i = 0$  and  $i = 1$ .  $\blacksquare$     Now we present the general idea behind our solution. $         \textbf{Claim:}$  The expected value of the indexed alternating sum of a randomly chosen subset  $T$  of  $S$  is,
        \begin{align*}
            \frac{1}{2^n} \sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi\left(a_i\right).
        \end{align*} $\textit{Proof.}$         To find the expected value of the indexed alternating sum for a \textit{randomly} chosen subset of  $S$ , it suffices to consider the probability a specific subset  $a_i$  is chosen, and multiply that by its indexed alternating sum. Hence the expected value is,
        \begin{align*}
         \sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \frac{1}{2^n} \psi\left(a_i\right) = \frac{1}{2^n} \sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi\left(a_i\right).
        \end{align*} $\blacksquare$ .

    Therefore it suffices to compute the sum  $\sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi \left(a_i \right)$ .
         $\textbf{Claim:}$  The sum evaluates as,
        \begin{align*}
            \sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi \left(a_i \right) = 2^{n - 1} x_1 - 2^{n - 2} x_2.
        \end{align*} $\textit{Proof.}$         Note that for some constants  $c_1, c_2, \dots, c_n$  we may write,
        \begin{align*}
            \sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi\left(a_n \right) = \sum_{i = 1}^n c_ix_i
        \end{align*}
        from the definition of the indexed alternating sum  $\psi$ . We explicitly find each  $c_i$  by considering the contribution of a specific element  $x_i$  to the sum.

         We present the following subclaim:
             $\textbf{Subclaim:}$  There are exactly  $\binom{i - 1}{m - 1} \cdot 2^{n - i}$  such subsets of  $S$ ,  $a_j$ , such that,


- In the indexed alternating sum  $\psi \left( a_j \right)$  the coefficient of  $x_i$  is  $(-1)^{m -1} \cdot m$ , with  $1 \leq m \leq i $ .
 $\textit{Proof.}$             Note for  $x_i$  to have a coefficient of  $(-1)^{m-1} m$  in  $\psi \left(a_j \right)$ , there must be exactly  $m - 1$  elements smaller than  $x_i$  in  $a_j$ , chosen from  $\{x_1, x_2, \dots, x_{i - 1} \}$ . We may choose these  $m - 1$  elements in  $\binom{i - 1}{m - 1}$  ways. 

      Additionally, the inclusion or exclusion of elements larger than  $x_i$  in  $a_j$ , namely  $\{ x_{i + 1}, x_{i + 2}, \dots, x_n \}$  does not affect the coefficient of  $x_i$  in  $\psi(a_j)$ . Hence we may choose which elements larger than  $x_i$  in  $2^{n - i}$  ways. 

             Thus the total number of such sets  $a_j$ , in which the coefficient of  $x_i$  in  $\psi(a_j)$  is  $(-1)^{m - 1} \cdot m$ , is given by  $2^{n - i} \cdot \binom{i - 1}{m - 1}$  as claimed.  $\blacksquare$         Therefore applying the claim to  $m \in \{1, 2, \dots, i\}$  we find the coefficient of  $x_i$  in the sum  $\sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi(a_n)$  is,
        \begin{align*}
            c_i = \sum_{m = 1}^{i} \left[ (-1)^{m - 1} \cdot m \right] \left[2^{n - i} \binom{i - 1}{m - 1} \right] &= 2^{n - i} \left( \sum_{m = 1}^{i} (-1)^{m - 1}m \binom{i - 1}{m - 1} \right) 
            &= 2^{n - i} \left( \sum_{m = 0}^{i - 1} (-1)^{m} (m + 1) \binom{i - 1}{m} \right) 
        \end{align*}
        From the lemma, for  $i - 1 > 1$ , or equivalently for  $i > 2$ , we have that the sum inside the brackets is  $0$ , and hence the expression is  $0$ . Thus the coefficients  $c_i$  of  $x_i$  in the sum  $\sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi(a_n)$ , for  $i > 2$  are  $0$ . 

         For  $i - 1 = 1$  and  $i - 1 = 0$  the lemma yields respectively,
        \begin{align*}
            c_2 = 2^{n - 2} \left( \sum_{m = 0}^{1} (-1)^{m} (m + 1) \binom{i - 1}{m} \right) &= -2^{n - 2}
            c_1 = 2^{n - 1} \left( \sum_{m = 0}^{0} (-1)^{m} (m + 1) \binom{i - 1}{m} \right) &= 2^{n - 1},
        \end{align*}
        and hence the coefficients  $c_1$  and  $c_2$  of  $x_1$  and  $x_2$  are respectively  $2^{n - 1}$  and  $-2^{n - 2}$ . Hence we have,
        \begin{align*}
            \sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi \left(a_i \right) = 2^{n - 1}x_1 - 2^{n - 2}x_2.
        \end{align*}  $\blacksquare$ .

     Finally from our third claim this implies that the expected value of  $\psi \left( a_i \right)$ , of a randomly chosen subset  $a_i$  of  $S$  is,
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{2^n} \sum_{i = 1}^{2^n} \psi \left(a_i \right) = \frac{2^{n - 1} x_1 - 2^{n - 2} x_2}{2^n} = \frac{2x_1 - x_2}{4}.
    \end{align*}
    Thus our answer is the expected value of the indexed alternating sum is,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[\psi(a_i)] = \frac{2x_1 - x_2}{4}.
    \end{align*}

