Notice that if  $\overline{AB}\perp\overline{CE}$ ,  $\overline{HPEC}$  collinear since  $H$  is the foot of the altitude from  $C$  since  $\triangle ABC$  is equilateral and  $H$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AB}$ , and  $\overline{HP}\perp\overline{AB}$ . Since  $\measuredangle AHP = \measuredangle AFP = 90^{\circ}$ ,  $AHPF$  is a cyclic quadrilateral, and  $\angle QPE = 60^{\circ}$ .

Notice that  $\angle BPD=120^{\circ}$  since  $\angle BAD = 60^{\circ}$  and  $P$  is the circumcenter of  $ABD$ . Thus, since  $\triangle BPD$  is isosceles (since  $P$  is the circumcenter) as shown on the diagram,  $\angle PBD = 30^{\circ}$ .

Let  $\theta = \angle HBP$ .  $\triangle AHP \cong \triangle BHP$  by  $HL$ , so  $\theta = \angle HBP = \angle HAP$ .  $\angle ABD = 30^{\circ}+\theta$  (works also when  $P$  is outside  $\triangle ABC$ ) and  $\angle BAD = 60^{\circ}$  so looking at  $\triangle ABD$ ,  $\angle ADB = 90^{\circ}-\theta$ . Looking at quadrilateral  $AHED$ ,  $\angle HED = 360^{\circ}-60^{\circ}-90^{\circ}-90^{\circ}+\theta = 120^{\circ}+\theta$ .  $\angle PAD = 60^{\circ}-\theta$ , and since  $\angle PAD$  and  $\angle DEP$  are supplementary,  $APED$  is cyclic. $Q$  is the circumcenter of  $APED$  since  $Q$  is the circumcenter of  $AED$ . This means  $\overline{PQ} = \overline{EQ}$ .

We see that  $\measuredangle{AHP} = \measuredangle{AFP} = 90^{\circ}$ , so  $AHPF$  is cyclic. Since  $\angle HAF = 60^{\circ}$ , and  $\overline{HPE}$  collinear,  $\angle EPQ = \angle HAF = 60^{\circ}$ . $\overline{PQ} = \overline{EQ}$  as we saw before, so  $\triangle PQE$  is isosceles, and since  $\angle EPQ = 60^{\circ}$ , this implies  $\angle PEQ = 60^{\circ}$ . Since all angles of a triangle add to  $180^{\circ}$ , we can conclude all angles of  $\triangle EPQ$  are  $60^{\circ}$ , and thus  $\triangle EPQ$  is equilateral.  $\blacksquare$ Thus,  $\triangle EPQ$  equilateral  $\iff \overline{AB}\perp\overline{CE}$ .</details>

