{\bf{Claim}}: { $PDBC$  is cyclic.}

{\it Proof:} We angle chase
\begin{align*}
    \angle BAD & = 60 \text{ by fact 1}
    \implies \angle BPD & = 2\angle BAD \text{ since } \angle BAD \text{ is an inscribed angle} 
    & = 120
\end{align*}
but since  $\angle DCB = 60,$  we have that  $PDBC$  is cyclic . \qed

{\bf{Claim}}: { $\angle PBD = 30$ }

{\it Proof:} Since  $P$  is the circumcenter of  $ABD,$  we must have that  $PB = PD.$  Thus, by fact 6, we have that  $P$  is the midpoint of arc  $BD$  in the circle  $(BPDC).$  Thus,  $\angle PBD = \angle PCD = \frac12\angle ACB = 30$  where the first equality is by fact 5, the second equality is true because  $P$  lies on the angle bisector of  $\angle ACB,$  and the final equality is true because of fact 1. \qed

{\bf{Lemma}}:  $PADE$  is cyclic if and only if  $\overline{CE}\perp\overline{AB}$ {\it Proof:}  Let  $\angle BDC = \alpha.$  First, some angle chase tells us that 
\begin{align*}
    & CE \perp AB 
    \iff & E \in \overline{CP} \text{ since }CP \perp AB
    \iff & \angle ECA = 30 \text{ by fact 3 } 
    \iff & \angle DEC = 150  -\alpha \text{ by fact 2 } 
    \iff & \angle DEP = 30 + \alpha \text{ by second line and collinear implies angle sum is 180} 
\end{align*}
We do casework on if  $P$  is inside  $\triangle ABC$  or not. We will angle chase in both solutions. 
