Note that  $ABCD$  is cyclic as  $\angle A + \angle C = 180$ . Define  $Q$  to be the intersection of  $\overline{AC}$  and  $\overline{BD}$ . We prove each direction of the problem separately but first provide a claim. $\textbf{Claim:}$  If the point  $P$  satisfying  $\angle BAP = \angle CAD$  and  $\angle BCP = \angle ACD$  exists for a given  $ABCD$  and lies strictly in  $\triangle ABC$ , then it is unique.   $\textit{Proof.}$  Define  $\ell_a$  to be the reflection of  $\overline{AC}$  about the angle bisector of acute  $\angle BAD$ , which we will denote  $j_a$ . Similarly define  $\ell_c$  to be the reflection of  $\overline{AC}$  about the angle bisector of obtuse  $\angle BCD$ , which we will denote  $j_c$ . Note that  $\ell_a$  passes through  $A$  and  $\ell_c$  passes through  $C$ . Moreover it follows that  $j_a$  bisects  $\angle(\ell_a, \overline{AC})$ , and  $j_c$  bisects  $\angle(j_a, \overline{AC})$  as from our definitions,
    \begin{align*}
        \angle(\overline{AC}, j_a) &= \angle(j_a, \ell_a)
        \angle(\overline{AC}, j_c) &= \angle(j_c, \ell_c).
    \end{align*}

 Now, note if a point  $P_a$  satisfies  $\angle BAP_a = \angle CAD$ , then it must lie on  $\ell_a$  by definition. Similarly if a point  $P_c$  satisfies  $\angle BCP_c = \angle ACD$  then it must lie on  $\ell_c$ . In particular, then  $P$  is the intersection point of  $\ell_a$  and  $\ell_c$ . This intersection point is unique if and only if  $\ell_a$  and  $\ell_c$  are not the same line. 

However if  $\ell_a$  and  $\ell_c$  were the same line, then the angle bisectors of  $\angle(\ell_a, \overline{AC})$  and  $\angle(\ell_c, \overline{AC})$  must be the same. However we previously showed that these lines are exactly  $j_a$  and  $j_c$  respectively. Hence this would imply the angle bisectors of  $\angle BAD$  and  $\angle BCD$  are the same, or more specifically that  $j_a = j_c = \overline{AC}$ . Then from our definitions we would find  $\ell_a = \ell_c = \overline{AC}$ , which would mean  $P$ , which lies on the common line  $\ell_a = \ell_c$ , would lie on  $\overline{AC}$ . This would contradict the fact that  $P$  lies inside  $\triangle ABC$ , proving the claim.  $\square$ We proceed by proving the first direction, namely  $\overline{AC} \perp \overline{BD}$  implies  $\overline{PB} \perp \overline{PD}$ .  $\textbf{Claim:}$  The point  $P$  is the circumcenter of the circumcircle of  $ABCD$ .  $\textit{Proof.}$     Let  $P'$  denote the circumcenter of  $(ABCD)$ . We will show that  $P'$  satisfies  $\angle BAP' = \angle CAD$  and  $\angle BCP' = \angle ACD$ , which from the uniqueness of  $P$  would imply  $P' \equiv P$ , or that  $P$  is the circumcenter of cyclic  $ABCD$ .

 Indeed from angle chasing by repeatedly using the inscribed angle theorem we find,
    \begin{align*}
        \angle P'AB = 90 - 1/2 \angle AP'B = 90 - \angle ACB &= 90 - \angle QCB
        &= \angle CBQ
        &= \angle CBD
        &= \angle CAD,
    \end{align*}
    where we use the fact that  $\angle CQB = 90$  as  $\overline{AC} \perp \overline{BD}$  to go from the first to the second line. Similarly we may find,
    \begin{align*}        
    \angle BCP' = 90 - 1/2 \angle BP'C = 90 - \angle BAC &= 90 - \angle BAQ
        &= \angle QBA
        &= \angle DBA
        &= \angle DCA.         
    \end{align*}
    Hence  $P'$  satisfies all the properties of  $P$ , so we must in fact have  $P \equiv P'$ , proving the claim.  $\square$ Now observe that the implication follows as from the inscribed angle theorem we find,
\begin{align*}
    \angle BPD = 2 \angle BAD = 2 \cdot 45 = 90, 
\end{align*}
which means  $\overline{PB} \perp \overline{PD}$  as desired. 

We now prove the other implication, namely  $\overline{PB} \perp \overline{PD}$  implies that  $\overline{AC} \perp \overline{BD}$ . To do so we use complex numbers as outlined in EGMO by Evan Chen.

Scale and rotate  $ABCD$  such that its circumcircle is the unit circle in the complex plane. Note that if  $O$  is the center of  $ABCD$  then,
\begin{align*}
    \angle BOD = 2 \angle BAD = 90,
\end{align*}
and so we may let  $O = 0$ ,  $B = b = 1$  and  $D = d = i$ . Furthermore let  $A = a$ ,  $C = c$ , for complex numbers  $a$  and  $c$  satisfying  $|a| = |c| = 1$  and let  $P = p$ . We now make two preliminary claims. 

 The first claim is used to deal with the conjugates of complex numbers  $a$  and  $c$ .
     $\textbf{Claim:}$  The conjugates of  $a$  and  $c$  are  $\overline{a} = \frac{1}{a}$  and  $\overline{c} = \frac{1}{c}$  respectively.  $\textit{Proof.}$  This is the statement of proposition 6.9 in EGMO.  $\square$  The second claim is used to identify  $p$  in the complex plane.  $    \textbf{Claim:}$  The complex number  $p$  satisfies the following three equations:


- $\overline{p} = \frac{p(1 - i)}{2p - 1 - i}$
- $a\overline{p} - 1 = cpi - aci$
- $c\overline{p} - 1 = api - aci$
 $\textit{Proof.}$     We prove each statement one at a time. For the first statement recall that  $\overline{PB} \perp \overline{PD}$ . This implies that,
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{b - p}{d - p} \in i \mathbb{R} \iff -\overline{\left(\frac{1 - p}{i - p} \right)} &= \frac{1 - p}{i - p}
        \iff \frac{1 - \overline{p}}{i + \overline{p}} &= \frac{1 - p}{i - p}
        \iff (1 - \overline{p})(i - p) &= (1 - p)(i + \overline{p})
        \iff -i\overline{p} - p + p\overline{p} &= -ip + \overline{p} - p\overline{p}
        \iff \overline{p} &= \frac{p(1 - i)}{2p - 1 - i},
    \end{align*}
    as claimed. For the second statement recall that  $\angle BAP = \angle CAD = \angle CBD$  from inscribed arcs in cyclic  $ABCD$ . This implies that,
    \begin{align*}
        \arg\left(\frac{b -a}{p - a} \right) = \arg\left(\frac{c - b}{d - b} \right) \iff \frac{b - a}{p - a} \div \frac{c - b}{d - b} \in \mathbb{R} \iff \overline{\left( \frac{(1 - a)(i - 1)}{(p - a)(c - 1)} \right)} &= \frac{(1 - a)(i - 1)}{(p - a)(c - 1)}
        \iff \frac{(1 - 1/a)(-i - 1)}{(\overline{p} - 1/a)(1/c - 1)} &= \frac{(1 - a)(i - 1)}{(p - a)(c - 1)}
        \frac{c(a - 1)(-i - 1)}{(a\overline{p} - 1)(1 - c)} &= \frac{(1 - a)(i - 1)}{(p - a)(c - 1)}
        \frac{- i - 1}{i - 1} &= \frac{a\overline{p} - 1}{cp - ca}
        \iff i &= \frac{a\overline{p} - 1}{cp - ca}
        \iff a\overline{p} - 1 &= cpi - aci,
    \end{align*}
    as claimed. Note we may divide by  $a - 1$  and  $c - 1$ , as these quantities are never  $0$  as that would imply either  $A$  and  $B$ , or  $B$  and  $C$  are the same point. Finally the third statement follows from  $\angle BCP = \angle ACD = \angle ABD$  which implies that,
    \begin{align*}
         \arg\left(\frac{b -c}{p - c} \right) = \arg\left(\frac{a - b}{d - b} \right).
    \end{align*}
    As this statement is simply that of statement  $2$  with  $a$  and  $c$  swapped, we find that this reduces to,
    \begin{align*}
        c\overline{p} - 1 = api - aci
    \end{align*}
    as desired. Hence the claim follows.  $\square$ We now use these characterizations of  $p$  to prove the following claim. $\textbf{Claim:}$   $P$  is the circumcenter of  $ABCD$ . In terms of complex numbers this implies that  $p = 0$ .  $\textit{Proof.}$     Note that  $p$  satisfies,
    \begin{align*}
        a\overline{p} - 1 &= cpi - aci
        c\overline{p} - 1 &= api - aci.
    \end{align*}
    Subtracting the two we find that,
    \begin{align*}
        (a - c)\overline{p} &= pi(c - a)
        \overline{p} &= -pi,
    \end{align*}
    as  $a \neq c$  because they lie in separate arcs of the unit circle. However we also have,
    \begin{align*}
        \overline{p} = \frac{p(1 - i)}{2p - 1 - i},
    \end{align*}
    and hence equating the two we find that,
    \begin{align*}
        -pi &= \frac{p(1 - i)}{2p - 1 - i}
        \iff -pi(2p - 1 - i) &= p(1 - i)
        \iff -2p^2i + pi - p &= p - pi
        \iff 2p^2i - 2pi + 2p &= 0
        \iff p(2pi - 2i + 2) &= 0
    \end{align*}
    which implies either  $p = 0$  or  $p = 1 + i$ . Clearly the latter fails as it would imply that  $p$  lies outside the unit circle and hence outside  $ABCD$ . Thus  $p = 0$  as desired, which satisfies the equations as well.  $\square$ Finally we make use of Example 6.10 in EGMO to conclude that to show  $\overline{AC} \perp \overline{BD}$  it suffices to show  $ac + i = 0$ . Indeed plugging in  $p = 0$  into either of the equations,
\begin{align*}
    a\overline{p} - 1 &= cpi - aci
    c\overline{p} - 1 &= api - aci,
\end{align*}
yields that,
\begin{align*}
    -1 &= -aci \iff -aci + 1 = 0 \iff ac + i = 0,
\end{align*}
as claimed.

If  $X, Y, Z\in(ABC),$  then we define   $\widehat{XZY}$  as the angle of the arc  $XY$  in the circumcircle of  $ABC$ , passing through  $Z.$  

Note that by the  $45, 135$  angle condition, we see that  $ABCD$  is cyclic, with  $\angle BOD = 90$  where  $O$  is the circumcenter. 

Draw the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{BD}.$  Label this as  $\ell.$   

Let  $A', C'$  be the reflection of  $A, C$  over  $\ell$ . Since  $O\in\ell$ , we know that  $A', C'$  lie on the circle. Note that it is possible for  $A = A'$  and  $C = C'.$ I claim that  $P \in \overline{A' C},$  and  $P \in \overline{AC'}.$  

We prove our claim with some simple angle chasing:
