<span style="color:#00f">**Proof:**</span>
    \begin{align*}
        \angle BAC' & = \frac{\widehat{BC'} }{2} \text{ from inscribed angle theorem}
        & = \frac{\widehat{CD}}{2} \text{ from definition of }C' 
        & = \angle CAD \text{ from inscribed angle theorem}
        & = \angle BAP \text{ from given}
    \end{align*}
    Thus,  $P\in \overline{AC'}.$  

    Similarly, 
    \begin{align*}
        \angle BCA' & = \frac{\widehat{BA'} }{2} \text{ from inscribed angle theorem}
        & = \frac{\widehat{AD}}{2} \text{ from definition of }A' 
        & = \angle ACD \text{ from inscribed angle theorem}
        & = \angle BCP \text{ from given}
    \end{align*}
    Hence,  $P\in\overline{A'C}.$  
Hence, we see that  $P = \overline{AC'}\cap \overline{A'C}$  other than a few cases. First, note that  $A,C$  both have to be on the same side of  $\ell,$  or else  $A, C'$  lie on the same side of  $\ell,$  resulting in  $P$  being outside of  $\triangle ABC.$  Now, we handle thse cases:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item if  $\overline{AC'} = \overline{A'C},$  then  $A = A', C = C',$  so then  $P \in \overline{AC},$  which contradicts the fact that  $P$  lies strictly inside  $\triangle ABC.$  
    \item if  $\overline{AC'} \parallel \overline{A'C},$  then  $A, C$  forms a diameter, which is clearly impossible since they need to be on the same side of  $\ell$ .
    \item also note that if  $A = A'$  or  $C = C',$  then the point  $P$  will be either  $A$  or  $C,$  which contradicts the fact that they lie strictly inside  $\triangle ABC$ .
\end{enumerate}

Note that  $AA'C'C$  is an isosceles trapezoid that is symmetric about  $\ell.$  Hence, we have that  $P\in\ell.$  

Now, notice that  $\angle BOD = 2\angle BAD = 90$  by inscribed angle theorem. Since  $P$  is varying along  $\ell$  within  $\triangle ABC$ , we have that  $\angle BPD = 90$  if and only if  $P = O$ (the only other point lies outside the circle, and thus, outside  $\triangle ABC$ ). 

However,  $P = O$  occurs if and only if  $O\in \overline{AC'}.$  This occurs if and only if  $O$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AC'}$  and  $\overline{A'C}.$  This occurs if and only if  $ACC'A'$  is a rectangle. However, since  $\overline{CC'} || \overline{BD},$  we have that  $\overline{AC}\perp \overline{BD}$  occurs if and only if  $\angle BPD = 90.$  
