Here's <details><summary>My Solution.</summary>The only solutions are   $$ (a, b, c)=\boxed{(1, 0, 0), (2, 0, 1), (1, 1, 2)}, $$  which we'll prove by using modular arithmetic and considering various cases on  $b$ . 

First, suppose  $b=0$ , so the equation becomes   $$ 2^a-3^c=1\iff 2^a=3^c+1. $$  If  $a=0$ , then there are no solutions for  $c$ . If  $a=1$ , then we have  $c=0$  and the solution  $(a, b, c)=(1, 0, 0)$ . If  $a=2$ , then we have  $c=1$ , giving  $(a, b, c)=(2, 0, 1)$ . Now suppose  $c\geq 3$ , so  $2^3=8$  must divide  $3^c+1$  and   $$ 3^c+1\equiv 0\pmod 8\iff 3^c\equiv -1\pmod 8. $$  Since since the next power of  $3$  is always  $3$  times the previous and  $3^2=9\equiv 1\pmod 8,$  the powers of  $3$  alternate between  $3^0=1$  and  $3^1=3$  in modulo  $8$ . Hence, no power of  $3$  is ever  $-1\pmod 8$ , so no such  $c$  exist and there are no solutions when  $b=0$  and  $a\geq 3$ . Thus, we've found all solutions when  $b=0$ . 

Now suppose  $b\geq 1$ . Rearranging to get  $2^a\cdot 5^b=3^c+1,$   we must have that  $5$  divides  $3^c+1$ , or  $3^c\equiv -1\pmod 5$ . It is well known that if  $k$  and  $n$  are relatively prime, then  $k^i\equiv k^j\pmod n$  if and only if  $i\equiv j\pmod {\text{ord}_nk}.$  (This fact is also Theorem 8.2 from Elementary Number Theory by David M. Burton). Considering the powers of  $3$  modulo  $5$ , we have 
\begin{align*}
    3^1\equiv& 3\pmod 5
    3^2\equiv& 4\equiv -1\pmod 5
    3^3\equiv& 2\pmod 5
    3^4\equiv& 1\pmod 5
\end{align*}

Hence  $3$  has order  $4$ , so   $$ 3^c\equiv -1\equiv 3^2\pmod 5 $$  if and only if  $c\equiv 2\pmod 4$ . In particular,  $c$  is even, so  $c=2\hat c$  for some nonnegative integer  $\hat c$ . Then our original equation becomes  $$ 2^a\cdot 5^b=3^{2\hat c}+1=9^{\hat c}+1. $$  Taking this modulo  $4$ , we get  $$ 2^a\cdot 5^b\equiv 9^{\hat c}+1\equiv1^{\hat c}+1\equiv 2\pmod 4. $$  Hence, the LHS is even but not a multiple of  $4$ , so  $a=1$  and our equation becomes  $$ 2\cdot 5^b=9^{\hat c}+1. $$  If  $b=1$ , then we get  $\hat c=1$ , so  $c=2$ , giving  $(1, 1, 2).$  Otherwise, since we are in the  $b\geq 1$  case, we must have  $b\geq 2$ . Thus  $5^2=25$  must divide  $9^{\hat c}+1$ , so  $9^{\hat c}\equiv -1\pmod {25}.$  We again list the powers of  $9\pmod {25}$ :
\begin{align*}
    9^1\equiv& 9\pmod {25}
    9^2\equiv& 6\pmod{25}
    9^3\equiv& 4\pmod{25}
    9^4\equiv& 11\pmod{25}
    9^5\equiv& -1\pmod{25}
    9^6\equiv& -9\pmod {25}
    9^7\equiv& -6\pmod{25}
    9^8\equiv& -4\pmod{25}
    9^9\equiv& -11\pmod{25}
    9^{10}\equiv& 1\pmod{25}
\end{align*}
In particular, the order of  $9$  modulo  $25$  is  $10$ , and  $9^5\equiv -1\pmod {25}$ . Thus  $$ 9^{\hat c}\equiv -1\equiv 9^5\pmod {25}, $$  which is true if and only if  $\hat c\equiv 5\pmod {10}.$  Therefore,  $\hat c=10\bar c+5$  where  $\bar c$  is a nonnegative integer. Then our equation becomes  $$ 2\cdot 5^b=9^{\hat c}+1=9^{10\bar c+5}+1=\left(9^5\right)^{2\bar c+1}+1^{2\bar c+1}. $$  Before continuing, we'll prove a useful property. Suppose  $p$  and  $q$  are integers and  $n$  is an odd integer, then  $p+q\equiv 0\pmod{p+q}$  so  $p\equiv -q\pmod{p+q}.$  Raising both sides to the odd power of  $n$ , we get that  $p^n\equiv q^n(-1)^n\equiv -q^n\pmod {p+q},$  so  $p^n+q^n\equiv 0\pmod {p+q}$  and  $(p+q)\mid \left (p^n+q^n\right).$ From our equation above, clearly  $2\bar c+1$  is odd, so this implies that   $$ \left(9^5+1\right)\mid \left(\left(9^5\right)^{2\bar c+1}+1^{2\bar c+1}\right)=\left(2\cdot 5^b\right) $$  However,   $$ 9^5+1=59050=2\cdot 5^2\cdot 1181, $$  so this means that  $1181$  must divide  $2\cdot 5^b$ . Since neither  $2$  nor  $5$  divide  $1181$ , there must be prime  $p$  other than  $2$  or  $5$  that divides  $1181$ , and thus  $p\mid 2\cdot 5^b$ . This, however, is impossible since  $p\not\in\{2, 5\},$  proving that there are no solutions when  $b\geq 2$ . This completes the last sub-case of the last case in our solution. Therefore, we've found all solutions to the equations and proved that no others exist. $_\square$</details>

I thought this problem was pretty nice, especially finishing it off with  $a+b\mid a^n+b^n$  when  $n$  is odd. I think this was my second solve after P3 because it looked the most interesting. Also my shortest P5 writeup to date!

