My problem!

<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $n=505$  and let the polygon be  $P_1P_2\dots P_{4n+2}$ . WLOG, assume that  $A\in P_1P_{4n+2}$ . By symmetry, we can assume that  $B$  is closer to  $P_1$  than to  $P_{4n+2}$ . Then, since the chance that  $B$  is between  $P_{4n+2}$  and  $P_n$  is equal to the chance that  $B$  is between  $P_{n+2}$  and  $P_{2n+2}$ , and furthermore because  $AB$  is always smaller in the former case than in the latter, we may assume that  $B\in P_nP_{n+1}\cup P_{n+1}P_{n+2}$ .

[asy]
unitsize(1inch); int n=4; pair P(int i){return dir(-180*(i+n)/(2*n+1));}
for(int i=1;i<=4*n+2;++i){draw(P(i-1)--P(i),grey);}
pen p1=royalblue,p2=orange;
dot(" $P_1$ ",P(1),P(1),p1);
dot(" $P_{4n+2}$ ",P(4n+2),P(4n+2),p1);
draw(P(1)--P(4n+2),p1);
dot(" $P_n$ ",P(n),P(n),p2);
dot(" $P_{n+1}$ ",P(n+1),P(n+1),p2);
dot(" $P_{n+2}$ ",P(n+2),P(n+2),p2);
path B=P(n)--P(n+1)--P(n+2);
draw(B,p2);
[/asy]

Now, instead of fixing the polygon and varying  $A$ , we fix  $A$  and vary the polygon. Let  $\mathcal B$  be the locus of  $B$ :

[asy]
unitsize(1inch); int n=4; pair P(int i){return dir(-180*(i+n)/(2*n+1));}
for(int j=0;j<=1;++j)for(int i=1;i<=4*n+2;++i){draw(shift(-P(j))*(P(i-1)--P(i)),darkgrey+dotted);}
pen p1=royalblue,p2=orange;
path B=P(n)--P(n+1)--P(n+2);
filldraw(shift(-P(0))*B--shift(-P(1))*reverse(B)--cycle,0.95*white+0.05*p2,p2);
label(" $\mathcal B$ ",P(n+1)-P(1),1.5*W,p2);
dot(" $A$ ",(0,0),S,p1);
[/asy]

Because the areas of the top and bottom halves of  $\mathcal B$  are the same, and because  $B$  is uniformly distributed in each half,  $B$  is uniformly distributed across  $\mathcal B$ . Therefore, the circle of radius  $c$  centered at  $A$  bisects  $\mathcal B$ . By symmetry, it therefore also bisects the following "banana":

[asy]
unitsize(1inch); int n=4; pair P(int i){return dir(-180*(i+n)/(2*n+1));}
for(int j=0;j<=1;++j)for(int i=1;i<=4*n+2;++i){draw(shift(-P(j))*(P(i-1)--P(i)),darkgrey+dotted);}
pen p1=royalblue,p2=rgb(13/15,11/15,0);
path B=P(1)--P(2);for(int i=1;i<2n;++i)B=B--P(i+2);
filldraw(shift(-P(0))*B--shift(-P(1))*reverse(B)--cycle,0.95*white+0.05*p2,p2);
dot(" $A$ ",(0,0),S,p1);
draw(arc((0,0),sqrt((4n+2)/pi*sin(2pi/(4n+2))),27(5-2/n),27(5+2/n)),brown+dashed);
[/asy]

Rotating this picture  $4n+2$  times about  $A$  gives:

[asy]
unitsize(1inch); int n=4; pair P(int i){return dir(-180*(i+n)/(2*n+1));}
for(int j=0;j<=1;++j)for(int i=1;i<=4*n+2;++i){draw(shift(-P(j))*(P(i-1)--P(i)),darkgrey+dotted);}
pen p1=royalblue,p2=rgb(13/15,11/15,0);
path B=P(1)--P(2);for(int i=1;i<2n;++i)B=B--P(i+2);
for(int i=0;i<4*n+2;++i)filldraw(rotate(180i/(2n+1))*(shift(-P(0))*B--shift(-P(1))*reverse(B)--cycle),0.95*white+0.05*p2,p2);
dot((0,0),p1);
draw(circle((0,0),sqrt((4n+2)/pi*sin(2pi/(4n+2)))),brown+dashed);
[/asy]

Therefore, the circle of radius  $c$  centered at  $A$  has half the total area of the banana bunch, which is a regular  $(4n+2)$ -gon with twice the side length and therefore quadruple the area of  $P_1P_2\dots P_{4n+2}$ . Therefore,  $\pi c^2=4/2$ , so  $c=\boxed{\sqrt{2/\pi}}$ .</details>
<details><summary>Remark 1</summary>The answer is different for non- $(4n+2)$ -gons, as  $B$  would not be uniformly distributed in the banana. For example, the answer for a square is  $\frac{2\sqrt{\pi-1}-2}{\pi-2}$ . Even though I wrote this problem in 2019, it could only work three years later. (This is also a reason why integration is doomed to fail.)</details>
<details><summary>Remark 2</summary>This problem had the ideal score distribution in my book among MOPpers. One person got 7, everyone else got 0.</details>

