label(" $n$ ", ((-0.5, 0) + (-0.5, rows))/2);
[/asy] $1$  comes from the big red rectangle. Green and blue rectangles are slotted into the even indexed columns and rows, requiring  $\left\lfloor\frac m2\right\rfloor$  and  $\left\lfloor\frac n2\right\rfloor$  rectangles respectively.

The red rectangle covers all the edges on the perimeter, the green rectangles cover all the internal vertical edges, and the blue rectangles cover all the internal horizontal edges, so this suffices.</details>
<details><summary>proof</summary><details><summary>edge case m=1</summary>Parallel to the side of length  $1$ , there are  $n+1$  edges. A rectangle can only cover  $2$ , so at least  $\left\lceil\frac{n+1}2\right\rceil$  rectangles are required. This equals the construction bound.</details>
