Consider the  $m-1$  vertical lines of length  $n$  on the interior of the grid. Color the first and last edges purple.
Similarly for the horizontal lines, color the first and last edges pink.
Finally, on the four lines forming perimeter, color the first edge clockwise orange.

There are  $2m-2$  purple,  $2n-2$  pink, and  $4$  orange edges. We show that each rectangle can cover at most  $4$  colored edges.

If the rectangle touches neither the left nor the right perimeter of the grid, it could only have purple edges. It has  $4$  purple edges iff it spans the entire height of the grid. Vice versa for pink.
Now consider rectangles that share both horizontal and vertical perimeter with the grid outline, which means it contains a corner, and hence an orange edge.
If it contains no other corner, it covers  $1$  orange,  $1$  purple and  $1$  pink edge, total of  $3$ .
If it contains one other corner, then it covers  $2$  orange edges, and:  $2$  purple edges if the corners are vertically adjacent, or  $2$  pink edges if the corners are horizontally adjacent. Either way,  $4$  total edges are covered.
And if it contains all corners, then it covers  $4$  orange edges.

In all cases,  $\leq4$  colored edges are covered. Therefore,  $\left\lceil\frac{(2m-2)+(2n-2)+4}4\right\rceil$  rectangles are required. This coincides with our answer, except for the case where  $m,n$  are both even, where we need to show  $1$  more rectangle is needed.

For the case that both  $m,n$  are even, every rectangle must cover exactly  $4$  unique colored edges for equality to hold, since  $2m-2+2n-2+4$  is divisible by  $4$ .
The number of purple edges is  $2$  mod  $4$ , which means that a rectangle must cover some number of purple edges that is neither  $0$  or  $4$ . This is only possible with the corner rectangle that covers  $2$  orange and  $2$  purple edges, which contains two vertically adjacent corners of the grid.
Similarly for pink, the rectangle must contain two horizontally adjacent corners of the grid.
But this forces the chosen rectangles to overlap, and thus share an orange edge, so equality cannot occur.

We conclude that  $1+\left\lceil\frac{(2m-2)+(2n-2)+4}4\right\rceil$  rectangles are required when both  $m,n$  are even, which coincides with the construction of  $1+\left\lfloor\frac m2\right\rfloor+\left\lfloor\frac n2\right\rfloor$ .</details>

