We will show that  $Q'$ , the orthocenter of  $\triangle BDF$ , satisfies the conditions of  $Q$ . To begin, observe that  $DQ'FP$  is a parallelogram, as  $P$  is the antipode of  $B$  with respect to the circumcircle of  $\triangle BDF$ . Furthermore,  $$ \angle DPC = \angle DEC = 90^\circ - \angle FEA = 90^\circ - \angle FPA, $$  which, in conjunction with  $\angle PDC = \angle AFP = 90^\circ$ , yields  $\triangle CPD\sim\triangle PAF$ . Combining these two results, we have  $$ \frac{CD}{DQ'} = \frac{CD}{PF} = \frac{CP}{PA}. $$  [asy]
