Let the  $A-$ symmedian intersects  $BC$  at  $J$  and  $(AQR)$  at  $K$ . We want to show  $K$  is the fixed point. It is equivalent to show  $P(J, (AQR))$  is fixed. Consider  $f:R^2\to R$  such that   $$ f(\bullet)=P(\bullet, (AQR))-P(\bullet, (ABC)) $$   Or equivalently it is sufficient to prove  $f(J)$  is fixed. We know that  $J=(0:b^2:c^2)$ . Let  $W=b^2+c^2$ . Now
\begin{align*}
f(J)&=\frac{c^2}{W}f(C)+\frac{b^2}{W}
f(B)
&=\frac{1}{W}\left(c^2\cdot CQ\cdot b+b^2\cdot BR\cdot c\right)
&=\frac{bc}{W}\left(CQ\cdot c+BR\cdot b\right)
&=\frac{b^2c^2}{W}
\end{align*}
Where  $CQ\cdot c+BR\cdot b=bc$  follows after adding  $\frac{CQ}{CA}=\frac{CP}{CB}, \frac{BR}{BA}=\frac{BP}{BC}$ . So  $f(J)$  is fixed and therefore  $K$  is fixed. Hence  $K\equiv X$ .

