Let  $M_A \equiv QD \cap (ABC)$  and  $N_A \equiv GD \cap (ABC)$ .

<span style="color:#00f">**Claim**</span>:  $Q$  is the  $A$ -Sharkydevil point of  $\triangle ABC$ .
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof*</span>: Inversion about the incircle takes the midpoints of  $EF,DE$  and  $FD$  to  $A,B$  and  $C$  respectively, the nine-point circle of  $\triangle DEF$  to  $(ABC)$  and  $EF$  to  $(AI)$ . Hence, it maps  $P$  to  $(AI) \cap (ABC) \equiv Q$  as desired.  $\square$ <span style="color:#00f">**Claim**</span>:  $GQBC$  is a harmonic quadrilateral.
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof*</span>: Note that we have \[(G,Q;B,C) \stackrel{A}{=} (P,AQ \cap EF;F,E) \stackrel{Q}{=} (I,A;E,F)=-1\] as desired.  $\square$ <span style="color:#00f">**Claim**</span>:  $GD$  bisects  $\angle BGC$ .
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof*</span>: Since  $Q$  is the  $A$ -Sharkydevil point, it is well known that  $M_A$  is the midpoint of arc  $BC$  in  $(ABC)$  not containing  $A$  and  $QD$  bisects  $\angle BQC$ . Then, we have \[-1=(G,Q;B,C) \stackrel D= (N_A,M_A;C,B)\]which implies  $N_A$  must be the midpoint of arc  $BAC$  in  $(ABC)$  and hence  $GD$  bisects  $\angle BGC$ .  $\square$ Now, by Claim  $2$ , in  $\triangle BQC$ ,  $QD$  is the angle bisector,  $QM$  is the median and  $QG$  is the symmedian, which gives  $\angle MQD=\angle GQD$ .
Again, by Claim  $2$  and  $3$ , in  $\triangle BGC$ ,  $GM$  is the median,  $GD$  is the angle bisector and  $GQ$  is the symmedian, which gives  $\angle QGD=\angle MGD$ .

Hence, we get  $D$  is the incenter of  $\triangle GQM$  which was to be shown.  $\blacksquare$

