Let  $f(S)$  denote the number of stable subsets of  $S$  with an even number of elements, and let  $g(S)$  denote the number of stable subsets of  $S$  with an odd number of elements. To prove the desired statement, apply induction on the number of elements in  $S.$  The base case,  $|S|=1,$  is trivial, as  $f(S) = g(S).$  Now, take a stable subset of  $n$  elements, assuming the hypothesis is true for  $n-1$  elements.

<span style="color:#00f">**Claim:**</span>If  $(x,y)$  is superior, and  $S^{\prime} = S/(x,y),$  then  $f(S^{\prime})-g(S^{\prime}) = f(S)-g(S).$ <span style="color:#f00">**Proof:**</span>Notice that, if a subset of  $S$  which is stable contains  $(x, y-1)$  and  $(x-1, y),$  then adding  $(x,y)$  keeps the subset stable, hence giving a bijection between odd-sized subsets of  $S$  and even-sized subsets of  $S$  which contain  $(x,y).$  Therefore, the stable subsets containing  $(x,y)$  can be disregarded, implying that  $f(S^{\prime})-g(S^{\prime}) = f(S)-g(S),$  as desired.  $\square$ This completes the inductive hypothesis, and we are done.

The answers are  $\{(1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 1) \}$ , and it is clear that they work. From hereon, we assume  $a, b > 2$  and  $a \neq b$ . We assign  $k = \left \lfloor  \sqrt{a} \right \rfloor = \left \lfloor  \sqrt{b} \right \rfloor$ . It is not hard to see that  $a$  and  $b$  must be relatively prime, so we have that \begin{align*} (a^4+1)(b^4+1) \equiv a^4+b^4+1 \equiv (a-b)^4+1 \equiv 0 \pmod{ab}\end{align*}
We now write \begin{align*} \ell = \frac{(a-b)^4+1}{ab} \end{align*} and have the following claim. **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $\ell \le 16$  and therefore  $\ell = 1$ . 

*Proof.* Observe that \begin{align*} \ell k^2 < \ell ab = (a-b)^4+1 \le (2k)^4+1\end{align*} implying that  $\ell \le 16$ . Now note that we also require  $\ell \mid (a-b)^4+1$ , so  $a-b$  has an order of  $8$  for any prime  $p \mid \ell$ . This implies  $p \equiv 1 \pmod{8}$  for all primes dividing  $\ell$ . The smallest prime that satisfies this is  $17 > 16$ , so  $\ell$  must be  $1$ .  $\square$ To finish, note that \begin{align*} k^4+1 < ab = (a-b)^4+1 < (k+1)^4+1 \end{align*} so there are no solutions if  $a, b > 2$ .  $\blacksquare$

