    [/asy]
    Note that  $(WXYZ)$  is cyclic since
    \[\measuredangle XWY + \measuredangle YZX = \measuredangle YWB + \measuredangle XZD = \measuredangle YCB + \measuredangle XAD = 0^\circ\]
    and  $(PQRS)$  is cyclic since
    \[\measuredangle PQS = \measuredangle YQS = \measuredangle YWS = \measuredangle PXZ = \measuredangle PRZ = \measuredangle SRP.\]
    Additionally,  $\overline{AD} \parallel \overline{PR}$  since
    \[\measuredangle DAX + \measuredangle AXP + \measuredangle XPR = \measuredangle YWX + \measuredangle WXY + \measuredangle XYW = 0^\circ,\]
    and  $\overline{BC} \parallel \overline{SQ}$  similarly. 

    Lastly,  $(ABCD)$  and  $(PQRS)$  are concentric; if not, using the radical axis theorem twice shows that their radical axis must be parallel to both  $\overline{AD}$  and  $\overline{BC}$ , contradiction.</details>

<details><summary>Solution G</summary>Let  $(BCY)$  meet  $\overline{AB}$  again at  $W$ , let  $(ADX)$  meet  $\overline{CD}$  again at  $Z$ , and let  $\overline{WZ}$  meet  $(ADX)$  and  $(BCY)$  again at  $R$  and  $S$ .
