This is a relatively straightforward computation with <details><summary>complex numbers</summary>Set  $(ABC)$  as unit circle: then we can let  $p=a\omega, q=b\omega, r=c\omega$  for some  $|\omega|=1$ . We will show   $$ \frac{a_2-(a+b+c)}{(a+b+c)-a} = \frac{a_2-a}{b+c}-1. $$   is symmetric in  $a,b,c$ . This will show there's a spiral similarity at  $a+b+c$  sending  $a,b,c$  to  $a_2,b_2,c_2$ . Due to laziness, we rename  $a_2$  as  $x$ .

Since  $x-a$  and  $b-c$  are parallel,  $$ \frac{x-a}{b-c}=\frac{\bar{x}-\tfrac{1}{a}}{\tfrac{1}{b}-\tfrac{1}{c}} \implies abc\bar{x}=bc+a^2-ax $$  and since  $x-a\omega$  and  $a\omega-(b+c-a)$  are perpendicular we have  $$ \frac{x-a\omega}{a\omega-(b+c)+a}+\frac{\bar{x}-\tfrac{1}{a\omega}}{\tfrac{1}{a\omega}-\tfrac{1}{b}-\tfrac{1}{c}+\tfrac{1}{a}}=0. $$  Substituting in our expression for  $\bar{x}$  and simplifying, the second term above is equal to   $$ \frac{\omega(bc+a^2-ax)-bc}{bc-a\omega(b+c)+bc\omega}. $$  Hence we have  $$ (x-a\omega)(bc-a\omega(b+c)+bc\omega)+(\omega(bc+a^2-ax)-bc)(a\omega-(b+c)+a)=0. $$  We wish to solve for  $x$ : this is a linear equation with
\begin{align*}
\text{coefficient of }x&=bc-a\omega(b+c)+bc\omega-a\omega(a\omega-(b+c)+a)
&=(\omega+1)(bc-a^2\omega)
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\text{constant term}&=-a\omega(bc-a\omega(b+c)+bc\omega)+(\omega(bc+a^2)-bc)(a\omega-(b+c)+a)
&=-abc\omega(\omega+1)+a^2\omega^2(b+c)+abc(\omega+1)(\omega-1)+a^3\omega(\omega+1)-bc(b+c)(\omega-1)-a^2\omega(b+c)
&=(\omega+1)(-abc\omega+abc(\omega-1)+a^3\omega)+(\omega-1)(b+c)(a^2\omega-bc)
&=a(\omega+1)(a^2\omega-bc)+(\omega-1)(b+c)(a^2\omega-bc)
&=(a^2\omega-bc)(a(\omega+1)+(\omega-1)(b+c))
\end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*}
x&=\frac{-\text{constant term}}{\text{coefficient of }x}=\frac{-\text{constant term}}{(\omega+1)(bc-a^2\omega)}
x-a&=\frac{a(\omega+1)(a^2\omega-bc)-(a^2\omega-bc)(a(\omega+1)+(\omega-1)(b+c))}{(\omega+1)(bc-a^2\omega)}
&=\frac{-(\omega-1)(b+c)}{(\omega+1)}
\end{align*}
So   $$ \frac{x-a}{b+c}-1=\frac{-(\omega-1)}{\omega+1}-1 $$  is indeed symmetric in  $a,b,c$  as desired.</details>.

