Let  $T:=\{\zeta^k:\gcd(k,p)=1\},$  then  $|T|=p^{r-1}(p-1)$  Note that Cyclotomic Polynomial  $$ \Phi_{p^r}(x)=\frac{x^{p^r}-1}{\Phi_{p^{r-1}}(x)\cdots \Phi_{{p}}(x)}=\frac{x^{p^r}-1}{x^{p^{r-1}}-1}=x^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}+\cdots +x^{p^{r-1}}+1. $$  Define  $\sigma_i$  be the denote the elementary symmetric sums for  $\frac{1}{1-\zeta}$  and  $S_i$  be the Newton Sum. Since  $$ \begin{aligned}
\prod_{\zeta\in T}\left(x-\frac 1{1-\zeta}\right)
&=\prod_{\zeta\in T}\frac x{1-\zeta}\left(1-\frac 1x-\zeta\right)
&=\frac{x^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}\Phi_{p^r}\left(1-\frac 1x\right)}{\Phi_{p^r}(1)}
&=\frac 1p{x^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}}\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\left(1-\frac 1x\right)^{p^{r-1}t}
&=\frac 1p{x^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}}\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\sum_{j=0}^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}\binom{p^{r-1}t}j\left(-\frac 1x\right) ^j
&=\frac 1p\sum_{j=0}^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}\left[(-1)^j\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\binom{p^{r-1}t}j\right]x^{p^{r-1}(p-1)-j}
&=\sum_{j=0}^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}(-1)^j\sigma_jx^{p^{r-1}(p-1)-j}.
\end{aligned} $$  Therefore for all  $0\le j\le p^{r-1}(p-1),$   $$ \sigma_j=\frac 1p\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\binom{p^{r-1}t}j. $$  We first prove that  $\sigma_j$  is not an integer iff  $j=p^{r-1}(p-1),$  by Lucas Theorem  $p\mid \sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\binom{p^{r-1}t}j$  when  $p^{r-1}\nmid j.$  When  $p^{r-1}\mid j,$  say  $j=p^{r-1}j',$  by Lucas Theorem  $$ \sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\binom{p^{r-1}t}j\equiv \binom{0}0^{r-1}\sum_{t=0}^{p-1}\binom{t}{j'}\equiv \binom{p}{j'+1}\pmod p. $$  Which is not multiple of  $p$  iff  $j=p-1,$   $j=p^{r-1}(p-1).$ Now we calculate Newton's Sum. When  $r=1$  obviously the answer is  $p-1,$  now let  $r\ge 2.$  First for all  $k\le p^{r-1}(p-1),$   $$ S_k=k(-1)^{k+1}\sigma_k+\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}(-1)^{i+1}\sigma_iS_{k-i}. $$  Since  $\sigma_1,\ldots ,\sigma_{p^{r-1}(p-1)-1}$  are all integer, apparently by induction we can show  $S_1,\ldots ,S_{p^{r-1}(p-1)-1}$  are all integer. Note that  $p^{r-1}(p-1)(-1)^{k+1}\sigma_{p^{r-1}(p-1)}\in\mathbb Z,$  this gives  $S_{p^{r-1}(p-1)}\in\mathbb Z.$ For all  $k>p^{r-1}(p-1),$   $$ \sum_{i=0}^{p^{r-1}(p-1)}\sigma_iS_{k-i}=0. $$  Since  $\sigma_1,\ldots ,\sigma_{p^{r-1}(p-1)-1}$  are all integer, if  $n_0$  is the minimum integer s.t.  $S_{n_0}\notin\mathbb Z,$  then  $n_1:=n_0-p^{r-1}(p-1)$  is the minimum integer s.t.  $S_{n_1}\notin p\mathbb  Z,$  define  $n_2,\ldots$  in this way. Now let  $n_0=kp^{r-1}(p-1)+m$  where  $k,m\in\mathbb N$  and  $m<p^{r-1}(p-1).$  Then  $n_k=n_0-kp^{r-1}(p-1)$   is the minimum integer s.t.  $S_{n_k}\notin p^k\mathbb  Z.$  Now we only need to calculate  $\nu_p(S_k)$  where  $k\le p^{r-1}(p-1).$  In fact by Newton's Sum straightforward induction shows for  $1 \le i \le (p-2)p^{r-1}$ ,  $S_i\in p^{r-1}\mathbb Z$ , but  $\nu_p(S_{(p-2)p^{r-1}+1})=r-2$ . Then induct again shows that for  $(p-2)p^{r-1} \leq i \leq (p-1)p^{r-1}$ ,  $S_i\in p^{r-2}\mathbb Z$ . Therefore  $n_k=p^{r-1}(p-2)+1,$  so   $$ n_0=\boxed{(r-1)p^{r-1}(r-1)+p^{r-1}(r-2)+1} $$   as desired. $\Box$

The answer is  $\boxed{((p-1)r-1)p^{r-1}+1}$ . Denote the sum in the problem as  $S(p,r,n)$ .

First, note that the  $x=\frac{1}{1-\zeta}$ 's are the roots of
\[\left(\left(1-\frac{1}{x}\right)^{(p-1)p^{r-1}}+\left(1-\frac{1}{x}\right)^{(p-2)p^{r-1}}+\dots+1\right)\cdot x^{(p-1)p^{r-1}}.\]
The leading coefficient is  $p$ . Therefore, by Newton sums, the sum in the problem is always an integer divided by a power of  $p$ .

Now consider the case where  $r=1$ . We get the polynomial
\[(x-1)^{p-1}+(x-1)^{p-2}x+(x-1)^{p-3}x^2+\dots+x^{p-1}=x^p-(x-1)^p\]
which has all coefficients besides the constant coefficient as a multiple of  $p$ , and leading coefficient  $p$ . Therefore, a straightforward Newton sums argument gives that  $s_1$  to  $s_{p-2}$  are integers,  $s_{p-1}$  has a  $\nu_p$  of  $-1$  and  $s_p$  to  $s_{2p-3}$  has a  $\nu_p$  of at least  $-1$ , so  $s_{2p-2}$  has a  $\nu_p$  of  $-2$ , etc, until  $s_{(p-1)r-1}$  still has a  $\nu_p$  of at least  $-r+1$  and  $s_{(p-1)r}$  finally has a  $\nu_p$  of  $-r$ . So  $p^{r-1}S(p,1,n)$  is first noninteger when  $n=(p-1)r$ . (Sorry for the reuse of  $r$  here)

Now we claim the following for  $r\ge 2$ , which will immediately solve the problem:
\[S(p,r,n)\equiv pS\left(p,r-1,\left\lceil\frac np\right\rceil\right)\pmod 1.\]
The rest of this proof will be dedicated to proving this fact.

Let  $\omega=e^{2\pi i/p}$ . Note that
\begin{align*}
S(p,r,n) &= \frac{\sum_\zeta\prod_{2\le k\le q,p\nmid k}(1-\zeta^k)^n}{\left(\prod_\zeta (1-\zeta)\right)^n} 
&= \frac{\sum_\zeta\prod_{2\le k\le q,p\nmid k}(1-\zeta^k)^n}{p^n} 
&= \frac{\sum_\zeta\Big(\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\prod_{j=1}^{p-1}(1-\zeta\omega^{k+j})^n\Big)\Big(\prod_{2\le k\le q,p\nmid k,k\not\equiv 1\pmod{p^{r-1}}}(1-\zeta^k)\Big)^n}{p^{n+1}}.
\end{align*}
The summand in the numerator is an integer polynomial in  $\zeta$  if we expand out  $\omega$  in terms of  $\zeta$  (it's fine that it's different  $\omega$ 's for different  $\zeta$ 's). Therefore, we only need to worry about the  $\zeta^{a\cdot p^{r-1}}$  terms. But the coefficients of those terms are going to get multiplied by some multiple of  $p^{r-1}$  when summing over all  $\zeta$ , so we may take coefficients in the summand mod  $p^2$ .

Now consider just the first factor:
\[\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\prod_{j=1}^{p-1}(1-\zeta\omega^{k+j})^n=\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}(1+(\zeta\omega^k)+(\zeta\omega^k)^2+\dots+(\zeta\omega^k)^{p-1})^n.\]
By roots of unity filter, this is equivalent to  $p$  times the sum of the  $x^0,x^p,x^{2p},\dots$  coefficients of
\[(1+\zeta x+\zeta x^2+\dots+\zeta^{p-1} x^{p-1})^n.\]
Therefore, we may take the coefficients in this polynomial mod  $p$ . And indeed,
\[1+\zeta x+\zeta x^2+\dots+\zeta^{p-1} x^{p-1}\equiv (1-\zeta x)^{p-1}\pmod p.\]
(As in, the coefficients are equal mod  $p$ .)

Therefore,
\begin{align*}
(1+\zeta x+\zeta x^2+\dots+\zeta^{p-1} x^{p-1})^n &\equiv (1-\zeta x)^{(p-1)n} 
&= (1-\zeta x)^{p\cdot\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor}\cdot(1-\zeta x)^{(p-1)n-p\cdot\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor} 
&\equiv (1-\zeta^p x^p)^{\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor}\cdot(1-\zeta x)^{(p-1)n-p\cdot\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor}\pmod p.
\end{align*}
Since the second factor has degree less than  $p$ , it is irrelevant, and the first factor after the roots of unity filter is just
\[p(1-\zeta^p)^{\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor}.\]
Therefore, we have shown that  $S(p,r,n)$  has the same integer part as
\begin{align*}
& p\cdot\frac{\sum_\zeta(1-\zeta^p)^{\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor}\Big(\prod_{2\le k\le q,p\nmid k,k\not\equiv 1\pmod{p^{r-1}}}(1-\zeta^k)\Big)^n}{p\cdot\left(\prod_\zeta (1-\zeta)\right)^n} 
=& \sum_\zeta\frac{(1-\zeta^p)^{\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor}}{\left(\prod_{k=0}^{p-1}(1-\zeta\omega^k)\right)^n} 
=& \sum_\zeta\frac{(1-\zeta^p)^{\left\lfloor\frac{(p-1)n}{p}\right\rfloor}}{(1-\zeta^p)^n} 
=& \sum_\zeta\frac{1}{(1-\zeta^p)^{\left\lfloor\frac np\right\rfloor}} 
=& pS\left(p,r-1,\left\lceil\frac np\right\rceil\right)\;\blacksquare
\end{align*}
(The factor of  $p$  appears because each  $\zeta^p$  is achieved by  $p$  values of  $\zeta$ .)

