***Lemma: Given four distinct points  $A,C,B,D$  in the plane, we have  $AC \perp BD $  if, and only if,   $$ {AB}^2-{AD}^2={CB}^2-{CD}^2. $$***
<details><summary>proof</summary>Let  $A^{*}$  and  $C^{*}$  be the feets of  $A$  and  $C$  onto line  $BD,$  respectively. We want to show that  $A^{*} = C^{*}$  if and only if the above relation holds. Indeed, taken the lenght directed we have by Pythagorean Theorem  $$  {AB}^2-{AD}^2 = {BA^{*}}^2-{A^{*}D}^2 = BD \cdot (BA^{*} - A^{*}D) $$  similarly  ${BC}^2-{CD}^2 = BD \cdot (BC^{*} - C^{*}D);$  then cleraly this expressions are equal if and only if  $A^{*} = C^{*}$  and the result follows</details>
