<details><summary>trig bash</summary>Now since  $X$  is the midpoint of  $BD,$  the above is equivalent to   $$  2\cdot MX \cdot XD \cdot \cos \angle MXD = 2\cdot NX \cdot XD \cdot \cos \angle NXB \iff  $$    $$  \iff MX \cdot \cos \angle MXD = NX \cdot \cos \angle NXB~(*) $$  Now denote by  $p = \angle XAB, q = \angle ABX, r = \angle ADX, s = \angle DAX.$  Remember that  $M$  is the midpoint of arc  $AXB;$  and so by angle chasing and trigonometry we have  $$  MX = XA\cdot \sin \angle MAX \cdot \frac{1}{\sin \angle ABX} = XA \cdot \sin \left( \frac{q-p}{2} \right) \cdot \frac{1}{\sin q}  $$  Also we have  $\angle MXD = \frac{p+q}{2}$  and then the LHS of  $(*)$  equals  $$ XA \cdot \frac{\sin \left( \frac{q-p}{2} \right) \cdot \cos \left( \frac{p+q}{2} \right)}{\sin q} = XA \cdot \frac{\sin q - \sin p}{2 \sin q} $$  where in the last expression we used the product to sum relations. Doing similary calculations to the RHS of  $(*),$  we reduce the problem to show  $$  \frac{\sin p}{\sin q} = \frac{\sin s}{ \sin r} \iff \frac{XB}{XA} = \frac{XD}{XA} $$  which is clear.</details>
