solution_md:
I'm assuming the problem is meant to be
<blockquote>Define  $T_n=\sum_{i=1}^n = i(n+1-i).$  Find  $\lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{T_n}{n^3}.$ </blockquote>
<details><summary>Solution</summary>We have
\begin{align*}
T_n &= \sum_{i=1}^n i(n+1-i) 
&= n\sum_{i=1}^n i + \sum_{i=1}^n i - \sum_{i=1}^n i^2 \
&= n \cdot \frac{n(n+1)}{2} + \frac{n(n+1)}{2} - \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} 
&= \frac{3n^2(n+1)+3n(n+1)-n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} 
&= \frac{p(n)}{6},
\end{align*}
where  $p(n)$  is a polynomial of degree  $3.$ Hence, we have
\begin{align*}
\lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{T_n}{n^3} &= \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{p(n)}{6n^3} 
&= \boxed{\frac{1}{6}}.
\end{align*} $\square$</details>
